(window.MAG=window.MAG||{}).d=(window.MAG.d||{});window.MAG.d["3-african-code"]=[["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p1","The Canons of the CCXVII Blessed Fathers Who Assembled at Carthage.","An attempt to write a commentary upon all the canons of the African Code, would have meant nothing less than the preparation of one volume or more on the canon law of the West. This is impossible and therefore, interesting as the field would be, I have been compelled to restrain my pen, and rather than give a scant and insufficient annotation, I have contented myself with providing the reader with as good a translation as I have been able to make of the very corrupt Latin (correcting it at times by the Greek), and have added the Ancient Epitome and the quaint notes in full of John Johnson from the Second Edition, of 1714, of his \"Clergyman's Vade-mecum,\" Pt. II., which occupy little space, but may not be easily reached by the ordinary reader. The student will find full scholia on these Canons in Van Espen in the Latin, and in Zonaras and Balsamon in the Greek. These latter are in Beveridge's Synodicon.","Tentamen commentarium scribendi in omnes canones Codicis Africani nihil aliud significasset quam unius voluminis vel plurium praeparationem de iure canonico occidentali. Hoc impossibile est et ideo, quamvis interesting sit campus, compulsum fui frenare stilum meum, et potius quam dare exiguam et insufficientem annotationem, contentus fui me providere lectori cum quam bona translatione quam potui facere de Latino valde corrupto (corrigendo illud interdum per Graecum), et addidi Epitomen Antiquam et notas integras concinnas Ioannis Johnson ex Editione Secunda, anni 1714, sui \"Clergyman's Vade-mecum,\" Pt. II., quae paruum spatium occupant, sed non facile attingi possunt a lectore ordinario. Studiosus inveniet plena scholia in his Canonibus apud Van Espen in Latino, et apud Zonaram et Balsamonem in Graeco. Haec posteriora sunt in Beveridge's Synodicon."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p2","Introductory Note.","Johnson writes an excellent Introduction to his Epitome of these Canons, as follows:","Johnson scribit introductionem excellentem ad suam Epitomen horum Canonum, ut sequitur:"],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p3","Introductory Note.","\"Councils were nowhere more frequently called in the Primitive Times than in Africa. In the year 418–19, all canons formerly made in sixteen councils held at Carthage, one at Milevis, one at Hippo, that were approved of, were read, and received a new sanction from a great number of bishops, then met in synod at Carthage. This Collection is the Code of the African Church, which was always in greatest repute in all Churches next after the Code of the Universal Church. This code was of very great authority in the old English Churches, for many of the Excerptions of Egbert were transcribed from it. And though the Code of the Universal Church ends with the canons of Chalcedon, yet these African Canons are inserted into the Ancient Code both of the Eastern and Western Churches.\"","\"Concilia nusquam frequentius convocata sunt in Temporibus Primitivis quam in Africa. Anno 418–19, omnes canones prius facti in sedecim conciliis habitis Carthagine, uno apud Milevim, uno apud Hipponem, qui approbati erant, lecti sunt, et acceperunt novam sanctionem a magno numero episcoporum, tunc in synodo convocatorum Carthagine. Haec Collectio est Codex Ecclesiae Africanae, qui semper in maxima existimatione fuit in omnibus Ecclesiis proxime post Codicem Ecclesiae Universalis. Hic codex fuit magnae auctoritatis in veteribus Anglicanis Ecclesiis, nam multae Exceptiones Egberti ex eo transcriptae sunt. Et quamvis Codex Ecclesiae Universalis desinat cum canonibus Chalcedonensibus, tamen hi Canones Africani inserti sunt in Codicem Antiquum tam Ecclesiarum Orientalium quam Occidentalium.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p4","Introductory Note.","\"These canons though ratified and approved by a synod, yet seem to have been divided or numbered by some private and unlearned hand, and have probably met with very unskilful transcribers, by which means some of them are much confounded and obscured, as to their sense and coherence. They are by Dionysius Exiguus and others entituled The Canons of the Synod of Africa. And though all were not originally made at one time, yet they were all confirmed by one synod of African bishops, who, after they had recited the Creed and the twenty canons of the Council of Nice, proceeded to make new canons, and re-enforce old ones.\"","\"Hi canones, quamvis rati habiti et approbati a synodo, tamen videntur divisi vel numerati esse ab aliqua manu privata et non erudita, et probabiliter convenerunt cum translatoribus valde imperitis, quo medio aliqui ex eis multum confusi et obscurati sunt, quoad sensum et cohaerentiam. Ipsi sunt a Dionysio Exiguo et aliis inscripti Canones Synodi Africae. Et quamvis omnes non sint originaliter facti uno tempore, tamen omnes confirmati sunt ab una synodo episcoporum Afrorum, qui, postquam recitaverunt Symbolum et viginti canones Concilii Nicaeni, processerunt ad faciendos novos canones, et ad roborandos veteres.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p5","Introductory Note.","In his \"Library of Canon Law\" (Bibliotheca Juris Canonici) Justellus gives these canons, and, in my opinion, gives them rightly, the title \"The Code of Canons of the African Church\" (Codex Canonum Ecclesiæ Africanæ), although Hefele describes them as \"the collection of those African Canons put together in 419 by Dionysius Exiguus.\"","In sua \"Bibliotheca Juris Canonici\" Justellus dat hos canones, et, mea sententia, dat eos recte, titulo \"Codex Canonum Ecclesiae Africanae,\" quamvis Hefele describit eos ut \"collectionem illorum Canonum Africanorum in unum coniunctorum anno 419 a Dionysio Exiguo.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p6","Introductory Note.","Hefele says that the title Dionysius gave them in his collection was \"The Statutes of an African Council\" (Statuta Concilii Africani) which would certainly be wholly inadequate and misleading; but in the edition of Dionysius in Migne's Patrologia Latina (Tom. LXVII., col. 181) in the Codex Canonum Ecclesiasticorum no such title occurs, but the perfectly accurate one, \"A Synod at Carthage in Africa, which adopted one hundred and thirty-eight canons.\"","Hefele dicit quod titulus quem Dionysius dedit eis in sua collectione erat \"Statuta Concilii Africani,\" quod certe esset omnino inadequatum et seductorium; sed in editione Dionysii in Migne's Patrologia Latina (Tom. LXVII., col. 181) in Codice Canonum Ecclesiasticorum nullus talis titulus occurrit, sed ille perfecte accuratus, \"Synodus Carthagine in Africa, quae adoptavit centum triginta octo canones.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p7","Introductory Note.","This is an exact description of what took place and of the origin of these most important dogmatic and disciplinary enactments. Hefele must have been thinking of Dionysius's Preface where the expression does occur but not as a title.","Haec est exacta descriptio eius quod factum est et originis horum maxime importantium dogmaticorum et disciplinarium constitutionum. Hefele cogitavisse debet de Praefatione Dionysii ubi expressio quidem occurrit sed non ut titulus."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p8","Introductory Note.","(Beveridge. Synodicon, Tom. II., p. 202.)","(Beveridge. Synodicon, Tom. II., p. 202.)"],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p9","Introductory Note.","Carthage was formerly the head of the whole of Africa, as St. Augustine tells us in his Epistle CLXII. From this cause it happened that a great number of councils were held there, gathered from all the provinces of Africa. Especially while Aurelius as Archbishop was occupying the throne were these meetings of bishops frequently holden; and by these, for the establishing of ecclesiastical discipline in Africa, many canons were enacted.","Carthago olim fuit caput totius Africae, ut S. Augustinus narrat nobis in Epistola CLXII. Ex hac causa accidit ut magnus numerus conciliorum ibi haberetur, congregatorum ex omnibus provinciis Africae. Praesertim dum Aurelius ut Archiepiscopus sedem occupabat, hae congregationes episcoporum frequenter habitae sunt; et per has, pro stabilimento disciplinae ecclesiasticae in Africa, multi canones constituti sunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p10","Introductory Note.","At last, after the consulate of Honorius (XII.) and Theodosius (VIII.), Augustuses, on the eighth day before the Calends of June, that is to say, on May 25, in the year of our Lord 419, another Council was held in the same city at which all the canons previously adopted were considered, and the greater part of them were again confirmed by the authority of the synod. These canons, thus confirmed by this council, merited to be called from that day to this \"The Code of Canons of the African Church.\"","Tandem, post consulatum Honorii (XII.) et Theodosii (VIII.), Augustorum, octavo die ante Calendas Iunias, id est, die 25 Maii, anno Domini nostri 419, aliud Concilium habitum est in eadem civitate in quo omnes canones antea adoptati considerati sunt, et maior pars eorum iterum confirmata est auctoritate synodi. Hi canones, sic confirmati ab hoc concilio, meruerunt vocari ab illo die usque ad hunc \"Codex Canonum Ecclesiae Africanae.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p11","Introductory Note.","These canons were not at first adopted in Greek but in Latin, and they were confirmed in the same language. This Dionysius Exiguus distinctly testifies to in his preface to the \"Code of Ecclesiastical Canons,\" in which they are included. It is uncertain when the canons of this Carthaginian synod were done into Greek. This only is certain, that they had been translated into Greek before the Council in Trullo by which, in its Second Canon, they were received into the Greek Nomocanon, and were confirmed by the authority of this synod; so that from that time these canons stand in the Eastern Church on an equality with all the rest.","Hi canones non sunt primo adoptati in Graeco sed in Latino, et confirmati sunt in eadem lingua. Hoc Dionysius Exiguus distincte testificatur in sua praefatione ad \"Codicem Canonum Ecclesiasticorum,\" in quo includuntur. Incertum est quando canones huius synodi Carthaginensis facti sunt in Graeco. Hoc solum certum est, quod translati fuerant in Graecum ante Concilium Trullanum a quo, in suo Secundo Canone, recepti sunt in Nomocanonem Graecum, et confirmati sunt auctoritate huius synodi; ita ut ab illo tempore hi canones stant in Ecclesia Orientali in aequalitate cum omnibus reliquis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p12","An Ancient Introduction.","After the consulate of the most glorious emperors, Honorius for the twelfth time and Theodosius for the eighth time, Augustuses, on the VIII. before the Calends of June at Carthage, in the Secretarium of the basilica of Faustus, when Pope Aurelius had sat down, together with Valentine of the primatial see of the province of Numidia, and Faustinus of the Potentine Church, of the Italian province Picenum, a legate of the Roman Church, and also with legates of the different African provinces, that is to say, of the two Numidias, of Byzacena, of Mauritania Cæsariensis, as well as of Tripoli, and with Vincent Colositanus, Fortunatian, and other bishops of the proconsular province, in all two hundred and seventeen, also with Philip and Asellus, presbyters and legates of the Roman Church, and while the deacons were standing by, Aurelius the bishop said, etc., ut infra.","Post consulatum gloriosissimorum imperatorum, Honorii XII. et Theodosii VIII., Augustorum, VIII. Kalendas Iunias Carthagine, in Secretario basilicae Fausti, cum Aurelius papa consedisset, una cum Valentino primae sedis provinciae Numidiae, et Faustino Potentinae Ecclesiae, provinciae Italiae Piceni, legato Ecclesiae Romanae, et etiam cum legatis diversarum provinciarum Africae, id est, utriusque Numidiae, Byzacenae, Mauritaniae Caesariensis, necnon et Tripolitanae, et cum Vincente Colositano, Fortunatiano, et ceteris episcopis provinciae proconsularis, in omnibus ducentis septemdecim, etiam cum Philippo et Asello, presbyteris et legatis Ecclesiae Romanae, et diaconibus assistentibus, Aurelius episcopus dixit, etc., ut infra."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p13","The Canons of the 217 Blessed Fathers who assembled at Carthage.","AURELIUS THE BISHOP said: You, most blessed brethren, remember that after the day fixed for the synod we discussed many things while we were waiting for our brethren who now have been sent as delegates and have arrived at the present synod, which must be placed in the acts. Wherefore let us render thanks to our Lord for the gathering together of so great an assembly. It remains that the acts of the Nicene Synod which we now have, and have been determined by the fathers, as well as those things enacted by our predecessors here, who confirmed that same Synod, or which according to the same form have been usefully enacted by all grades of the clergy, from the highest even to the lowest, should be brought forward. The whole Council said: Let them be brought forward.","AURELIUS EPISCOPUS dixit: Meministis, fratres beatissimi, post constitutum synodi diem multa nos inter se tractasse, expectantes fratres nostros qui nunc legati directi ad praesentem synodum convenerunt, quae actis inserenda sunt. Quapropter gratias agamus Domino nostro pro tantae congregationis conventu. Reliquum est ut gesta Nicaenae synodi quae nunc habemus, et a patribus sunt determinata, necnon et ea quae a decessoribus nostris hic constituta sunt, qui eandem synodum firmaverunt, vel quae secundum eandem formam utiliter ab omnibus gradibus clericorum a summo usque ad imum sunt constituta, proferantur. Tota Synodus dixit: Proferantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p14","The Canons of the 217 Blessed Fathers who assembled at Carthage.","Daniel the Notary read: The profession of faith or statutes of the Nicene Synod are as follows. And while he was speaking, Faustinus, a bishop of the people of Potentia, of the Italian province of Picenum, a legate of the Roman Church said: There have been entrusted to us by the Apostolic See certain things in writings, and certain other things as in ordinances to be treated of with your blessedness as we have called to memory in the acts above, that is to say, concerning the canons made at Nice, that their decrees and customs be observed; for some things are observed out of decree and canon, but some from custom. Concerning these things therefore in the first place let us make enquiry, if it please your blessedness; and afterwards let the other ordinances which have been adopted or proposed be confirmed; so that you may be able to show by your rescripts to the Apostolic See, and that you may declare to the same venerable Pope, that we have diligently remembered these things; although the headings of action taken had been already inserted in the acts.","Daniel Notarius legit: Professio fidei vel statuta Nicaenae synodi haec sunt. Et eo dicente, Faustinus episcopus plebis Potentiae, provinciae Italiae Piceni, legatus Ecclesiae Romanae dixit: Commendata sunt nobis a Sede Apostolica quaedam in scriptis, et quaedam alia quasi ex ordinationibus tractanda cum beatitudine vestra, sicut in actis superius memoravimus, id est, de canonibus apud Nicaeam factis, ut eorum decreta et consuetudines observentur; quaedam enim observantur ex decreto et canone, quaedam vero ex consuetudine. De his ergo in primis inquirimus, si placet beatitudini vestrae; et postea cetera ordinationes quae constitutae vel propositae sunt confirmentur; ut possitis ostendere per rescripta vestra ad Apostolicam Sedem, et ut eidem venerabili Papae declaretis, quia diligenter haec memoravimus; licet capitula actionis habitae iam actis inserta fuerint."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p15","The Canons of the 217 Blessed Fathers who assembled at Carthage.","Aurelius said: Let the commonitorium be brought forward, which our brethren and fellow-ministers lately placed in the acts, and let the rest of the things done or to be done, follow in order.","Aurelius dixit: Proferatur commonitorium, quod nuper fratres et consacerdotes nostri actis inseruerunt, et cetera gesta vel gerenda ordine consequantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p16","The Canons of the 217 Blessed Fathers who assembled at Carthage.","Daniel the Notary read the Commonitorium. To our brother Faustinus and to our sons, the presbyters Philip and Asellus, Zosimus, the bishop. You well remember that we committed to you certain businesses, and now [we bid you] carry out all things as if we ourselves were there (for), indeed, our presence is there with you; especially since ye have this our commandment, and the words of the canons which for greater certainty we have inserted in this our commonitory. For thus said our brethren in the Council of Nice when they made these decrees concerning the appeals of bishops:","Daniel Notarius legit Commonitorium. Fratri nostro Faustino et filiis nostris presbyteris Philippo et Asello, Zosimus episcopus. Bene meministis quia quaedam negotia vobis iniunximus, et nunc omnia peragite ac si nos ipsi adessemus (nam, nostra praesentia vobiscum est); praesertim cum habeatis hoc nostrum mandatum, et verba canonum quae pro maiore certitudine huic nostro commonitorio inseruimus. Sic enim dixerunt fratres nostri in Concilio Nicaeno quando haec decreta de appellationibus episcoporum fecerunt:"],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p17","The Canons of the 217 Blessed Fathers who assembled at Carthage.","\"But it seemed good that if a bishop had been accused, etc.\" [Here follows verbatim Canon v. of Sardica.]","\"Sed placuit ut si episcopus accusatus fuerit, etc.\" [Hic sequitur verbatim Canon V. Sardicensis.]"],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p18","Ancient Epitome.","If bishops shall have deposed a bishop, and if he appeal to the Roman bishop, he should be benignantly heard, the Roman bishop writing or ordering.","Si episcopi deposuerint episcopum, et si appellaverit ad Romanum episcopum, benigne audiatur, Romano episcopo scribente vel iubente."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p19","Epitome Antiqua.","And when this had been read, Alypius, bishop of the Tagastine Church, and legate of the province of Numidia, said: On this matter there has been some legislation in former sessions of our council, and we profess that we shall ever observe what was decreed by the Nicene Council; yet I remember that when we examined the Greek copies of this Nicene Synod, we did not find these the words quoted—Why this was the case, I am sure I do not know. For this reason we beg your reverence, holy Pope Aurelius, that, as the authentic record of the decrees of the Council of Nice are said to be preserved in the city of Constantinople, you would deign to send messengers with letters from your Holiness, and not only to our most holy brother the bishop of Constantinople, but also to the venerable bishops of Alexandria and Antioch, who shall send to us the decrees of that council with the authentification of their signatures, so that hereafter all ambiguity should be taken away, for we failed to find the words cited by our brother Faustinus; notwithstanding this however we promise to be ruled by them for a short time, as I have already said, until reliable copies come to hand. Moreover the venerable bishop of the Roman Church, Boniface, should be asked likewise to be good enough to send messengers to the aforementioned churches, who should have the same copies according to his rescript, but the copies of the aforementioned Nicene Council which we have, we place in these Acts.","Quo lecto, Alypius episcopus ecclesiae Tagastinae, legatus provinciae Numidiae, dixit: In hac re aliquid constitutum est in prioribus sessionibus concilii nostri, et profitemur nos semper observaturos quod Nicaeno concilio decretum est; tamen memini quod cum examinaremus exemplaria Graeca huius Nicaenae synodi, non invenimus haec verba quae citata sunt—Cur hoc factum sit, me prorsus ignorare. Propter hoc rogamus reverentiam vestram, sancte pater Aureli, ut, quia authentica gesta decretorum Concilii Nicaeni in urbe Constantinopolitana servari dicuntur, dignemini mittere nuntios cum epistolis a Sanctitate Vestra, non solum ad sanctissimum fratrem nostrum episcopum Constantinopolitanum, sed etiam ad venerabiles episcopos Alexandrinum et Antiochenum, qui mittant nobis decreta illius concilii cum authentificatione subscriptionum suarum, ut omnis ambiguitas auferatur, quia verba a fratre nostro Faustino citata invenire non potuimus; nihilominus tamen promittimus nos ab eis per breve tempus regulandos esse, sicut iam dixi, donec certa exemplaria ad manus venerint. Praeterea venerabilis episcopus Ecclesiae Romanae Bonifacius rogandus est ut similiter dignetur mittere nuntios ad praedictas ecclesias, qui eadem exemplaria secundum rescriptum suum habeant; exemplaria autem praefati Concilii Nicaeni quae habemus, in his Actis ponimus."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p20","Epitome Antiqua.","Faustinus the bishop, legate of the Roman Church, said: Let not your holiness do dishonour to the Roman Church, either in this matter or in any other, by saying the canons are doubtful, as our brother and fellow-bishop Alypius has vouchsafed to say: but do you deign to write these things to our holy and most blessed pope, so that he seeking out the genuine canons, can treat with your holiness on all matters decreed. But it suffices that the most blessed bishop of the city of Rome should make enquiry just as your holiness proposes doing on your part, that there may not seem to have arisen any contention between the Churches, but that ye may the rather be enabled to deliberate with fraternal charity, when he has been heard from, what is best should be observed.","Faustinus episcopus, legatus Ecclesiae Romanae, dixit: Non faciat sanctitas vestra iniuriam Ecclesiae Romanae, neque in hac causa neque in alia, dicendo canones dubios esse, sicut frater et coepiscopus noster Alypius dignatus est dicere: sed dignemini scribere haec ad sanctum et beatissimum papam nostrum, ut ipse perquirens veros canones, possit tractare cum sanctitate vestra de omnibus decretis. Sed sufficit quod beatissimus episcopus urbis Romae inquirat, sicut sanctitas vestra ex parte vestra facere proponit, ne videatur orta esse contentio inter Ecclesias, sed ut potius possitis deliberare cum fraterna caritate, cum ab eo auditum fuerit, quid optimum servari debeat."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p21","Epitome Antiqua.","Aurelius the bishop said: In addition to what is set down in the acts, we, by the letters from our insignificance, must more fully inform our holy brother and fellow-bishop Boniface of everything which we have considered. Therefore if our plan pleases all, let us be informed of this by the mouth of all. And the whole council said: It seems good to us.","Aurelius episcopus dixit: Praeter ea quae actis inserta sunt, nos per litteras ex nostra parvitate plenius informare debemus sanctum fratrem et coepiscopum nostrum Bonifacium de omnibus quae consideravimus. Ideo si consilium nostrum omnibus placet, hoc ore omnium nobis significetur. Et tota synodus dixit: Nobis placet."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p22","Epitome Antiqua.","Novatus the bishop, legate of Mauritania Sitifensis, said: We now call to mind that there is contained in this commonitory something about presbyters and deacons, how they should be tried by their own bishops or by those adjoining, a provision which we find nothing of in the Nicene Council. For this cause let your holiness order this part to be read.","Novatus episcopus, legatus Mauritaniae Sitifensis, dixit: Nunc meminimus contineri in hoc commonitorio aliquid de presbyteris et diaconibus, quomodo iudicari debeant a suis episcopis vel a vicinis, quod in Nicaeno Concilio nihil invenimus. Propter hoc iubeat sanctitas vestra hanc partem legi."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p23","Epitome Antiqua.","Aurelius the bishop said: Let the place asked for be read. Daniel the notary read as follows: Concerning the appeals of clergymen, that is of those of inferior rank, there is a sure answer of this very synod, concerning which thing what ye should do, we think should be inserted, as follows:","Aurelius episcopus dixit: Legatur locus petitus. Daniel notarius legit ut infra: De appellationibus clericorum, id est inferiorum, certa responsio est ipsius synodi, de qua re quid facere debeatis, inserendum putamus, ut infra:"],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p24","Epitome Antiqua.","\"Hosius the bishop said: I should not conceal what has come into my mind up to this time. If any bishop perchance has been quickly angered (a thing which should not happen) and has acted quickly or sharply against a presbyter or a deacon of his, and has wished to drive him out of the Church, provision should be made that the innocent be not condemned, or be deprived of communion: he that has been ejected should have the right of appeal to the bishops of the bordering dioceses, that his case should be heard, and it should be carried on all the more diligently because to him who asks a hearing it should not be denied. And the bishop who either justly or unjustly rejected him, should patiently allow the affair to be discussed, so that his sentence be either approved or else emended, etc.\"","\"Osius episcopus dixit: Non debeo celare quod usque huc in mentem meam venit. Si episcopus quis forte iracundus (quod non debet) cito aut aspere commoveatur adversus presbyterum aut diaconum suum, et exterminare eum de ecclesia voluerit, providendum est ne innocens damnetur aut perdat communionem: eiectus habeat potestatem finitimos episcopos interpellandi, ut causa eius audiatur, et diligentius tractetur, quia non oportet ei negari audientiam roganti. Et ille episcopus, qui aut iuste aut iniuste eum abiecit, patienter accipiat ut negotium discutiatur, ut vel probetur sententia eius vel emendetur, etc.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p25","Ancient Epitome.","A presbyter or deacon who has been cut off, has the privilege of appealing to the neighbouring bishops. Moreover, he who cut him off should bear with equanimity the conclusion arrived at.","Presbyter aut diaconus abscissus habet privilegium appellandi ad vicinos episcopos. Porro qui eum abscidit aequanimiter ferat conclusionem."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p26","Epitome Antiqua.","This is the first part of Canon xiv. of Sardica, as the canon previously quoted is Canon v. of the same synod.","Hic est prima pars Canonis XIV. Sardicensis, sicut canon superius citatus est Canon V. eiusdem synodi."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p27","Epitome Antiqua.","And when this had been read, Augustine, the bishop of the Church of Hippo of the province of Numidia, said: We promise that this shall be observed by us, provided that upon more careful examination it be found to be of the Council of Nice. Aurelius the bishop said. If this also is pleasing to the charity of you all, give it the confirmation of your vote. The whole Council said: Everything that has been ordained by the Nicene Council pleases us all. Jocundus, the bishop of the Church of Suffitula, legate of the province of Byzacena, said: What was decreed by the Nicene Council cannot in any particular be violated.","Quo lecto, Augustinus episcopus ecclesiae Hipponensis provinciae Numidiae dixit: Promittimus hoc a nobis observandum, si diligentius examinatum fuerit Nicaeni Concilii esse. Aurelius episcopus dixit: Si hoc quoque caritati vestrae placet, confirmationem vestri suffragii date. Tota synodus dixit: Omnia quae a Nicaeno Concilio constituta sunt omnibus nobis placent. Iocundus episcopus ecclesiae Suffitulanae, legatus provinciae Byzacenae, dixit: Quod a Nicaeno Concilio decretum est, nullo modo violari potest."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p28","Epitome Antiqua.","Faustinus the bishop, legate of the Roman Church, said: So far as has developed by the confession of your holiness as well as of the holy Alypius, and of our brother Jocundus, I believe that some of the points have been made weak and others confirmed, which should not be the case, since even the very canons themselves have been brought into question. Therefore, that there may be harmony between us and your blessedness, let your holiness deign to refer the matter to the holy and venerable bishop of the Roman Church, that he may be able to consider whether what St. Augustine vouchsafed to enact, should be conceded or not, I mean in the matter of appeals of the inferior grade. If therefore there still is doubt, on this head it is right that the bishop of the most blessed see be informed, if this can be found in the canons which have been approved.","Faustinus episcopus, legatus Ecclesiae Romanae, dixit: Quantum ex confessione sanctitatis vestrae necnon et sancti Alypii, et fratris nostri Iocundi processit, credo quaedam infiirmata quaedam vero confirmata, quod non debet fieri, cum etiam ipsi canones in quaestionem deducti sint. Ideo ut concordia sit inter nos et beatitudinem vestram, dignetur sanctitas vestra rem referre ad sanctum et venerabilem episcopum Ecclesiae Romanae, ut possit considerare utrum id quod S. Augustinus dignatus est constituere, concedendum sit necne, in causa videlicet appellationum inferioris gradus. Si ergo adhuc dubium est, de hoc capite oportet beatissimae sedis episcopum informari, si hoc in canonibus qui probati sunt inveniri potest."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p29","Ancient Epitome.","Since the written decrees of the Nicene Council have not been found, let the Roman bishop deign to write to the bishop of Constantinople and to him of Alexandria, and let us know what he receives from them.","Quia scripta decreta Concilii Nicaeni non sunt inventa, dignetur episcopus Romanus scribere episcopo Constantinopolitano et Alexandrino, et sciamus quid ab eis acceperit."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p30","Epitome Antiqua.","Aurelius the bishop said: As we have suggested to your charity, pray allow the copies of the statutes of the Nicene Council to be read and inserted in the acts, as well as those things what have been most healthfully defined in this city by our predecessors, according to the rule of that council, and those which now have been ordained by us. And the whole council said: The copies of the Creed, and the statutes of the Nicene Synod which formerly were brought to our council through Cæcilean of blessed memory, the predecessor of your holiness (who was present at it), as well as the copies of the decrees made by the Fathers in this city following them, or which now we have decreed by our common consultation, shall remain inserted in these ecclesiastical acts, so that (as has been already said) your blessedness may vouchsafe to write to those most venerable men of the Church of Antioch, and of that of Alexandria, and also of that of Constantinople, that they would send most accurate copies of the decrees of the Council of Nice under the authentification of their signatures, by which, the truth of the matter having become evident, those chapters which in the commonitory our brother who is present, and fellow-bishop Faustinus, as well as our fellow-presbyters Philip and Asellus brought with them, if they be found therein, may be confirmed by us; or if they be not found, we will hold a synod and consider the matter further. Daniel the notary read the profession of faith of the Council of Nice and its statutes to the African Council.","Aurelius episcopus dixit: Sicut caritati vestrae suggessimus, iubete ut exemplaria statutorum Concilii Nicaeni legantur et actis inserantur, necnon et ea quae saluberrime in hac civitate a decessoribus nostris definita sunt secundum regulam illius concilii, et quae nunc a nobis ordinata sunt. Et tota synodus dixit: Exemplaria Symboli et statutorum Nicaenae Synodi quae olim ad concilium nostrum per Caecileanum beatae memoriae, decessorem sanctitatis vestrae (qui ei interfuit), allata sunt, necnon et exemplaria decretorum quae a Patribus in hac civitate sequentibus facta sunt, vel quae nunc communi consilio decrevimus, inserta manebunt in his actis ecclesiasticis, ut (sicut iam dictum est) beatitudo vestra dignetur scribere ad venerabiles viros ecclesiae Antiochenae, et Alexandrinae, necnon et Constantinopolitanae, ut mittant accuratissima exemplaria decretorum Concilii Nicaeni cum authentificatione subscriptionum suarum, per quae veritate rei patefacta, capitula quae in commonitorio frater noster qui praesens est et coepiscopus Faustinus, necnon et consacerdotes nostri Philippus et Asellus secum detulerunt, si in eis inventa fuerint, a nobis confirmentur; aut si non inventa fuerint, synodum habebimus et ulterius considerabimus. Daniel notarius legit professionem fidei Concilii Nicaeni et statuta eius ad Concilium Africanum."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p31","Epitome Antiqua.","The Profession of Faith of the Nicene Council.","Professio Fidei Concilii Nicaeni."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p32","Epitome Antiqua.","We believe in one God, etc.,…and in the Holy Ghost. But those who say, etc.,…anathematize them.","Credimus in unum Deum, etc.,…et in Spiritum Sanctum. Eos autem qui dicunt, etc.,…anathematizamus."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p33","Epitome Antiqua.","The statutes also of the Nicene Council in twenty heads were likewise read, as are found written before. Then what things were promulgated in the African Synods, were inserted in the present acts.","Statuta quoque Concilii Nicaeni in capitulis viginti similiter lecta sunt, sicut ante scripta inveniuntur. Tunc quae in Africanis Synodis promulgata sunt, inserta sunt praesentibus actis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p34","Canon I.","That the statutes of the Nicene Council are to be scrupulously observed.","Ut statuta Concilii Nicaeni studiose serventur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p35","Canon I.","AURELIUS the bishop said: Such are the statutes of the Nicene Council, which our fathers at that time brought back with them: and preserving this form, let these things which follow, adopted and confirmed by us, be kept firm.","AURELIUS episcopus dixit: Haec sunt statuta Concilii Nicaeni, quae patres nostri tunc secum detulerunt: et hanc formam servantes, quae secuntur a nobis constituta atque firmata, firma teneantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p36","Ancient Epitome of Canon I.","Let the copies of the decrees of the Nicene Council which our fathers brought back with them from that synod, be observed.","Exemplaria decretorum Concilii Nicaeni quae patres nostri secum ab illa synodo detulerunt, observentur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p37","Canon II.","Of Preaching the Trinity.","De Praedicanda Trinitate."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p38","Canon II.","THE whole Council said: By the favour of God, by a unanimous confession the Church's faith which through us is handed down should be confessed in this glorious assembly before anything else; then the ecclesiastical order of each is to be built up and strengthened by the consent of all. That the minds of our brethren and fellow bishops lately elevated may be strengthened, those things should be propounded which we have certainly received from our fathers, as the unity of the Trinity, which we retain consecrated in our senses, of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost, which has no difference, as we say, so we shall instruct the people of God. Moreover by all the bishops lately promoted it was said: So we openly confess, so we hold, so we teach, following the Evangelic faith and your teaching.","TOTA Synodus dixit: Favore Dei, unanimi confessione fides Ecclesiae quae per nos traditur, in hoc glorioso coetu ante omnia confitenda est; tunc ordo ecclesiasticus singulorum ex consensu omnium exstruendus atque roborandus est. Ut mentes fratrum et coepiscoporum nuper promotorum roborentur, ea proponenda sunt quae a patribus certa dispositione accepimus, Trinitatis unitatem, quam nostris in sensibus consecratam retinemus, Patris et Filii et Spiritus Sancti, quae nullam noscitur habere differentiam, sicut dicimus, ita Dei populos instruemus. Porro ab omnibus episcopis nuper promotis dictum est: Sic plane confitemur, sic tenemus, sic docemus, evangelicam fidem et doctrinam vestram sequentes."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p39","Ancient Epitome of Canon II.","No difference is recognised or taught by the decrees of the Council of Nice between the Persons of the Holy Trinity.","Nulla differentia agnoscitur vel docetur decretis Concilii Nicaeni inter Personas Sanctae Trinitatis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p40","Epitome Antiqua Canonis II.","This canon, or rather introduction, is taken from Canon j., of the Council of Carthage held under Genethlius, A.D. 387 or 390.","Hic canon, vel potius introductio, sumptus est ex Canone I. Concilii Carthaginensis sub Genethlio habiti, anno 387 vel 390."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p41","Canon III.","Of Continence.","De Continentia."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p42","Canon III.","AURELIUS the bishop said: When at the past council the matter on continency and chastity was considered, those three grades, which by a sort of bond are joined to chastity by their consecration, to wit bishops, presbyters, and deacons, so it seemed that it was becoming that the sacred rulers and priests of God as well as the Levites, or those who served at the divine sacraments, should be continent altogether, by which they would be able with singleness of heart to ask what they sought from the Lord: so that what the apostles taught and antiquity kept, that we might also keep.","AURELIUS episcopus dixit: Cum in praeterito concilio de continentiae et castitatis moderamine tractaretur, gradus isti tres, qui quasi quodam vinculo castitati per consecrationem annexi sunt, episcopi videlicet, presbyteri et diaconi, ita placuit, ut condecet sacrosanctos antistites ac Dei sacerdotes necnon et Levitas, vel qui sacramentis divinis inserviunt, continentes esse in omnibus, quo possint simpliciter quod a Domino postulant impetrare: ut quod apostoli docuerunt et antiquitas custodivit, nos quoque custodiamus."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p43","Ancient Epitome of Canon III.","Let a bishop, a presbyter, and a deacon be chaste and continent.","Episcopus, presbyter et diaconus casti et continentes sint."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p44","Epitome Antiqua Canonis III.","This canon is taken from Canon ij., of Carthage 387 or 390.","Hic canon sumptus est ex Canone II. Carthaginensi anni 387 vel 390."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p45","Canon IV.","Of the different orders that should abstain from their wives.","De diversis ordinibus qui abstinere debent ab uxoribus suis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p46","Canon IV.","FAUSTINUS, the bishop of the Potentine Church, in the province of Picenum, a legate of the Roman Church, said: It seems good that a bishop, a presbyter, and a deacon, or whoever perform the sacraments, should be keepers of modesty and should abstain from their wives.","FAUSTINUS episcopus Potentinae Ecclesiae, provinciae Piceni, legatus Ecclesiae Romanae, dixit: Bonum videtur ut episcopus, presbyter et diaconus, vel qui sacramenta contrectant, pudicitiae custodes sint et ab uxoribus se abstineant."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p47","Canon IV.","By all the bishops it was said: It is right that all who serve the altar should keep pudicity from all women.","Ab universis episcopis dictum est: Iustum est ut omnes qui altario inserviunt pudicitiam ab omnibus mulieribus custodiant."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p48","Ancient Epitome of Canon IV.","Let those who pray abstain from their wives that they may obtain their petitions.","Qui orant abstineant se ab uxoribus ut impetrare possint quae petunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p49","Epitome Antiqua Canonis IV.","This canon is taken from Canon ij., of Carthage 387 or 390, last mentioned.","Hic canon sumptus est ex Canone II. Carthaginensi anni 387 vel 390, ultimo citato."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p50","Canon V.","Of Avarice.","De Avaritia."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p51","Canon V.","AURELIUS, the bishop, said: The cupidity of avarice (which, let no one doubt, is the mother of all evil things), is to be henceforth prohibited, lest anyone should usurp another's limits, or for gain should pass beyond the limits fixed by the fathers, nor shall it be at all lawful for any of the clergy to receive usury of any kind. And those new edicts (suggestiones) which are obscure and generally ambiguous, after they have been examined by us, will have their value fixed; but with regard to those upon which the Divine Scripture hath already most plainly given judgment, it is unnecessary that further sentence should be pronounced, but what is already laid down is to be carried out. And what is reprehensible in laymen is worthy of still more severe censure in the clergy. The whole synod said: No one hath gone contrary to what is said in the Prophets and in the Gospels with impunity.","AURELIUS episcopus dixit: Avaritiae cupiditas (quae omnium malorum mater esse nullus dubitat) inhibenda est, ne quis alienos fines usurpet, aut per praemium terminos a patribus statutos transcendat, nec omnino cuiquam clericorum de qualibet re foenus accipere liceat. Et suggestionum novellae, quae obscurae sunt et sub genere latentes, inspectae a nobis formam accipient; de quibus autem apertissime divina Scriptura iam iudicavit, non est differenda sententia, sed pocius exsequenda quod iam statutum est. Et quod in laicis repraehenditur, multo magis in clericis est praedamnandum. Tota synodus dixit: Nemo contra prophetas, nemo contra evangelia facit sine periculo."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p52","Ancient Epitome of Canon V.","As the taking of any kind of usury is condemned in laymen, much more is it condemned in clergymen.","Sicut foenerationis qualiscumque exactio damnatur in laicis, multo magis damnatur in clericis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p53","Epitome Antiqua Canonis V.","This canon is made up of Canons x. and xiij. of the Synod of Carthage held under Gratus in A.D. 345–348.","Hic canon conflatus est ex Canonibus X. et XIII. Synodi Carthaginensis sub Grato habitae, anno 345–348."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p54","Canon VI.","That the chrism should not be made by presbyters.","Ut chrisma a presbyteris non fiat."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p55","Canon VI.","FORTUNATUS the bishop, said: In former councils we remember that it was decreed that the chrism or the reconciliation of penitents, as also the consecration of virgins be not done by presbyters: but should anyone be discovered to have done this, what ought we to decree with regard to him?","FORTUNATUS episcopus dixit: In praeteritis conciliis meminimus fuisse decretum, ut chrisma vel reconciliatio paenitentium necnon et consecratio virginum a presbyteris non fiat: si quis autem hoc fecisse inventus fuerit, quid de eo statuendum est?"],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p56","Canon VI.","Aurelius the bishop said: Your worthiness has heard the suggestion of our brother and fellow-bishop Fortunatus; What answer will you give?","Aurelius episcopus dixit: Audivit dignatio vestra suggestionem fratris et coepiscopi nostri Fortunati; quid ad haec dicitis?"],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p57","Canon VI.","And all the bishops replied: Neither the making of the chrism, nor the consecration of virgins, is to be done by presbyters, nor is it permitted to a presbyter to reconcile anyone in the public mass (in publica missa), this is the pleasure of all of us.","Et omnes episcopi responderunt: Nec chrismatis confectio nec virginum consecratio a presbyteris fiat, nec in publica missa paenitentem reconciliare presbytero liceat, hoc omnibus placet."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p58","Ancient Epitome of Canon VI.","Let no presbyter make the chrism, nor prepare the unction, nor consecrate virgins, nor publicly reconcile anyone to communion.","Nullus presbyter chrisma conficiat, nec unctionem praeparet, nec virgines consecret, nec publice quemquam in communione reconciliet."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p59","Epitome Antiqua Canonis VI.","This is Canon iij. of the Carthaginian Synod under Genethlius, A.D. 387 or 390.","Hic est Canon III. Synodi Carthaginensis sub Genethlio, anno 387 vel 390."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p60","Canon VII.","Concerning those who are reconciled in peril of death.","De his qui in periculo mortis reconciliantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p61","Canon VII.","AURELIUS the bishop said: If anyone had fallen into peril of death during the absence of the bishop, and had sought to reconcile himself to the divine altars, the presbyter should consult the bishop, and so reconcile the sick man at his bidding, which thing we should strengthen with healthy counsel. By all the bishops it was said: Whatever your holiness has taught us to be necessary, that is our pleasure.","AURELIUS episcopus dixit: Si quis in periculo mortis constitutus fuerit absente episcopo, et se divinis altaribus reconciliari petierit, debet presbyter consulere episcopum, et sic periclitantem eius praecepto reconciliare, quam rem debemus salubri consilio roborare. Ab universis episcopis dictum est: Quod sanctitas vestra nos necessario instruere dignata est, hoc nobis placet."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p62","Ancient Epitome of Canon VII.","A priest desiring to reconcile anyone in peril to the sacred altars must consult the bishop and do what seems good to him.","Presbyter volens reconciliare aliquem in periculo sacris altaribus debet consulere episcopum et facere quod ei placuerit."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p63","Epitome Antiqua Canonis VII.","This is Canon iv. of the Synod of 387 or 390.","Hic est Canon IV. Synodi anni 387 vel 390."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p64","Canon VIII.","Of those who make accusation against an elder; and that no criminal is to be suffered to bring a charge against a bishop.","De his qui accusationem faciunt adversus maiorem natu; et ut nullus criminalis permittatur accusationem facere adversus episcopum."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p65","Canon VIII.","NUMIDIUS, the bishop of Maxula, said: Moreover, there are very many, not of good life, who think that their elders or bishops should be the butt for accusation; ought such to be easily admitted or no? Aurelius the bishop said: Is it the pleasure of your charity that he who is ensnared by divers wickednesses should have no voice of accusation against these? All the bishops said: If he is criminous, his accusation is not to be received.","NUMIDIUS episcopus Maxulitanus dixit: Praeterea sunt quamplurimi non bonae conversationis, qui existimant maiores natu vel episcopos in accusationem pulsandos; debentne tam facile admitti necne? Aurelius episcopus dixit: Placet igitur caritati vestrae ut is qui aliquibus sceleribus irretitus est, vocem adversus istos accusandi non habeat? Omnes episcopi dixerunt: Si criminosus est, accusatio eius non recipienda est."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p66","Ancient Epitome of Canon VIII.","It has seemed good that they who are themselves defendants for crimes should not bring accusations; nor should they be allowed to lay crimes to anyone's charge.","Placuit ut qui ipsi pro criminibus rei sunt, accusationes non inferant; nec eis liceat alicui crimina obicere."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p67","Epitome Antiqua Canonis VIII.","This is Canon vi. of Genethlius's Synod at Carthage, A.D. 387 or 390.","Hic est Canon VI. Synodi Genethlii Carthagine, anno 387 vel 390."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p68","Canon IX.","Of those who on account of their deeds are justly cast forth from the congregation of the Church.","De his qui propter facta sua iuste de congregatione Ecclesiae pelluntur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p69","Canon IX.","AUGUSTINE the bishop, the legate of the Numidian province, said: Deign to enact that if any perchance have been rightly on account of their crimes cast forth from the Church, and shall have been received into communion by some bishop or presbyter, such shall be considered as guilty of an equal crime with them who flee away from the judgment of their own bishop. And all the bishops said: This is the pleasure of all of us.","AUGUSTINUS episcopus, legatus provinciae Numidiae, dixit: Dignemini statuere ut si qui forte iuste propter crimina sua de Ecclesia eiecti fuerint, et ab aliquo episcopo vel presbytero in communionem suscepti fuerint, tales pari crimine teneantur obnoxii cum his qui de iudicio proprii episcopi refugiunt. Et omnes episcopi dixerunt: Hoc omnibus nostrum placet."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p70","Ancient Epitome of Canon IX.","Let him be excommunicated who communicates with one excommunicated.","Excommunicetur qui communicat excommunicato."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p71","Epitome Antiqua Canonis IX.","This is Canon vii. of the same synod of 387 or 390.","Hic est Canon VII. eiusdem synodi anni 387 vel 390."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p72","Canon X.","Of presbyters who are corrected by their own bishops.","De presbyteris qui a propriis episcopis corripiuntur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p73","Canon X.","ALYPIUS the bishop, a legate of the province of Numidia, said: Nor should this be passed over; if by chance any presbyter when corrected by his bishop, inflamed by self-conceit or pride, has thought fit to offer sacrifices to God separately [from the authority of the bishop] or has believed it right to erect another altar, contrary to ecclesiastical faith and discipline, such should not get off with impunity. Valentine, of the primatial see of the province of Numidia, said: The propositions made by our brother Alypius are of necessity congruous to ecclesiastical discipline and faith; therefore enact what seems good to your belovedness.","ALYPIUS episcopus, legatus provinciae Numidiae, dixit: Nec illud praetermittendum est; si quis forte presbyter ab episcopo suo correptus, tumore vel superbia inflammatus, separatim sacrificia Deo offerenda putaverit vel aliud altare erigendum contra ecclesiasticam fidem disciplinamque crediderit, non impunitus evadat. Valentinus primae sedis provinciae Numidiae dixit: Necessario disciplinae ecclesiasticae et fidei congrua sunt quae frater noster Alypius prosecutus est; proinde quod videtur dilectioni vestrae, decernite."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p74","Ancient Epitome of Canon X.","If one condemned by his bishop shall separate himself and set up an altar or make the offering, he should be punished.","Si quis damnatus ab episcopo suo separaverit se et altare erexerit vel oblationem fecerit, puniatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p75","Epitome Antiqua Canonis X.","This and the following canon are Canon viii. of the so often mentioned synod of 387 or 390.","Hic et sequens canon sunt Canon VIII. saepius memoratae synodi anni 387 vel 390."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p76","Canon XI.","If any presbyter, inflated against his bishop, makes a schism, let him be anathema.","Si quis presbyter contra episcopum suum inflatus schisma fecerit, anathema sit."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p77","Canon XI.","ALL the bishops said: If any presbyter shall have been corrected by his superior, he should ask the neighbouring bishops that his cause be heard by them and that through them he may be reconciled to his bishop: but if he shall not have done this, but, puffed up with pride, (which may God forbid!) he shall have thought it proper to separate himself from the communion of his bishop, and separately shall have offered the sacrifice to God, and made a schism with certain accomplices, let him be anathema, and let him lose his place; and if the complaint which he brought against his bishop shall not have been found to be well founded, an enquiry should be instituted.","OMNES episcopi dixerunt: Si quis presbyter a suo praeposito correptus fuerit, debet vicinos episcopos rogare ut causa eius ab ipsis audiatur et per ipsos episcopo suo reconcilietur: quod si non fecerit, sed superbia (quod absit) inflatus, separandum se a communione episcopi sui duxerit, et separatim sacrificium Deo obtulerit, et cum quibusdam complicibus schisma fecerit, anathema sit, et locum suum amittat; et si querimonia quam adversus episcopum habuit, non inventa fuerit iusta, inquirendum erit."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p78","Ancient Epitome of Canon XI.","A Presbyter condemned by his bishop, is allowed to appeal to the neighbouring bishops: but if he shall not make any appeal, but shall make a schism, and be elated with conceit and shall offer the Holy Gifts to God, let him be anathema.","Presbyter damnatus ab episcopo suo licentiam habet appellandi ad vicinos episcopos: si autem non appellaverit, sed schisma fecerit, et elatus superbia sancta dona Deo obtulerit, anathema sit."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p79","Canon XII.","If any bishop out of Synod time shall have fallen under accusation, let his cause be heard by 12 bishops.","Si quis episcopus absque synodi tempore in accusationem incurrerit, a duodecim episcopis audiatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p80","Canon XII.","FELIX the bishop, said: I suggest, according to the statutes of the ancient councils, that if any bishop (which may God forbid!) shall have fallen under any accusation, and there shall have been too great necessity to wait for the summoning of a majority of the bishops, that he may not rest under accusation, let his cause be heard by 12 bishops; and let a presbyter be heard by six bishops with his own bishop, and a deacon shall be heard by three.","FELIX episcopus dixit: Secundum statuta veterum conciliorum suggero, ut si quis episcopus (quod Deus avertat!) in accusationem aliquam incurrerit, et nimia necessitas fuerit expectandam plurimorum episcoporum convocationem, ne sub accusatione remaneat, a duodecim episcopis audiatur; et presbyter a sex episcopis cum suo episcopo audiatur, et diaconus a tribus audiatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p81","Ancient Epitome of Canon XII.","When a bishop is to be tried, if the whole synod does not sit, let at least twelve bishops take up the matter; and for the case of a presbyter, six and his own diocesan; and for the case of a deacon, three.","Quando episcopus iudicandus est, si tota synodus non sederit, saltem duodecim episcopi causam suscipiant; pro presbytero autem sex cum suo episcopo; pro diacono vero tres."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p82","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XII.","This is Canon x. of the Synod of Genethlius.","Hic est Canon X. Synodi Genethlii."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p83","Canon XIII.","That a bishop should not be ordained except by many bishops, but if there should be necessity he may be ordained by three.","Ut episcopus non ordinetur nisi a multis episcopis, sed si necessitas fuerit a tribus ordinetur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p84","Canon XIII.","BISHOP AURELIUS said: What says your holiness on this matter? By all the bishops it was answered: The decrees of the ancients must be observed by us, to wit, that without the consent of the Primate of any province even many bishops assembled together should not lightly presume to ordain a bishop. But should there be a necessity, at his bidding, three bishops should ordain him in any place they happen to be, and if anyone contrary to his profession and subscription shall come into any place he shall thereby deprive himself of his honour.","AURELIUS episcopus dixit: Quid ad haec dicit sanctitas vestra? Ab universis episcopis responsum est: Decreta antiquorum a nobis servari debent, ut videlicet sine consensu primatis cuiuslibet provinciae nec multi episcopi congregati temere praesumant episcopum ordinare. Si autem necessitas fuerit, iubente eo, tres episcopi in quocumque loco fuerint eum ordinent, et si quis contra professionem et subscriptionem suam in aliquo loco venerit, ipse se honore suo privabit."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p85","Ancient Epitome of Canon XIII.","At the bidding of the Primate even three bishops can make a bishop. But whoever goes counter to his profession and subscription, is deprived of his honour by his own judgment.","Iubente primate etiam tres episcopi possunt facere episcopum. Qui autem contra professionem et subscriptionem suam vadit, suo iudicio honore privatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p86","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XIII.","This is Canon xij. of the before mentioned Synod of 387 or 390.","Hic est Canon XII. praedictae Synodi anni 387 vel 390."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p87","Canon XIV.","That one of the bishops of Tripoli should come as legate, and that a presbyter might be heard there by five bishops.","Ut unus de episcopis Tripolitanis in legationem veniat, et ut presbyter ibi a quinque episcopis audiatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p88","Canon XIV.","IT also seemed good that one bishop from Tripoli, on account of the poverty of the province, should come as a legation, and that there a presbyter might be heard by five bishops, and a deacon by three, as has been noted above, his own bishop presiding.","Item placuit ut de Tripoli propter inopiam provinciae unus episcopus in legationem veniat, et ut ibi presbyter a quinque episcopis audiatur, et diaconus a tribus, sicut superius notatum est, proprio episcopo residente."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p89","Ancient Epitome of Canon XIV.","On account of the scarcity of bishops in Tripoli, one bishop shall suffice for a legation.","Propter paucitatem episcoporum in Tripoli, unus episcopus sufficiat in legationem."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p90","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XIV.","This canon is made up of two parts. The first part is Canon v. of the synod of Hippo, A.D. 393, and was repeated at the Carthaginian synod of 397. The second half is from Canon viij. of the same council.","Hic canon conflatus est ex duabus partibus. Prima pars est Canon V. synodi Hipponensis, anno 393, et repetitus est apud Carthaginem anno 397. Altera pars est ex Canone VIII. eiusdem concilii."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p91","Canon XV.","Of the divers orders who serve the Church, that if any one fall into a criminal business and refused to be tried by the ecclesiastical court, he ought to be in danger therefor; and that the sons of bishops are not to attend worldly shows.","De diversis ordinibus qui Ecclesiae serviunt, ut si quis in causam criminalem inciderit et noluerit iudicio ecclesiastico iudicari, inde periclitari debeat; et ut filii sacerdotum spectacula saecularia non attendant."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p92","Canon XV.","MOREOVER it seemed good that if any bishop, presbyter, or deacon, who had a criminal charge brought against him or who had a civil cause, refused to be tried by the ecclesiastical tribunal, but wished to be judged by the secular courts, even if he won his suit, nevertheless he should lose his office. This is the law in a criminal suit; but in a civil suit he shall lose that for the recovery of which he instituted the proceedings, if he wishes to retain his office.","Item placuit ut si quis episcopus, presbyter aut diaconus, qui criminali crimine pulsatus fuerit aut civilem causam habuerit, noluerit ecclesiastico iudicio iudicari, sed saecularibus iudiciis iudicari voluerit, etiamsi in causa vicerit, honorem tamen amittat. Hoc in criminali causa; in civili vero perdat quod repetendo egit, si honorem retinere voluerit."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p93","Canon XV.","This also seemed good, that if from some ecclesiastical judges an appeal was taken to other ecclesiastical judges who had a superior jurisdiction, this should in no way injure the reputation of those from whom the appeal was taken, unless it could be shown that they had given sentence moved by hatred or some other mental bias, or that they had been in some way corrupted. But if by the consent of both parties judges had been chosen, even if they were fewer in number than is specified, no appeal can be taken.","Illud etiam placuit, ut si ab ecclesiasticis iudicibus ad alios iudices ecclesiasticos qui superiorem iurisdictionem habent appellatum fuerit, hoc nullo modo laedat existimationem eorum a quibus appellatum est, nisi ostendi possit eos vel odio vel aliquo animi vitio commotos sententiam protulisse, vel aliquo modo corruptos fuisse. Si autem ex consensu utriusque partis iudices electi fuerint, etiamsi pauciores fuerint quam constitutum est, appellari non potest."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p94","Canon XV.","And [it seemed good] that the sons of bishops should not take part in nor witness secular spectacles. For this has always been forbidden to all Christians, so let them abstain from them, that they may not go where cursing and blasphemy are to be found.","Et [placuit] ut filii episcoporum spectacula saecularia non exerceant nec videant. Hoc enim omnibus christianis semper interdictum est, ab his ergo abstineant, ne eant ubi maledicta et blasphemiae inveniuntur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p95","Ancient Epitome of Canon XV.","A bishop or cleric who has a criminal suit brought against him, if he leaves the Church and betakes himself to secular judges even if he had been unjustly used, shall lose his rank. And if he was successful in his political affairs, if he follows this, he shall lose his own grade. No appeal can be taken from the ecclesiastical judges, except they be proved to have given their decision beforehand moved thereto by a bribe or by hatred. No appeal can be taken from the decision of judges chosen by each side.","Episcopus vel clericus qui criminalem causam habens, si Ecclesiam relinquit et ad saeculares iudices confugit, etiamsi laesus fuerit, gradum amittat. Et si in sua causa saeculari victor extiterit, si hoc facit, proprium gradum perdat. Ab ecclesiasticis iudicibus appellari non potest, nisi probentur praeiudicato animo vel odio vel pretio corrupti sententiam protulisse. A iudicibus ab utraque parte electis appellari non potest."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p96","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XV.","This canon is made up of Canons ix., x., and xj. of the Council of Hippo, A.D. 393.","Hic canon conflatus est ex Canonibus IX., X. et XI. Concilii Hipponensis, anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p97","Canon XVI.","That no bishop, presbyter or deacon should be a \"conductor\"; and that Readers should take wives; and that the clergy should abstain from usury; and at what age they or virgins should be consecrated.","Ut nullus episcopus, presbyter aut diaconus conductor sit; et ut lectores uxores accipiant; et ut clerici a foenore abstineant; et qua aetate ipsi vel virgines consecrentur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p98","Canon XVI.","LIKEWISE it seemed good that bishops, presbyters, and deacons should not be \"conductors\" or \"procurators\"; nor seek their food by any base and vile business, for they should remember how it is written, \"No man fighting for God cumbereth himself with worldly affairs.\"","Item placuit ut episcopi, presbyteri et diacones non sint conductores vel procuratores; nec aliquo turpi et vili quaestu victum suum quaerant, quia meminisse debent quia scriptum est: \"Nemo militans Deo implicat se negotiis saecularibus.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p99","Canon XVI.","Also it seemed good that Readers when they come to years of puberty, should be compelled either to take wives or else to profess continence.","Item placuit ut lectores cum ad annos pubertatis pervenerint, cogantur aut uxores accipere aut continentiam profiteri."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p100","Canon XVI.","Likewise it seemed good that if a clergyman had lent money he should get it back again, but if kind (speciem) he should receive back the same kind as he gave.","Item placuit ut si clericus pecuniam crediderit, recipiat; si speciem, eandem speciem recipiat quam dedit."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p101","Canon XVI.","And that younger than twenty-five years deacons should not be ordained, nor virgins consecrated.","Et ut minores viginti quinque annis diacones non ordinentur, nec virgines consecrentur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p102","Canon XVI.","And that readers should not salute the people.","Et ut lectores populum non salutent."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p103","Ancient Epitome of Canon XVI.","A bishop, presbyter, and deacon may not be a \"conductor\" or a \"procurator.\" A reader when he comes to puberty must contract marriage or profess continence. A cleric who has lent to someone, what he gave let him receive, or as much. Let not him be a deacon, who is made a deacon being under twenty-five. And let not readers salute the people.","Episcopus, presbyter et diaconus non sint conductores vel procuratores. Lector cum ad pubertatem venerit, aut matrimonium contrahat aut continentiam profiteatur. Clericus qui crediderit alicui, quod dedit recipiat, vel tantundem. Non sit diaconus qui sub viginti quinque annis diaconus ordinatur. Et lectores populum non salutent."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p104","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XVI.","This canon is made up of Canons xv., xviij., and xxj., and added to these Canon j. of the same Second Series of the synod of Hippo, A.D. 393.","Hic canon conflatus est ex Canonibus XV., XVIII. et XXI., et additus his Canon I. eiusdem Secundae Seriei synodi Hipponensis, anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p105","Canon XVII.","That any province on account of its distance, may have its own Primate.","Ut aliqua provincia propter longinquitatem suum proprium Primatem habeat."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p106","Canon XVII.","IT seemed good that Mauretania Sitiphensis, as it asked, should have a Primate of its own, with the consent of the Primate of Numidia from whose synod it had been separated. And with the consent of all the primates of the African Provinces and of all the bishops permission was given, by reason of the great distance between them.","Placuit ut Mauretania Sitiphensis, sicut petivit, proprium habeat Primatem, consentiente Primati Numidiae a cuius synodo separata fuerat. Et consentientibus omnibus primatibus Africanarum Provinciarum et omnibus episcopis permissa est propter magnam inter eos distantiam."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p107","Ancient Epitome of Canon XVII.","Mauretania Sitiphensis, on account of the great distance, is permitted to have its own Primate.","Mauretania Sitiphensis, propter magnam distantiam, permittitur proprium habere Primatem."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p108","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XVII.","This canon is Canon iij. of the first series of canons enacted at Hippo in 393.","Hic canon est Canon III. primae seriei canonum Hippone editorum anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p109","CANON XVIII. (Gk. xviii.)","If any cleric is ordained he ought to be admonished to observe the constitutions. And that neither the Eucharist nor Baptism should be given to the bodies of the dead. And that every year in every province the Metropolitans come together in synod.","Si quis clericus ordinatur, admoneri debet ut constitutiones observet. Et ut nec Eucharistia nec Baptismus corporibus mortuorum detur. Et ut omni anno in omni provincia Metropolitani in synodum conveniant."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p110","CANON XVIII. (Gk. xviii.)","(Gk. Canon xix.) IT seemed good that before bishops, or clerics were ordained, the provisions of the canons should be brought to their notice, lest, they might afterwards repent of having through ignorance acted contrary to law.","(Gr. Canon XIX.) Placuit ut antequam episcopi vel clerici ordinentur, constitutiones canonum eis innotescant, ne postea ignorentes contra legem fecisse poeniteat."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p111","Ancient Epitome of Greek Canon XIX.","The things which have been adopted by the synods should be made known to him who is to be ordained.","Ea quae a synodis constituta sunt, ei qui ordinandus est innotescant."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p112","Epitome Antiqua Canonis Graeci XIX.","(Gk. Canon xx.) It also seemed good that the Eucharist should not be given to the bodies of the dead. For it is written: \"Take, Eat,\" but the bodies of the dead can neither \"take\" nor \"eat.\" Nor let the ignorance of the presbyters baptize those who are dead.","(Gr. Canon XX.) Item placuit ut corporibus mortuorum Eucharistia non detur. Scriptum est enim: \"Accipite, Manducate,\" sed corpora mortuorum nec \"accipere\" nec \"manducare\" possunt. Nec ignorantia presbyterorum mortuos baptizet."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p113","Ancient Epitome of Greek Canon XX.","The Eucharist is not to be given to the body of one dead for it neither eats nor drinks. The ignorance of a presbyter shall not baptize a dead man.","Corpori mortui non detur Eucharistia, quia nec manducat nec bibit. Ignorantia presbyteri non baptizet mortuum."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p114","Epitome Antiqua Canonis Graeci XX.","(Gk. Canon xxi.) And therefore in this holy synod should be confirmed in accordance with the Nicene decrees, on account of Ecclesiastical causes, which often are delayed to the injury of the people, that every year there should be a synod, to which all, who are primates of the provinces, should send bishops as legates, from their own synods, two or as many as they choose; so that when the synod meets it may have full power to act.","(Gr. Canon XXI.) Et ideo in hac sancta synodo confirmetur secundum Nicaena decreta, propter causas Ecclesiasticas, quae saepe in detrimentum populi differuntur, ut omni anno synodus fiat, ad quam omnes qui primatum in provinciis obtinent, de suis synodis duos vel quot elegerint episcopos legatos mittant; ita ut synodus congregata plenam habeat potestatem agendi."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p115","Ancient Epitome of Greek Canon XXI.","According to the decrees of the Nicene Fathers a yearly synod shall be assembled, and two legates or as many as they shall choose, shall be sent by the primates of every province.","Secundum decreta Nicenorum Patrum annua synodus congregetur, et duo legati vel quot elegerint, a primatibus cuiuslibet provinciae mittantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p116","Epitome Antiqua Canonis Graeci XXI.","This is composed of Canons II., IV., and V. of the second series of enactments of Hippo, A.D. 393.","Hic conflatus est ex Canonibus II., IV. et V. secundae seriei constitutionum Hipponensium, anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p117","CANON XIX. (Greek xxii.)","That if any bishop is accused the cause should be brought before the primate of his own province.","Ut si quis episcopus accusatus fuerit, causa eius ad primatem propriae provinciae deferatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p118","CANON XIX. (Greek xxii.)","AURELIUS, the bishop, said: Whatever bishop is accused the accuser shall bring the case before the primates of the province to which the accused belongs, and he shall not be suspended from communion by reason of the crime laid to his charge unless he fails to put in an appearance on the appointed day for arguing his cause before the chosen judges, having been duly summoned by the letters; that is, within the space of one month from the day in which he is found to have received the letters. But should he be able to prove any true necessity which manifestly rendered it impossible for him to appear, he shall have the opportunity of arguing his case within another full month; but after the second month he shall not communicate until he is acquitted.","AURELIUS episcopus dixit: Quicumque episcopus accusatus fuerit, accusator causam deferat ad primates provinciae ad quam accusatus pertinet, et non suspendatur a communione propter crimen illi obiectum, nisi die constituta ad causam agendam coram electis iudicibus non occurrerit, legitime per epistolas evocatus; id est, intra spatium unius mensis a die quo epistolas recepisse claruerit. Quod si probare potuerit veram aliquam necessitatem quae ei manifeste occurrere non potuisset, habeat licentiam causam suam agendi intra alium integrum mensem; post secundum vero mensem non communicet, donec absolvatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p119","CANON XIX. (Greek xxii.)","But if he is not willing to come to the annual general council, so that his cause may there be terminated, he himself shall be judged to have pronounced the sentence of his own condemnation at the time in which he does not communicate, nor shall he communicate either in his own church or diocese. But his accuser, if he has not missed any of the days for pleading the cause, shall not be shut out from communion; but if he has missed some of them, withdrawing himself, then the bishop shall be restored to communion and the accuser shall be removed from communion; so, nevertheless, that the possibility of going on with the case be not taken from him, if he shall prove that his absence was caused by lack of power and not by lack of will.","Quod si ad anniversarium universale concilium noluerit occurrere, ut ibi causa eius terminetur, ipse se damnationis suae sententiam protulisse iudicabitur eo tempore quo non communicat, nec communicet nec in sua ecclesia nec in sua dioecesi. Accusator autem eius, si nullum diem causae agendae praetermiserit, non excludatur a communione; si vero aliquos dies praetermiserit se subtrahendo, tunc episcopus communioni restituatur et accusator a communione removeatur; ita tamen ut non ei tollatur facultas causam persequendi, si probaverit suam absentiam non voluntate sed impotentia factam."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p120","CANON XIX. (Greek xxii.)","And this is enacted, that if the accuser turn out to be himself a criminal when the case against the bishop has come to argument, he shall not be allowed to testify unless he asserts that the causes are personal and not ecclesiastical.","Et hoc constitutum est, ut si accusator ipse criminalis exstiterit, cum causa episcopi ad discussionem venerit, non admittatur ad testimonium, nisi causas personales et non ecclesiasticas esse dixerit."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p121","Ancient Epitome of Canon XIX.","A bishop accused and haled to judgment shall have the space of two months; if there is any excuse for his delay from the other side. But after this he shall be excommunicated if he does not appear. But if when the accused is present the accuser flees, then the accuser shall be deprived of communion. But the accuser who is infamous shall not be an accuser at all.","Episcopus accusatus et in iudicium vocatus habeat spatium duorum mensium; si qua est excusatio morae eius ex adverso. Post hoc autem excommunicetur si non occurrerit. Si autem praesente accusatore accusator fugerit, tunc accusator communione privetur. Accusator autem infamis omnino accusator non sit."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p122","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XIX.","This canon is made up from Canons VI. and VII. of the last mentioned second series of the enactments of Hippo, 393.","Hic canon conflatus est ex Canonibus VI. et VII. ultimae memoratae secundae seriei constitutionum Hipponensium, anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p123","CANON XX. (Greek xxiii.)","Of accused presbyters or clerks.","De presbyteris vel clericis accusatis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p124","CANON XX. (Greek xxiii.)","BUT if presbyters or deacons shall have been accused, there shall be joined together from the neighbouring places with the bishop of the diocese, the legitimate number of colleagues, whom the accused shall seek from the same; that is together with himself six in the case against a presbyter, in that against a deacon three. They shall discuss the causes, and the same form shall be kept with regard to days and postponements and removals from communion, and in the discussion of persons between the accusers and the accused.","Si autem presbyteri aut diacones accusati fuerint, ex vicinis locis cum episcopo dioecesis societur legitimus numerus collegarum, quos ipse accusatus ab eisdem petierit; id est, adversus presbyterum secum sex, adversus diaconum tres. Causas discutiant, et eadem forma servetur tam de diebus et dilationibus et communione removenda, quam de personarum discussione inter accusatores et accusatos."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p125","CANON XX. (Greek xxiii.)","But the causes of the rest of the clergy, the bishop of the place shall take cognizance of and determine alone.","Ceterorum autem clericorum causas episcopus loci solus cognoscat et terminet."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p126","Ancient Epitome of Canon XX.","When a presbyter is accused, six of the neighbouring bishops together with the bishop of that region shall judge the matter. But for a deacon, three. What things concern the other clerics even one bishop shall examine.","Presbytero accusato, sex episcopi vicini cum episcopo regionis illius causam iudicent. Pro diacono autem tres. Quae ad ceteros clericos pertinent, unus etiam episcopus examinet."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p127","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XX.","This is Canon viii. of Hippo, 393.","Hic est Canon VIII. Hipponensis, anni 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p128","CANON XXI. (Greek xxiv.)","That the sons of clergymen are not to be joined in marriage with heretics.","Ut filii clericorum haereticis matrimonio non iungantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p129","CANON XXI. (Greek xxiv.)","LIKEWISE it seemed good that the sons of clergymen should not be joined in matrimony with gentiles and heretics.","Item placuit ut filii clericorum cum gentilibus et haereticis matrimonio non iungantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p130","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXI.","[The same as the canon.]","[Idem quod canon.]"],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p131","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XXI.","This is Canon xij. of Hippo, 393.","Hic est Canon XII. Hipponensis, anni 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p132","CANON XXII. (Greek xxv.)","That bishops or other clergymen shall give nothing to those who are not Catholics.","Ut episcopi vel ceteri clerici nihil donent his qui non sunt Catholici."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p133","CANON XXII. (Greek xxv.)","AND that to those who are not Catholic Christians, even if they be blood relations, neither bishops nor clergymen shall give anything at all by way of donation of their possessions.","Et ut his qui non sunt Catholici Christiani, etiamsi consanguinei sint, nec episcopi nec clerici omnino aliquid donando de facultatibus suis largiantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p134","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXII.","Bishops and clergymen shall give nothing of their goods to heretics, nor confer aught upon them even if they be their relatives.","Episcopi et clerici nihil de bonis suis haereticis donent, nec eis aliquid conferant etiam si sint eorum propinqui."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p135","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XXII.","This is Canon xiv. of Hippo, 393.","Hic est Canon XIV. Hipponensis, anni 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p136","CANON XXIII. (Greek xxvi.)","That bishops shall not go across seas.","Ut episcopi trans mare non vadant."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p137","CANON XXIII. (Greek xxvi.)","ITEM, That bishops shall not go beyond seas without consulting the bishop of the primatial see of his own province: so that from him they may be able to receive a formed or commendatory letter.","Item, ut episcopi sine consultu primatis suae provinciae trans mare non vadant: ut ab eo possint formatam vel commendaticiam epistolam accipere."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p138","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXIII.","A bishop is not to cross the seas unless he has received from the Primate of his region a letter dimissory.","Episcopus trans mare non transgrediatur, nisi a Primati regionis suae litteras dimissorias acceperit."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p139","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XXIII.","This is Canon xxvij. of Hippo, 393.","Hic est Canon XXVII. Hipponensis, anni 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p140","CANON XXIV. (Greek xxvii.)","That nothing be read in church besides the Canonical Scripture.","Ut nihil in ecclesia legatur praeter Scripturam Canonicam."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p141","CANON XXIV. (Greek xxvii.)","ITEM, that besides the Canonical Scriptures nothing be read in church under the name of divine Scripture.","Item, ut praeter Scripturas Canonicas nihil in ecclesia legatur sub nomine divinarum Scripturarum."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p142","CANON XXIV. (Greek xxvii.)","But the Canonical Scriptures are as follows: Genesis. Exodus. Leviticus. Numbers. Deuteronomy. Joshua the Son of Nun. The Judges. Ruth. The Kings, iv. books. The Chronicles, ij. books. Job. The Psalter. The Five books of Solomon. The Twelve Books of the Prophets. Isaiah. Jeremiah. Ezechiel. Daniel. Tobit. Judith. Esther. Ezra, ij. books. Macchabees, ij. books.","Canonicae autem Scripturae sunt hae: Genesis, Exodus, Leviticus, Numeri, Deuteronomium, Iesus Nave, Iudicum, Ruth, Regnorum libri IV, Paralipomenon libri II, Iob, Psalterium, Salomonis libri V, Duodecim libri Prophetarum, Isaias, Hieremias, Ezechiel, Daniel, Tobias, Iudith, Hester, Hesdrae libri II, Machabaeorum libri II."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p143","CANON XXIV. (Greek xxvii.)","THE NEW TESTAMENT. The Gospels, iv. books. The Acts of the Apostles, j. book. The Epistles of Paul, xiv. The Epistles of Peter, the Apostle, ij. The Epistles of John the Apostle, iij. The Epistles of James the Apostle, j. The Epistle of Jude the Apostle, j. The Revelation of John, j. book.","NOVI AUTEM TESTAMENTI. Evangelia libri IV, Actus Apostolorum liber I, Epistulae Pauli XIV, Epistulae Petri Apostoli II, Epistulae Ioannis Apostoli III, Epistula Iacobi Apostoli I, Epistula Iudae Apostoli I, Apocalypsis Ioannis liber I."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p144","CANON XXIV. (Greek xxvii.)","Let this be sent to our brother and fellow bishop, Boniface, and to the other bishops of those parts, that they may confirm this canon, for these are the things which we have received from our fathers to be read in church.","Hoc etiam fratri et coepiscopo nostro Bonifacio et aliis illarum partium episcopis mittatur, ut hoc canonem confirment; haec sunt enim quae a patribus accepimus in ecclesia legenda."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p145","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXIV.","Let nothing besides the canonical Scriptures be read in church.","Nihil praeter Scripturas canonicas in ecclesia legatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p146","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XXIV.","This is Canon xxxvj. of Hippo., 393. The last phrase allowing the reading of the \"passions of the Martyrs\" on their Anniversaries is omitted from the African code.","Hic est Canon XXXVI. Hipponensis, anni 393. Ultima clausula permittens \"passiones Martyrum\" in eorum anniversariis legi omissa est ex codice Africano."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p147","CANON XXV. (Greek xxviii.)","Concerning bishops and the lower orders who wait upon the most holy mysteries. It has seemed good that these abstain from their wives.","De episcopis et inferioribus ordinibus qui sanctissimis mysteriis deserviunt. Placuit ut hi abstineant ab uxoribus suis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p148","CANON XXV. (Greek xxviii.)","AURELIUS, the bishop, said: We add, most dear brethren, moreover, since we have heard of the incontinency of certain clerics, even of readers, towards their wives, it seemed good that what had been enacted in divers councils should be confirmed, to wit, that subdeacons who wait upon the holy mysteries, and deacons, and presbyters, as well as bishops according to former statutes, should contain from their wives, so that they should be as though they had them not and unless they so act, let them be removed from office. But the rest of the clergy are not to be compelled to this, unless they be of mature age. And by the whole council it was said: What your holiness has said is just, holy, and pleasing to God, and we confirm it.","AURELIUS episcopus dixit: Addimus, fratres carissimi, insuper, quia audivimus incontinentiam quorundam clericorum, etiam lectorum, erga uxores suas, placuit ut quod in diversis conciliis constitutum erat confirmetur, ut subdiacones qui sacris mysteriis deserviunt, et diacones, et presbyteri, necnon et episcopi secundum priora statuta, abstineant ab uxoribus suis, ut sint quasi non habentes; et nisi sic egerint, ab officio removeantur. Ceteri autem clerici ad hoc non cogantur, nisi maturi aetatis sint. Et ab universo concilio dictum est: Quod sanctitas vestra dixit, iustum, sanctum et Deo placitum est, et nos confirmamus."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p149","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXV.","Those who handle holy things should abstain even from their own wives at the times of their ministration.","Qui sancta tractant, abstineant etiam ab uxoribus suis temporibus ministeriorum suorum."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p150","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XXV.","This is founded upon Canon iv. of the Council of Carthage, which met September 13th, 401, but the provisions are more stringent here, subdeacons as well as deacons being constrained to continence.","Hic fundatur super Canone IV. Concilii Carthaginensis, quod convenerat Idibus Septembribus, anno 401, sed provisiones sunt severiores hic, subdiaconibus sicut et diaconibus ad continentiam astringendis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p151","CANON XXVI. (Greek xxix.)","That no one should take from the possessions of the Church.","Ut nullus de possessionibus Ecclesiae auferat."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p152","CANON XXVI. (Greek xxix.)","LIKEWISE it seemed good that no one should sell anything belonging to the Church: that if there was no revenue, and other great necessity urged thereto, this might be brought before the Metropolitan of the province that he might deliberate with the appointed number of bishops whether this should be done: that if such urgent necessity lay upon any church that it could not take counsel beforehand, at least let it call together the neighbouring bishops as witnesses, taking care to refer all the necessities of his church to the council: and that if he shall not do this, he shall be held as responsible toward God, and as a seller in the eye of the council, and he shall have lost thereby his honour.","Item placuit ut nullus res Ecclesiae vendat: quod si nihil reddit, et alia magna necessitas urget, hoc ad Metropolitanum provinciae deferatur ut cum constituto numero episcoporum deliberet utrum hoc faciendum sit: quod si tanta urgens necessitas alicui ecclesiae incubuerit ut non possit ante consulere, saltem vicinos episcopos testes adhibeat, curans omnes necessitates ecclesiae suae ad concilium referre: et si hoc non fecerit, quasi reus apud Deum et quasi venditor in conspectu concilii habeatur, et honorem suum inde amittat."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p153","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXVI.","Church goods must not be sold. If they bring in no revenue they may be sold at the will of the bishops. If the necessity does not allow that consultation should take place, he who sells shall call together the neighbouring bishops. If he does not do so he shall be held responsible to God and to the Synod.","Res Ecclesiae non sunt vendendae. Si non reddunt, voluntate episcoporum possunt vendi. Si necessitas non permittit ut consultatio fiat, qui vendit vicinos episcopos convocet. Si non fecerit, Deo et Synodo reus teneatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p154","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XXVI.","This is Canon v. of the Synod of Carthage, Sept. 13th, 401.","Hic est Canon V. Synodi Carthaginensis, Idibus Septembribus, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p155","CANON XXVII. (Greek xxx.)","Presbyters and deacons convicted of the graver crimes shall not receive laying on of hands, like laymen.","Presbyteri et diacones maiorum criminum convicti non recipiant manus impositionem sicut laici."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p156","CANON XXVII. (Greek xxx.)","IT also was confirmed that if presbyters or deacons were convicted of any of the greater crimes on account of which it was necessary that they should be removed from the ministry, that hands should not be laid upon them as upon penitents, or as upon faithful layman, nor should it be permitted that they be baptized over again and then advanced to the clerical grade.","Item confirmatum est ut si presbyteri vel diacones aliquibus maioribus criminibus convicti fuerint propter quae necesse est ut a ministerio removeantur, non eis manus imponatur sicut paenitentibus, vel sicut fidelibus laicis, nec permittatur eos denuo baptizari et tunc ad gradum clericalem promoveri."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p157","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXVII.","A presbyter convicted and repenting, is not to be rebaptized as one to be advanced, neither as a layman is he to be reordained.","Presbyter convictus et paenitens, non rebaptizetur quasi promovendus, nec sicut laicus reordinetur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p158","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XXVII.","This is Canon xij. of the before-mentioned Council of Carthage, Sept. 13th, 401.","Hic est Canon XII. praedicti Concilii Carthaginensis, Idibus Septembribus, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p159","CANON XXVIII. (Greek xxxi.)","Presbyters, deacons, or clerics, who shall think good to carry appeals in their causes across the water shall not at all be admitted to communion.","Presbyteri, diacones vel clerici, qui putaverint causas suas trans mare per appellationem deferre, omnino non admittantur ad communionem."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p160","CANON XXVIII. (Greek xxxi.)","IT also seemed good that presbyters, deacons, and others of the inferior clergy in the causes which they had, if they were dissatisfied with the judgments of their bishops, let the neighbouring bishops with the consent of their own bishop hear them, and let the bishops who have been called in judge between them: but if they think they have cause of appeal from these, they shall not betake themselves to judgments from beyond seas, but to the primates of their own provinces, or else to an universal council, as has also been decreed concerning bishops. But whoso shall think good to carry an appeal across the water shall be received to communion by no one within the boundaries of Africa.","Item placuit ut presbyteri, diacones et ceteri inferioris gradus clerici in causis quas habuerint, si de iudiciis episcoporum suorum conquesti fuerint, vicini episcopi cum consensu episcopi sui eos audiant, et inter eos episcopi qui adhibiti fuerint iudicent: quod si ab his putaverint appellandum, non se ad transmarina iudicia conferant, sed ad primates suarum provinciarum, aut ad universale concilium, sicut et de episcopis saepe constitutum est. Qui autem trans mare appellandum putaverit, intra fines Africae a nullo ad communionem recipiatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p161","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXVIII.","Clerics who have been condemned, if they take exception to the judgment, shall not appeal beyond seas, but to the neighbouring bishops, and to their own; if they do otherwise let them be excommunicated in Africa.","Clerici damnati, si iudicium recusaverint, non trans mare appellent, sed ad vicinos episcopos et ad suos; si aliter fecerint, in Africa excommunicentur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p162","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XXVIII.","This canon is the same as Canon xvij. of the Synod of Carthage of 418, but it has some words with regard to appeals which that canon does not contain, viz.: \"Aut ad universale concilium, sicut et de episcopis saepe constitutum est.\"","Hic canon idem est cum Canone XVII. Synodi Carthaginensis anni 418, sed habet aliqua verba de appellationibus quae ille canon non continet, videlicet: \"Aut ad universale concilium, sicut et de episcopis saepe constitutum est.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p163","CANON XXIX. (Greek xxxii.)","If anyone who is excommunicated shall receive communion before his cause is heard he brings damnation on himself.","Si quis excommunicatus antequam causa eius audiatur communionem receperit, damnationem sibi ipsi acquirit."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p164","CANON XXIX. (Greek xxxii.)","LIKEWISE it pleased the whole Council that he who shall have been excommunicated for any neglect, whether he be bishop, or any other cleric, and shall have presumed while still under sentence, and his cause not yet heard, to receive communion, he shall be considered by so doing to have given sentence against himself.","Item placuit universae Synodo ut qui pro aliqua negligentia excommunicatus fuerit, sive episcopus sive quilibet alius clericus, et adhuc sub sententia constitutus, nondum causa eius audita, communionem praesumpserit accipere, ipse se hoc facto damnasse iudicetur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p165","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXIX.","One excommunicate who shall communicate before absolution sentences himself.","Excommunicatus qui ante absolutionem communicaverit, seipsum damnat."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p166","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XXIX.","This canon seems to be founded upon Canon iv. of Antioch.","Hic canon fundari videtur super Canone IV. Antiocheno."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p167","CANON XXX. (Greek xxxiii.)","Concerning the accused or accuser.","De accusato vel accusatore."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p168","CANON XXX. (Greek xxxiii.)","LIKEWISE it seemed good that the accused, or the accuser, if (living in the same place as the accused) he fears some evil may be done him by the tumultuous multitude, may choose for himself a place near by, where the cause may be determined, and where there will be no difficulty in producing the witnesses.","Item placuit ut accusatus vel accusator, si (in eodem loco cum accusato habitans) timet ne quid ei fiat a tumultuosa multitudine, eligat sibi locum vicinum ubi causa terminetur, et ubi nulla difficultas erit producendi testes."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p169","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXX.","Accuser or accused may select for himself a safe place if he fears violence.","Accusator vel accusatus eligat sibi locum tutum si vim timet."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p170","CANON XXXI. (Greek xxxiv.)","If certain clerics advanced by their own bishops are supercilious, let them not remain whence they are unwilling to come forth.","Si qui clerici ab episcopis suis promoti superciliosi fuerint, non remaneant unde noluerint exire."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p171","CANON XXXI. (Greek xxxiv.)","IT also seemed good that whoever of the clergy or of the deacons would not help the bishop in the necessities of the churches, when he wished to lift them to a higher position in his diocese, should no longer be allowed to exercise the functions of that grade from which they were not willing to be removed.","Item placuit ut quicumque de clero vel de diaconibus in necessitatibus ecclesiarum episcopo non adiuvaret, cum eos ad superiorem gradum in sua dioecesi levare voluisset, non diutius liceat ei fungi officio illius gradus a quo noluerit amoveri."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p172","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXXI.","Who despises a greater honour shall lose what he hath.","Qui maiorem honorem contemnit, amittat quod habet."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p173","CANON XXXII. (Greek xxxv.)","If any poor cleric, no matter what his rank may be, shall acquire any property, it shall be subject to the power of the bishop.","Si quis pauper clericus, cuiuscumque gradus sit, aliquam proprietatem acquisierit, episcopi potestati subiaceat."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p174","CANON XXXII. (Greek xxxv.)","IT also seemed good that bishops, presbyters, deacons and any other of the clergy, who when they were ordained had no possessions, and in the time of their episcopate or after they became clerics, shall purchase in their own names lands or any other property, shall be held guilty of the crime of intrenching upon the Lord's goods, unless, when they are admonished to do so, they place the same at the disposal of the Church. But should anything come to them personally by the liberality of anyone, or by succession from some relative, let them do what they will with it; if, however, they demand it back again, contrary to what they proposed, they shall be judged unworthy of ecclesiastical honour as back-sliders.","Item placuit ut episcopi, presbyteri, diacones et quilibet alii de clero, qui cum ordinarentur nihil possederunt, et in tempore episcopatus sui vel postquam clerici facti sunt, in nominibus suis agros vel quaslibet possessiones comparaverint, rei de crimine invadendarum rerum dominicarum habeantur, nisi, cum admoniti fuerint, easdem ecclesiae disposuerint. Quod si quid eis obvenerit ex alicuius liberalitate vel ex successione propinqui, faciant inde quod voluerint; si tamen contra propositum suum repetierint, quasi refugae indigni honore ecclesiastico iudicentur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p175","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXXII.","Whoso after his ordination although he has nothing yet buys a field, shall give it to the Church, unless he got it by succession from a relation or by pure liberality.","Quisquis post ordinationem suam, cum nihil habeat, tamen emit agrum, donet ecclesiae, nisi ex successione propinqui vel ex pura liberalitate acceperit."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p176","CANON XXXIII. (Greek xxxvi.)","That presbyters should not sell the goods of the Church in which they are constituted; and that no bishop can rightly use anything the title to which vests in the ecclesiastical maternal centre.","Ut presbyteri res Ecclesiae in qua constituti sunt non vendant; et ut nullus episcopus recte uti possit aliqua re cuius dominium ad matricem ecclesiasticam pertinet."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p177","CANON XXXIII. (Greek xxxvi.)","IT also seemed good that presbyters should not sell the ecclesiastical property where they are settled without their bishop's knowledge; and it is not lawful for bishops to sell the goods of the Church without the council or their presbyters being aware of it. Nor should the bishop without necessity usurp the property of the maternal Church.","Item placuit ut presbyteri sine episcopi sui conscientia res ecclesiasticas ubi consistunt non vendant; nec episcopis licet res Ecclesiae sine concilii vel presbyterorum suorum conscientia vendere. Nec episcopus sine necessitate usurpet res matricis Ecclesiae."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p178","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXXIII.","A presbyter is not to sell ecclesiastical property without the consent of the bishop. A bishop is not to sell without the approbation of his synod a country property.","Presbyter non vendat res ecclesiasticas sine consensu episcopi. Episcopus non vendat sine approbatione synodi suae possessionem ruralem."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p179","CANON XXXIV. (Greek xxxvii.)","That nothing of those things enacted in the Synod of Hippo is to be corrected.","Ut nihil eorum quae in Synodo Hipponensi constituta sunt corrigatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p180","CANON XXXIV. (Greek xxxvii.)","BISHOP EPIGONIUS said: In this summary (Breviarium) which was adopted at the Synod of Hippo, we think nothing should be amended, nor anything added thereto except that the day on which the holy Feast of Easter falls should be announced in Synod.","EPIGONIUS episcopus dixit: In hoc Breviario quod apud Hipponem in Synodo constitutum est, nihil putamus emendandum, nec aliquid ei addendum, nisi ut dies quo sanctum Pascha celebratur in Synodo annuntietur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p181","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXXIV.","Nothing is to be corrected in the synod of Hippo, nor anything added thereto, except that the time of celebrating Easter should be announced in time of synod.","Nihil corrigendum est in Synodo Hipponensi, nec aliquid ei addendum, nisi tempus celebrandi Paschae in tempore synodi annuntietur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p182","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XXXIV.","The first of these introductions is that of the Synod of Hippo in A.D. 393; the next that of Carthage in A.D. 394, and the third that of the same place, held August 28th, A.D. 397. This canon (number xxxiv. of the code) is the beginning of Canon v. of the last named Synod.","Prima harum introductionum est Synodi Hipponensis anno 393; secunda est Carthaginensis anno 394; et tertia eiusdem loci, habita V. Kalendas Septembres, anno 397. Hic canon (numeri xxxiv. codicis) est principium Canonis V. ultimae dictae Synodi."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p183","CANON XXXV. (Greek xxxviii.)","That bishops or clergymen should not easily set free their sons.","Ut episcopi vel clerici non facile suos filios liberos faciant."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p184","CANON XXXV. (Greek xxxviii.)","THAT bishops or clerics should not easily let their children pass out of their power; unless they were secure of their morals and age, that their own sins may pertain to them.","Ut episcopi vel clerici non facile filios suos de potestate sua dimittant; nisi de moribus et aetate eorum securi fuerint, ut sua peccata ad ipsos pertineant."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p185","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXXV.","Bishops and clergy shall not set their children free until their morals are established.","Episcopi et clerici non liberos faciant filios suos donec mores eorum stabiles facti fuerint."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p186","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XXXV.","This canon is Canon xiij. of the Synod of Hippo A.D. 393.","Hic canon est Canon XIII. Synodi Hipponensis, anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p187","CANON XXXVI. (Greek xxxix.)","That bishops or clergymen are not to be ordained unless they have made all their family Christians.","Ut episcopi vel clerici non ordinentur nisi omnem domum suam Christianam fecerint."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p188","CANON XXXVI. (Greek xxxix.)","NONE shall be ordained bishop, presbyters, or deacons before all the inmates of their houses shall have become Catholic Christians.","Non ordinetur episcopus, presbyteri vel diacones, priusquam omnes domesticos suos Catholicos Christianos fecerint."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p189","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXXVI.","He shall not be ordained who hath not made all his household orthodox.","Non ordinetur qui non omnem domum suam orthodoxam fecerit."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p190","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XXXVI.","This canon is Canon xvij. of the Synod of Hippo, A.D. 393.","Hic canon est Canon XVII. Synodi Hipponensis, anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p191","CANON XXXVII. (Greek xl.)","It is not lawful to offer anything in the Holy Mysteries except bread and wine mixed with water.","Non licet aliquid offerre in Sanctis Mysteriis nisi panem et vinum aquae mixtum."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p192","CANON XXXVII. (Greek xl.)","IN the sacraments of the body and blood of the Lord nothing else shall be offered than that which the Lord himself ordained, that is to say, bread and wine mixed with water. But let the first-fruits, whether honey or milk, be offered on that one most solemn day, as is wont, in the mystery of the infants. For although they are offered on the altar, let them have nevertheless their own benediction, that they may be distinguished from the sacraments of the Lord's body and blood; neither let there be offered as first-fruits anything other than grapes and corns.","In sacramentis corporis et sanguinis Domini nihil aliud offeratur quam quod ipse Dominus ordinavit, id est, panis et vinum aquae mixtum. Primitiae vero sive mel sive lac, illo uno sacratissimo die offerantur, sicut solet, in mysterio infantium. Quamvis enim offerantur super altare, habeant tamen suam benedictionem, ut distinguantur a sacramentis corporis et sanguinis Domini; nec offerantur aliae primitiae nisi uvarum et frugum."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p193","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXXVII.","Let bread and wine mixed with water only be offered.","Panis et vinum aquae mixtum solum offerantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p194","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XXXVII.","This canon is Canon xxiij. of the Synod of Hippo, A.D. 393.","Hic canon est Canon XXIII. Synodi Hipponensis, anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p195","CANON XXXVIII. (Greek xli.)","That clerics or those who are continent shall not visit virgins or widows.","Ut clerici vel continentes non visitent virgines vel viduas."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p196","CANON XXXVIII. (Greek xli.)","NEITHER clerics nor those who profess continence should enter the houses of widows or virgins without the bidding or consent of the bishops or presbyters: and then let them not go alone, but with some other of the clergy, or with those assigned by the bishop or presbyter for this purpose; not even bishops and presbyters shall go alone to women of this sort, except some of the clergy are present or some other grave Christian men.","Nec clerici nec continentes ingrediantur domos viduarum vel virginum sine iussu vel consensu episcoporum vel presbyterorum: et tunc non soli eant, sed cum aliquo alio de clero, vel cum his qui ab episcopo vel presbytero ad hoc deputati sunt; nec episcopi et presbyteri soli ad huiusmodi mulieres pergant, nisi adsint aliqui de clero vel alii graves Christiani viri."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p197","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXXVIII.","Clerics and those who are continent shall not go to widows or virgins, unless at the bidding of the bishop and presbyter: and even then not alone, but with those with whom presbyters and deacons visit them.","Clerici et continentes non pergant ad viduas vel virgines, nisi iubente episcopo et presbytero: et tunc non soli, sed cum his cum quibus presbyteri et diacones eas visitant."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p198","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XXXVIII.","This canon is canon xxiv. of the Synod of Hippo, A.D. 393.","Hic canon est Canon XXIV. Synodi Hipponensis, anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p199","CANON XXXIX. (Greek xlii.)","That a bishop should not be called the chief of the priests.","Ut episcopus non appelletur princeps sacerdotum."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p200","CANON XXXIX. (Greek xlii.)","THAT the bishop of the first see shall not be called Prince of the Priests or High Priest or any other name of this kind, but only Bishop of the First See.","Ut primae sedis episcopus non appelletur Princeps Sacerdotum vel Summus Sacerdos vel alio huiusmodi nomine, sed tantum Episcopus Primae Sedis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p201","Ancient Epitome of Canon XXXIX.","The first bishop shall not be called Prince of the Priests nor High Priest but Bishop of the first see.","Primus episcopus non appelletur Princeps Sacerdotum nec Summus Sacerdos sed Episcopus primae sedis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p202","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XXXIX.","This canon is Canon xxv. of the Synod of Hippo, A.D. 393.","Hic canon est Canon XXV. Synodi Hipponensis, anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p203","CANON XL. (Greek xliii.)","Concerning the non-frequenting of taverns by the clergy, except when travelling.","De non frequentandis tabernis a clero, nisi peregrinantibus."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p204","CANON XL. (Greek xliii.)","THAT the clergy are not to enter taverns for eating or drinking, nor unless compelled to do so by the necessity of their journey.","Ut clerici tabernas non ingrediantur ad manducandum vel bibendum, nec nisi necessitate itineris compulsi."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p205","Ancient Epitome of Canon XL.","A cleric on a journey may enter a tavern, otherwise not.","Clericus peregrinans potest intrare tabernam, aliter non."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p206","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XL.","This canon is Canon xxvj. of the Synod of Hippo, A.D. 393.","Hic canon est Canon XXVI. Synodi Hipponensis, anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p207","CANON XLI. (Greek xliv.)","That by men who are fasting sacrifices are to be offered to God.","Ut a ieiunis sacrificia Deo offerantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p208","CANON XLI. (Greek xliv.)","THAT the Sacraments of the Altar are not to be celebrated except by those who are fasting, except on the one anniversary of the celebration of the Lord's Supper; for if the commemoration of some of the dead, whether bishops or others, is to be made in the afternoon, let it be only with prayers, if those who officiate have already breakfasted.","Ut Sacramenta Altaris non celebrentur nisi a ieiunis, excepto uno anniversario die Cenae Domini; nam si commemoratio aliquorum defunctorum, sive episcoporum sive aliorum, post prandium facienda est, tantum cum orationibus fiat, si iam qui officiunt pranderint."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p209","Ancient Epitome of Canon XLI.","The holy mysteries are not offered except by those who are fasting.","Sancta mysteria non offerantur nisi a ieiunis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p210","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XLI.","This canon is Canon xxviij. of the Synod of Hippo, A.D. 393.","Hic canon est Canon XXVIII. Synodi Hipponensis, anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p211","CANON XLII. (Greek xiv.)","Concerning the not having feasts under any circumstances in churches.","De non habendis conviviis sub qualibet occasione in ecclesiis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p212","CANON XLII. (Greek xiv.)","THAT no bishops or clerics are to hold feasts in churches, unless perchance they are forced thereto by the necessity of hospitality as they pass by. The people, too, as far as possible, are to be prohibited from attending such feasts.","Ut nulli episcopi vel clerici convivia in ecclesiis exerceant, nisi forte necessitate hospitalitatis transeuntes cogantur. Populus quoque, quantum fieri potest, a frequentandis talibus conviviis prohibeatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p213","Ancient Epitome of Canon XLII.","A cleric is not to feast in a church, unless perchance he is driven thereto by the necessity of hospitality. This also is forbidden to the laity.","Clericus non convivetur in ecclesia, nisi forte necessitate hospitalitatis cogatur. Hoc etiam laicis interdictum est."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p214","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XLII.","This canon is Canon xxix. of the Synod of Hippo, A.D. 393.","Hic canon est Canon XXIX. Synodi Hipponensis, anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p215","CANON XLIII. (Greek xlvi.)","Concerning penitents.","De paenitentibus."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p216","CANON XLIII. (Greek xlvi.)","THAT to penitents the times of their penance shall be assigned by the will of the bishop according to the difference of their sins; and that a presbyter shall not reconcile a penitent without consulting the bishop, unless the absence of the bishop urges him necessarily thereto. But when of any penitent the offence has been public and commonly known, so as to have scandalized the whole Church, he shall receive imposition of the hand before the altar.","Ut paenitentibus tempora paenitentiae secundum differentiam peccatorum episcopi arbitrio constituantur; et ut presbyter sine consilio episcopi paenitentem non reconciliet, nisi absentia episcopi necessitate compellat. Cum autem alicuius paenitentis delictum publicum et omnibus notum fuerit, ut universam Ecclesiam scandalizaverit, ante altare manum impositionem accipiat."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p217","Ancient Epitome of Canon XLIII.","The bishops shall fix the time of penance for those doing penance according to their sins. A presbyter without his knowledge shall not reconcile one doing penance, even when necessity impels him thereto.","Episcopi tempora paenitentiae paenitentibus secundum peccata constituant. Presbiter sine eius conscientia non reconciliet paenitentem, etiam cum necessitas eum impellit."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p218","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XLIII.","This canon is canon xxx. of the Synod of Hippo, A.D. 393.","Hic canon est Canon XXX. Synodi Hipponensis, anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p219","CANON XLIV. (Greek xlvii.)","Concerning Virgins.","De Virginibus."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p220","CANON XLIV. (Greek xlvii.)","THAT holy virgins when they are separated from their parents by whom they have been wont to be guarded, are to be commended by the care of the bishop, or presbyter where the bishop is absent, to women of graver age, so that living with them they may take care of them, lest they hurt the reputation of the Church by wandering about.","Ut sanctae virgines cum a parentibus suis a quibus custodiri solebant separatae fuerint, episcopi vel presbyteri (ubi episcopus absens fuerit) cura gravioribus aetate mulieribus commendentur, ut cum eis habitantes custodiant eas, ne vagando Ecclesiae famam laedant."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p221","Ancient Epitome of Canon XLIV.","She who leaves her father for the sake of virginity is to be commended to grave women.","Quae propter virginitatem patrem relinquit, gravioribus mulieribus committatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p222","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XLIV.","This canon is Canon xxxj. of the Synod of Hippo, A.D. 393.","Hic canon est Canon XXXI. Synodi Hipponensis, anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p223","CANON XLV. (Greek xlviii.)","Concerning those who are sick and cannot answer for themselves.","De his qui aegrotant et pro se respondere non possunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p224","CANON XLV. (Greek xlviii.)","THAT the sick are to be baptized who cannot answer for themselves if their [servants] shall have spoken at their own proper peril a testimony of the good will [of the sick man].","Ut aegroti baptizentur qui pro se respondere non possunt, si sui [servi] suo periculo testimonium bonae voluntatis [aegroti] dederint."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p225","CANON XLV. (Greek xlviii.)","(Greek Canon xlix.) Concerning players who are doing penance and are converted to the Lord.","(Gr. Canon xlix.) De histrionibus qui paenitentiam agunt et convertuntur ad Dominum."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p226","CANON XLV. (Greek xlviii.)","THAT to players and actors and other persons of that kind, as also to apostates when they are converted and return to God, grace or reconciliation is not to be denied.","Ut histrionibus et scenicis et aliis huiusmodi personis, necnon et apostatis cum convertuntur et redeunt ad Deum, gratia vel reconciliatio non negetur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p227","Ancient Epitome of Canon XLV.","That he who cannot answer for himself on account of illness is to be baptized when he shall have given evidence of his desire. A repentant actor is to be received to penance.","Ut qui propter languorem pro se respondere non potest, baptizetur cum dederit indicium voluntatis suae. Histrio paenitens ad paenitentiam recipiatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p228","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XLV.","This canon is made up of Canons xxxij. and xxxiij. of the Synod of Hippo, A.D. 393.","Hic canon conflatus est ex Canonibus XXXII. et XXXIII. Synodi Hipponensis, anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p229","CANON XLVI. (Greek l.)","Concerning the passions of the martyrs.","De passionibus martyrum."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p230","CANON XLVI. (Greek l.)","THE passions of the Martyrs may be read when their anniversary days are celebrated.","Passiones Martyrum legantur cum dies anniversarii eorum celebrantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p231","Ancient Epitome of Canon XLVI.","The passions of the martyrs are to be read on their commemorations.","Passiones martyrum in commemorationibus eorum legantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p232","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XLVI.","This canon is the last part of Canon xxxvj. of the Synod of Hippo, A.D. 393.","Hic canon est ultima pars Canonis XXXVI. Synodi Hipponensis, anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p233","CANON XLVII. (Greek li.)","Concerning [the Donatists and] the children baptized by the Donatists.","De [Donatistis et] infantibus qui a Donatistis baptizati sunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p234","CANON XLVII. (Greek li.)","CONCERNING the Donatists it seemed good that we should hold counsel with our brethren and fellow priests Siricius and Simplician concerning those infants alone who are baptized by Donatists: lest what they did not do of their own will, when they should be converted to the Church of God with a salutary determination, the error of their parents might prevent their promotion to the ministry of the holy altar.","De Donatistis placuit ut cum fratribus et consacerdotibus nostris Siricio et Simpliciano consuleremus de solis infantibus qui a Donatistis baptizantur: ne quod non sua sponte fecerunt, cum salubri proposito ad Ecclesiam Dei conversi fuerint, error parentum eorum impediat promotionem ad ministerium sancti altaris."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p235","CANON XLVII. (Greek li.)","But when these things had been begun, Honoratus and Urbanus, bishops of Mauritania Sitifensis, said: When some time ago we were sent to your holiness, we laid aside what things had been written on this account, that we might wait for the arrival of our brethren the legates from Numidia. But because not a few days have passed in which they have been looked for and as yet they are not arrived, it is not fitting that we should delay any longer the commands we received from our brother-bishops; and therefore, brethren, receive our story with alacrity of mind. We have heard concerning the faith of the Nicene tractate: True it is that sacrifices are to be forbidden after breakfast, so that they may be offered as is right by those who are fasting, and this has been confirmed then and now.","His autem inchoatis, Honoratus et Urbanus episcopi Mauritaniae Sitifensis dixerunt: Cum olim ad sanctitatem vestram missi fuissemus, ea quae super hac re scripta erant deposuimus, ut expectaremus adventum fratrum nostrorum legatorum de Numidia. Sed quia non pauci dies transierunt quibus expectati sunt et adhuc non venerunt, non decet ut diutius differamus mandata quae a fratribus episcopis accepimus; ideoque, fratres, hilari animo narrationem nostram accipite. Audivimus de fide Niceni tractatus: Verum est ut sacrificia post prandium interdicantur, ut a ieiunis rite offerantur, et hoc tunc et nunc confirmatum est."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p236","Ancient Epitome of Canon XLVII.","When those in infancy baptized by Donatists are converted, this shall be no impediment to them. And the Holy Mysteries, as is right, are to be celebrated only by them fasting.","Cum infantes a Donatistis baptizati convertuntur, hoc eis non sit impedimento. Et Sancta Mysteria, sicut decet, non nisi a ieiunis celebrentur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p237","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XLVII.","This canon is made from Canon xxxvij. of the Synod of Hippo, A.D. 393, and from Canon j. of the Synod of Carthage of August 28th, A.D. 397.","Hic canon confectus est ex Canone XXXVII. Synodi Hipponensis, anno 393, et ex Canone I. Synodi Carthaginensis, V. Kalendas Septembres, anno 397."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p238","CANON XLVIII. (Greek lii.)","Of rebaptisms, reordinations, and translations of bishops.","De rebaptizationibus, reordinationibus, et translationibus episcoporum."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p239","CANON XLVIII. (Greek lii.)","BUT we suggest that we decree what was set forth by the wisdom of the plenary synod at Capua, that no rebaptisings, nor reordinations should take place, and that bishops should not be translated. For Cresconius, bishop of Villa Regis, left his own people and invaded the Church of Tubinia and having been admonished down to this very day, to leave, according to the decree, the diocese he had invaded, he treated the admonition with disdain. We have heard that the sentence pronounced against him has been confirmed; but we seek, according to our decree, that ye deign to grant that being driven thereto by necessity, it be free to us to address the rector of the province against him, according to the statutes of the most glorious princes, so that whoever is not willing to acquiesce in the mild admonition of your holiness and to amend his lawlessness, shall be immediately cast out by judicial authority. Aurelius the bishop said: By the observance of the constituted form, let him not be judged to be a member of the synod, if he has been asked by you, dear brethren, to depart and has refused: for out of his own contempt and contumacy he has fallen to the power of the secular magistrate. Honoratus and Urban the bishops said: This pleases us all, does it not? And all the bishops answered: It is just, it pleases us.","Sed suggerimus ut decernamus quod a plenaria Synodo Capuae sapienter constitutum est, ut nullae rebaptizationes nec reordinationes fiant, et ut episcopi non transferantur. Cresconius enim, episcopus Villae Regis, relicto populo suo, invasit Ecclesiam Tubiniensem, et admonitus usque in hodiernum diem ut secundum decretum dioecesim quam invaserat desereret, admonitionem contempsit. Audivimus sententiam adversus eum prolatam confirmatam esse; sed petimus, secundum decretum nostrum, ut dignemini concedere ut necessitate compulsi, liceat nobis rectorem provinciae adversus eum adire, secundum statuta gloriosissimorum principum, ut quicumque salubri admonitioni sanctitatis vestrae acquiescere et iniquitatem suam emendare noluerit, statim auctoritate iudiciaria expellatur. Aurelius episcopus dixit: Per observationem constitutae formae, non iudicetur membrum synodi esse, si a vobis, fratres carissimi, rogatus ut discederet et renuit: nam ex propria contemptia et contumacia in potestatem saecularis magistratus incidit. Honoratus et Urbanus episcopi dixerunt: Hoc omnibus nobis placet, nonne? Et omnes episcopi responderunt: Iustum est, placet nobis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p240","Ancient Epitome of Canon XLVIII.","Let there be no rebaptisms, nor reordinations nor translations of bishops. Therefore let Cresconius be forbidden by judicial authority, for he has left his own people, and has taken possession of the diocese of Ceneum, although ecclesiastically admonished that he was not to change.","Nullae fiant rebaptizationes, nec reordinationes nec translationes episcoporum. Ideo Cresconius auctoritate iudiciaria arceatur, quia reliquit populum suum, et dioecesim Cenei occupavit, licet ecclesiastice admonitus ne mutaret."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p241","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XLVIII.","This canon is Canon j., of the Synod of Carthage of August 28th. A.D. 397.","Hic canon est Canon I. Synodi Carthaginensis, V. Kalendas Septembres, anno 397."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p242","CANON XLIX. (Greek liii.)","How many bishops there should be to ordain a bishop.","Quanti episcopi debeant esse ad ordinandum episcopum."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p243","CANON XLIX. (Greek liii.)","HONORATUS and Urban, the bishops, said: We have issued this command, that (because lately two of our brethren, bishops of Numidia, presumed to ordain a pontiff,) only by the concurrence of twelve bishops the ordination of bishops be celebrated. Aurelius, the bishop, said: The ancient form shall be preserved, that not less than three suffice who shall have been designated for ordaining the bishop. Moreover, because in Tripoli, and in Arzug the barbarians are so near, for it is asserted that in Tripoli there are but five bishops, and out of that number two may be occupied by some necessity; but it is difficult that all of the number should come together at any place whatever; ought this circumstance to be an impediment to the doing of what is of utility to the Church? For in this Church, to which your holiness has deigned to assemble, we frequently have ordinations and nearly every Lord's day; could I frequently summon twelve, or ten, or about that number of bishops? But it is an easy thing for me to join a couple of neighbours to my littleness. Wherefore your charity will agree with me that this cannot be observed.","HONORATUS et Urbanus episcopi dixerunt: Hoc mandatum dedimus, ut (quia nuper duo fratres nostri episcopi Numidiae pontificem ordinare praesumpserunt) nonnisi per duodecim episcoporum concursum episcoporum ordinationes celebrentur. Aurelius episcopus dixit: Forma antiqua servabitur, ut non minus quam tres sufficiant qui ad ordinandum episcopum designati fuerint. Praeterea, quia in Tripoli et in Arzug barbari tam vicini sunt, nam asseritur in Tripoli non amplius quam quinque episcopos esse, ex quibus numero duo aliqua necessitate occupari possunt; difficile est autem ut omnes ex numero in unum quemlibet locum conveniant; hoc impedimento esse debet quominus id fiat quod Ecclesiae utile est? Nam in hac Ecclesia, ad quam sanctitas vestra convenire dignata est, frequenter ordinationes habemus et fere per omnem dominicam diem; numquid possum duodecim vel decem, vel circa hunc numerum episcoporum frequenter convocare? Mihi autem facile est duos vel tres vicinos ad meam parvitatem adiungere. Quapropter caritas vestra consentiet mecum hoc observari non posse."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p244","Ancient Epitome of Canon XLIX.","Fewer than three bishops do not suffice for the ordination of a bishop.","Minus quam tres episcopi non sufficiunt ad ordinationem episcopi."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p245","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XLIX.","This is Canon ij., of the Synod of Carthage, August 28th, 397.","Hic est Canon II. Synodi Carthaginensis, V. Kalendas Septembres, anno 397."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p246","CANON L. (Greek liv.)","How many bishops should be added to the number of those ordaining, if any opposition had been made to the one to be ordained.","Quanti episcopi addi debeant numero ordinantium, si aliqua contradictio facta fuerit ei qui ordinandus est."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p247","CANON L. (Greek liv.)","BUT this should be decreed, that when we shall have met together to choose a bishop, if any opposition shall arise, because such things have been treated by us, the three shall not presume to purge him who was to be ordained, but one or two more shall be asked to be added to the aforesaid number, and the persons of those objecting shall first be discussed in the same place (plebe) for which he was to be ordained. And last of all the objections shall be considered; and only after he has been cleared in the public sight shall he at last be ordained. If this agrees with the mind of your holiness, let it be confirmed by the answer of your worthiness. All the bishops said, We are well pleased.","Hoc autem decernendum est, ut cum convenerimus ad eligendum episcopum, si aliqua contradictio emerserit, quia talia a nobis tractata sunt, non praesumant tres eum qui ordinandus est purgare, sed unus aut duo alii addi rogentur praedicto numero, et personae contradicentium primum in eadem plebe pro qua ordinandus erat discutiatur. Et postremo obiectiones considerentur; et tunc demum, postquam in conspectu publici purgatus fuerit, tandem ordinetur. Si hoc animo sanctitatis vestrae concordat, responsione dignationis vestrae confirmetur. Omnes episcopi dixerunt: Valde placet."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p248","Ancient Epitome of Canon L.","If any controversy arise concerning a bishop who has been elected by three bishops, let two others be coöpted, and so let there be an examination made of his affairs; and if it shall appear that he is pure, let him be ordained.","Si qua contentio orta fuerit de episcopo qui a tribus episcopis electus est, duo alii cooptentur, et sic fiat examinatio negotiorum eius; et si purus apparuerit, ordinetur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p249","Epitome Antiqua Canonis L.","This canon is Canon iij., of the Synod of Carthage, Aug. 28th, 397.","Hic canon est Canon III. Synodi Carthaginensis, V. Kalendas Septembres, anno 397."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p250","CANON LI. (Greek lv.)","That the date of Easter is to be announced by the Church of Carthage.","Ut dies Paschae ab Ecclesia Carthaginensi annuntietur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p251","CANON LI. (Greek lv.)","HONORATUS and Urban, the bishops, said: Since all things treated by our commonitory are known, we add also what has been ordered concerning the day of Easter, that we be informed of the date always by the Church of Carthage, as has been accustomed and that no short time before. Aurelius, the bishop, said: If it seems good to your holiness, since we remember that we pledged ourselves sometime ago that every year we would come together for discussion, when we assemble, then let the date of the holy Easter be announced through the legates present at the Council. Honoratus and Urban, the bishops, said: Now we seek of the present assembly that ye deign to inform our province of that day by letters. Aurelius, the bishop, said: It is necessary it should be so.","HONORATUS et Urbanus episcopi dixerunt: Quoniam omnia quae a commonitorio nostro tractata sunt nota sunt, addimus etiam illud quod de die Paschae ordinatum est, ut semper a Carthaginensi Ecclesia de die certioremur, sicut consuetum est, nec parvo tempore ante. Aurelius episcopus dixit: Si sanctitati vestrae videtur, quoniam meminimus nos olim spopondisse ut omni anno ad tractatum conveniremus, cum convenimus, tunc dies sancti Paschae per legatos in Concilio praesentes annuntietur. Honoratus et Urbanus episcopi dixerunt: Nunc petimus a praesenti conventu ut dignemini provinciam nostram de illo die per epistolas certiorem facere. Aurelius episcopus dixit: Oportet ita fieri."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p252","Ancient Epitome of Canon LI.","Let the day on which Easter is to be kept be announced by the Church of Carthage in the annual synod.","Dies quo Pascha tenendum est a Carthaginensi Ecclesia in annua synodo annuntietur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p253","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LI.","This canon is the first part of Canon iv. of the Synod of Carthage, August 28th, 397.","Hic canon est prima pars Canonis IV. Synodi Carthaginensis, V. Kalendas Septembres, anno 397."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p254","CANON LII. (Greek lvi.)","Of visiting provinces.","De visitandis provinciis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p255","CANON LII. (Greek lvi.)","HONORATUS and Urban, the bishops, said: This was commanded to us in word, that because it had been decreed in the Council of Hippo that each province should be visited in the time of the council, that ye also deign that this year or next, according to the order ye have drawn up, you should visit the province of Mauritania.","HONORATUS et Urbanus episcopi dixerunt: Hoc nobis verbo mandatum est, ut quia in Concilio Hipponensi decretum est ut unaquaeque provincia in tempore concilii visitaretur, ut etiam vos dignemini hoc anno vel proximo, secundum ordinem quem constituistis, provinciam Mauritaniae visitare."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p256","CANON LII. (Greek lvi.)","Aurelius, the bishop, said: Of the province of Mauritania because it is situated in the confines of Africa, we have made no decree, for they are neighbours of the barbarians; but God grant (not however that I make any rash promise of doing so), we may be able to come to your province. For ye should consider, brethren, that this same thing our brethren of Tripoli and of the Arzuges region could demand also, if occasion offered.","Aurelius episcopus dixit: De provincia Mauritaniae quia in confinibus Africae sita est, nihil decrevimus, barbaris enim vicini sunt; sed Deus concedat (non tamen ut aliquam temerariam promissionem faciam) ut possimus ad provinciam vestram venire. Considerare enim debetis, fratres, quod hoc ipsum fratres nostri Tripolitani et Arzugum regionis, si occasio se dederit, postulare possunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p257","Ancient Epitome of Canon LII.","As the Synod at Hippo decreed, every province should be visited in an annual Synod.","Sicut Synodus Hipponensis decrevit, omnis provincia in annua Synodo visitetur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p258","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LII.","This canon is the last part of canon iv. of the Council of Carthage, August 28th, A.D. 397.","Hic canon est ultima pars canonis IV. Concilii Carthaginensis, V. Kalendas Septembres, anno 397."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p259","CANON LIII. (Greek lvii.)","That dioceses should not receive a bishop except by the consent of its own bishop.","Ut dioeceses non accipiant episcopum nisi consentiente proprio episcopo."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p260","CANON LIII. (Greek lvii.)","EPIGONIUS, the bishop, said: In many councils it has been decreed by the sacerdotal assembly that such communities as are contained in other dioceses and ruled by their bishops, and which never had any bishops of their own, should not receive rulers, that is bishops, for themselves except with the consent of the bishop under whose jurisdiction they have been. But because some who have attained a certain domination abhor the communion of the brethren, or at least, having become depraved, claim for themselves domination with what is really tyranny, for the most part tumid and stolid presbyters, who lift up their heads against their own bishops or else win the people to themselves by feasting them or by malignant persuasion, that they may by unlawful favour wish to place themselves as rulers over them; we indeed hold fast that glorious desire of your mind, most pious brother Aurelius, for thou hast often opposed these things, paying no heed to such petitioners; but on account of their evil thoughts and basely conceived designs this I say, that such a community, which has always been subject in a diocese, ought not to receive a rector, nor should it ever have a bishop of its own. Therefore if this which I have proposed seems good to the whole most holy council, let it be confirmed.","EPIGONIUS episcopus dixit: In multis conciliis sacerdotali conventu decretum est ut eae plebes quae in aliis dioecesibus continentur et ab episcopis suis reguntur, et quae numquam proprios episcopos habuerunt, non accipiant sibi rectores, id est episcopos, nisi consensu episcopi sub cuius iurisdictione fuerunt. Sed quia quidam qui quandam dominationem adepti sunt, fratrum communionem exhorrent, aut certe depravati facti, dominationem sibi cum tyrannide quadam vindicant, plerumque tumidi et stolidi presbyteri, qui contra proprios episcopos capita levant aut populum conviviis aut maligna persuasione sibi conciliant, ut se illis illicito favore rectores praeficere velint; nos quidem tenemus gloriosum illud animi tui desiderium, piissime frater Aureli, nam saepe haec opposuisti, talibus precatoribus nullam attentionem adhibens; sed propter eorum malas cogitationes et turpiter conceptas voluntates hoc dico, quod talis plebs, quae semper in dioecesi subiecta fuit, non debeat recipere rectorem, nec umquam proprium episcopum habere. Ideo si hoc quod proposui omni sanctissimo concilio videtur, confirmetur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p261","CANON LIII. (Greek lvii.)","Aurelius, the bishop, said: I am not in opposition to the proposition of our brother and fellow bishop: but I confess that this has been and shall be my practice concerning those who were truly of one mind, not only with regard to the Church of Carthage, but concerning every sacerdotal assemblage. For there are many who, as has been said, conspire with the people whom they deceive, tickling their ears and blandly seducing them, men of vicious lives, or at least puffed up and separated from this meeting, who think to watch over their own people, and never come to our council for fear that their wickedness should be discussed. I say, if it seems good, that not only should these not keep their dioceses, but that every effort should be made to have them expelled by public authority from that church of theirs which has evilly favoured them, and that they be removed even from the chief sees. For it is right that he who cleaves to all the brethren and the whole council, should possess with full right not only his church but also the dioceses. But they who think that the people suffice them and spurn the love of the brethren, shall not only lose their dioceses, but (as I have said,) they shall be deprived by public authority of their own cures as rebels. Honoratus and Urban, the bishops, said: The lofty provision of your holiness obtains the adherence of the minds of all of us, and I think that by the answer of all what you have deigned to propose will be confirmed. All the bishops said: Placet, placet.","Aurelius episcopus dixit: Non sum contrarius propositioni fratris et coepiscopi nostri: sed confiteor hoc fuisse et futurum esse meum propositum de his qui vere unanimes fuerunt, non solum respectu Ecclesiae Carthaginensis, sed respectu omnis sacerdotalis conventus. Multi enim sunt, sicut dictum est, qui conspirant cum populo quem decipiunt, aures eorum permulcentes et blande seducentes, homines vitiosae vitae, aut certe inflati et ab hoc conventu separati, qui proprios populos se custodire putant, et numquam ad concilium nostrum veniunt ne eorum nequitia discutiatur. Dico, si placet, quod non solum hi non debeant suas dioeceses retinere, sed omni modo agendum est ut auctoritate publica expellantur de ecclesia sua quae illis male favit, et etiam de primatibus sedibus removeantur. Iustum est enim ut qui omnibus fratribus et toti concilio adhaeret, pleno iure possideat non solum ecclesiam suam sed et dioeceses. Qui autem putant sibi sufficere populos et fratrum caritatem spernunt, non solum dioeceses suas amittant, sed (sicut dixi) auctoritate publica velut rebelles propriis sedibus priventur. Honoratus et Urbanus episcopi dixerunt: Alta provisio sanctitatis vestrae omnium nostrorum animos obtinet, et puto quod omnium responsione quae dignata est sanctitas vestra proponere confirmabuntur. Omnes episcopi dixerunt: Placet, placet."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p262","Ancient Epitome of Canon LIII.","Whoso shall neglect his call to a synod, and shall despise the charity of his brethren, putting his trust in the multitude who are with him, let him be deprived of them by the imperial authority.","Quisquis neglexerit vocationem suam ad synodum, et caritatem fratrum contempserit, confidentiam habens in multitudine quae cum eo est, auctoritate imperiali privetur eis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p263","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LIII.","This canon is Canon v. of the Synod of Carthage of August 28th, A.D. 397, beginning with the second clause.","Hic canon est Canon V. Synodi Carthaginensis, V. Kalendas Septembres, anno 397, incipiens a secunda clausula."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p264","CANON LIV. (Greek lviii.)","That a strange cleric is under no circumstances to be received by another.","Ut alienus clericus nulla ratione recipiatur ab alio."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p265","CANON LIV. (Greek lviii.)","EPIGONIUS, the bishop, said: This has been decreed in many councils, also just now it has been confirmed by your prudence, most blessed brethren, that no bishop should receive a strange cleric into his diocese without the consent of the bishop to whose jurisdiction the cleric belongs. But I say that Julian, who is ungrateful for the layouts bestowed upon him by God through my littleness, is so rash and audacious, that a certain man who was baptized by me, when he was a most needy boy, commended to me by the same, and when for many years he had been fed and reared by me, it is certain that this one, as I have said, was baptized in my church, by my own unworthy hands; this same man began to exercise the office of reader in the Mappalien diocese, and read there for nearly two years, with a most incomprehensible contempt of my littleness, the aforenamed Julian took this man, whom he declared to be a citizen of his own city Vazarita, and without consulting me ordained him deacon. If, most blessed brethren, that is permissible, let it be declared to us; but if not, let such an impudent one be restrained that he may in no way mix himself in someone's communion.","EPIGONIUS episcopus dixit: Hoc in multis conciliis decretum est, etiam nunc a prudentia vestra confirmatum est, fratres beatissimi, ut nullus episcopus alienum clericum in suam dioecesim recipiat sine consensu episcopi ad cuius iurisdictionem clericus pertinet. Dico autem quod Iulianus, qui ingratus est beneficiis a Deo per parvitatem meam sibi collatis, ita temerarius est et audax, ut quidam qui baptizatus est a me, cum esset puer pauperrimus, mihi ab eodem commendatus, et per multos annos a me nutritus et educatus, certum est quod iste, sicut dixi, in ecclesia mea baptizatus est, meis indignis manibus; idem ipse coepit officium lectoris in dioecesi Mappaliensi exercere, et ibi per duos fere annos legit, cum contemptu parvitatis meae incomprehensibili, praefatus Iulianus hunc hominem, quem civem suae civitatis Vazaritae esse dicebat, sine mea consultatione diaconum ordinavit. Si, beatissimi fratres, hoc licitum est, nobis declaretur; si autem non, talis impudens coerceatur ut nullo modo se alicuius communioni misceat."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p266","CANON LIV. (Greek lviii.)","Numidius, the bishop, said: If, as it seems, Julian did this without your worthiness being asked for his consent, nor even consulted, we all judge that this was done iniquitously and unworthily. Wherefore unless Julian shall correct his error, and shall return the cleric to your people with proper satisfaction, since what he did was contrary to the decrees of the council, let him be condemned and separated from us on account of his contumacy. Epigonius, the bishop, said: Our father in age, and most ancient by his promotion, that laudable man, our brother and colleague Victor wishes that this petition should be made general to all.","Numidius episcopus dixit: Si, ut videtur, Iulianus hoc fecit sine tua dignatione rogata, immo nec consulta, omnes iudicamus inique et indigne hoc factum esse. Quapropter nisi Iulianus correxerit errorem suum, et clericum populo tuo cum satisfactione reddiderit, quia contra decreta concilii fecit, condemnetur et separetur a nobis propter contumaciam suam. Epigonius episcopus dixit: Pater noster aetate, et antiquissimus promotione, vir laudabilis, frater et collega noster Victor vult ut haec petitio ad omnes generaliter fiat."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p267","Ancient Epitome of Canon LIV.","Since Julian has ordained a reader of Epigonius's to the diaconate, unless he shall shew authority received from him to do so, he shall increase the penalty of his contumacy.","Cum Iulianus lectorem Epigonii ad diaconatum ordinaverit, nisi ostenderit auctoritatem ab eo acceptam, augebit poenam contumaciae suae."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p268","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LIV.","This canon is Canon vj. of the Synod of Carthage, August 28th, A.D. 397.","Hic canon est Canon VI. Synodi Carthaginensis, V. Kalendas Septembres, anno 397."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p269","CANON LV. (Greek lix.)","That it be lawful for the bishop of Carthage to ordain a cleric whenever he wishes.","Ut episcopo Carthaginensi liceat ordinare clericum quandocumque voluerit."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p270","CANON LV. (Greek lix.)","AURELIUS, the bishop, said: My brethren, pray allow me to speak. It often happens that ecclesiastics who are in need seek deacons, or presbyters or bishops from me: and I, bearing in mind what things have been ordained these I observe, to wit, I summon the bishop of the cleric who is sought for, and I shew him the state of affairs, how that they of a certain church ask for a certain one of his clergy. Perchance then they make no objection, but lest it happen that afterwards they might object when in this case they shall have been demanded by me, who (as you know) have the care of many churches and of the ordinands. It is right therefore that I should summon a fellow bishop with two or three witnesses from our number. But if he be found indevotus, what does your charity think should be done? For I, as ye know, brethren, by the condescension of God have the care of all the churches.","AURELIUS episcopus dixit: Fratres, orate, permittite me loqui. Saepe accidit ut ecclesiastici qui indigent diacones, vel presbyteros vel episcopos a me petant: et ego, memor eorum quae ordinata sunt, haec observo, videlicet, convoco episcopum illius clerici qui petitur, et ostendo ei statum rei, quomodo illi alicuius ecclesiae aliquem de clero eius petant. Fortassis tunc non contradicunt, sed ne postea contingat ut contradicant, cum in hac causa a me postulati fuerint, qui (sicut scitis) multarum ecclesiarum et ordinandorum curam habeo. Oportet igitur ut convocem coepiscopum cum duobus vel tribus testibus de numero nostro. Quod si inventus fuerit indevotus, quid caritas vestra faciendum putat? Ego enim, sicut scitis, fratres, per Dei indulgentiam omnium ecclesiarum curam habeo."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p271","CANON LV. (Greek lix.)","Numidius, the bishop, said: This see always had the power of ordaining a bishop according to the desire of each Church as he wills and on whose name there was agreement. Epigonius, the bishop, said: Your good nature makes small use of your powers, for you make much less use of them than you might, since, my brother, you are good and gentle to all; for you have the power, but it is far from your practice to satisfy the person of each bishop in prima tantummodo conventione. But if it should be thought that the rights of this see ought to be vindicated, you have the duty of supporting all the churches, wherefore we do not give thee power, but we confirm that power thou hast, viz.: that thou hast the right at thy will always to choose whom thou wilt, to constitute prelates over peoples and churches who shall have asked thee to do so, and when thou so desirest. Posthumianus, the bishop, said: Would it be right that he who had only one presbyter should have that one taken away from him? Aurelius, the bishop, said: But there may be one bishop by whom many presbyters can be made through the divine goodness, but one fit to be made bishop is found with difficulty. Wherefore if any bishop has a presbyter necessary for the episcopate and has one only, my brother, as you have said, even that one he ought to give up for promotion. Posthumianus, the bishop, said: If some other bishop has plenty of clergy, should that other diocese come to my help? Aurelius, the bishop, said: Of course, when you have come to the help of another Church, he who has many clerics should be persuaded to make one over to you for ordination.","Numidius episcopus dixit: Haec sedes semper potestatem habuit ordinandi episcopum secundum desiderium uniuscuiusque Ecclesiae sicut vult et de cuius nomine conventus fuerit. Epigonius episcopus dixit: Bonitas tua parum utitur potestate tua, nam multo minus utris ea quam posses, cum, frater, bonus sis et mitis ad omnes; potestatem enim habes, sed longe est a consuetudine tua ut satisfacias personae uniuscuiusque episcopi in prima tantummodo conventione. Quod si putandum est iura huius sedis vindicanda esse, tu habes officium sustentandi omnes ecclesias, quapropter non damus tibi potestatem, sed confirmamus potestatem quam habes, videlicet: quod habes ius ad voluntatem tuam semper eligendi quem volueris, constituendi praelatos super populos et ecclesias qui te rogaverint, et quando volueris. Posthumianus episcopus dixit: Iustum est ut qui unum tantum presbyterum haberet, ille unus ei auferatur? Aurelius episcopus dixit: Sed potest esse unus episcopus a quo multi presbyteri per divinam bonitatem fieri possunt, sed unus qui episcopus fieri dignus sit difficilius invenitur. Quapropter si quis episcopus habet presbyterum ad episcopatum necessarium et unum tantum habet, frater mi, sicut dixisti, etiam illum dare debet ad promotionem. Posthumianus episcopus dixit: Si aliquis alius episcopus multos clericos habet, debet illa altera dioecesis mihi succurrere? Aurelius episcopus dixit: Utique, cum alteri Ecclesiae succurreris, qui multos clericos habet suadendus est ut unum tibi ad ordinationem concedat."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p272","Ancient Epitome of Canon LV.","It is lawful for the bishop of Carthage, whenever he wills, to choose those who are to be set over the churches: even if there were only one presbyter worth of rule. For one bishop can ordain many presbyters, but one fit for the episcopate is hard to find.","Licet episcopo Carthaginensi, quandocumque voluerit, eligere eos qui praeficiendi sunt ecclesiis: etiam si solus presbyter fuerit regimine dignus. Potest enim unus episcopus multos presbyteros ordinare, sed unus ad episcopatum idoneus difficilius invenitur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p273","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LV.","This canon is the first half of Canon vij. of the Council of Carthage held August 28th A.D. 397.","Hic canon est prima pars Canonis VII. Concilii Carthaginensis habiti V. Kalendas Septembres, anno 397."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p274","CANON LVI. (Greek lx.)","That bishops who were ordained for dioceses shall not choose for themselves dioceses [in the Greek provinces].","Ut episcopi qui pro dioecesibus ordinati sunt non sibi eligant dioeceses [in Graecis provinciis]."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p275","CANON LVI. (Greek lx.)","HONORATUS and Urban, the bishops, said: We have heard that it has been decreed that dioceses should not be deemed fit to receive bishops, unless with the consent of their founder: but in our province since some have been ordained bishops in the diocese, by the consent of that bishop by whose power they were established, have even seized dioceses for themselves, this should be corrected by the judgment of your charity, and prohibited for the future. Epigonius, the bishop, said: To every bishop should be reserved what is right, so that from the mass of dioceses no part should be snatched away, so as to have its own bishop, without consent from the proper authority. For it shall suffice, if the consent be given, that the diocese thus set apart have its own bishop only, and let him not seize other dioceses, for only the one cut off from the many merited the honour of receiving a bishop. Aurelius, the bishop, said: I do not doubt that it is pleasing to the charity of you all, that he who was ordained for a diocese by the consent of the bishop who held the mother see, should retain only the people for whom he was ordained. Since therefore I think that everything has been treated of, if all things are agreeable to your mind, pray confirm them all by your suffrage. All the bishops said: We all are well pleased, and we have confirmed them with our subscription. And they signed their names.","HONORATUS et Urbanus episcopi dixerunt: Audivimus decretum esse ut dioeceses non debeant dignae haberi ad recipiendos episcopos, nisi cum consensu fundatoris sui: sed in provincia nostra, cum quidam in dioecesi ordinati sint episcopi, consensu illius episcopi a quo constituti sunt, etiam dioeceses sibi usurpaverunt, hoc iudicio caritatis vestrae corrigendum est, et in futurum prohibendum. Epigonius episcopus dixit: Unicuique episcopo suum ius reservetur, ut de multitudine dioecesium nulla pars abripiatur, ut proprium episcopum habeat, sine consensu ab auctoritate debita. Sufficiet enim, si consensus datus fuerit, ut dioecesis ita separata proprium tantum episcopum habeat, et non alias dioeceses invadat, sola enim quae de multis abscissa est meruit honorem recipiendi episcopum. Aurelius episcopus dixit: Non dubito placere caritati vestrae universae, ut qui pro dioecesi ordinatus est consensu episcopi qui matricem sedem tenuit, solum populum pro quo ordinatus est retineat. Cum igitur putem omnia tractata esse, si cuncta animo vestro congruunt, rogo ut ea omnia vestro suffragio confirmetis. Omnes episcopi dixerunt: Omnes valde placati sumus, et ea subscriptione nostra confirmavimus. Et subscriptserunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p276","Ancient Epitome of Canon LVI.","If any diocese has received consent to have a bishop of its own from him who has the right, that one shall not invade the rest of the dioceses.","Si qua dioecesis consensum acceperit ut proprium episcopum habeat ab eo qui ius habet, ille non invadet reliquas dioeceses."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p277","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LVI.","This is the last part of Canon vij. of the Synod of Carthage, August 28, A.D. 397.","Hic est ultima pars Canonis VII. Synodi Carthaginensis, V. Kalendas Septembres, anno 397."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p278","CANON LVII. (Greek lxi.)","That persons baptized when children by the Donatists may be ordained clergymen in the Catholic Church.","Ut personae ab infantia a Donatistis baptizatae possint in Ecclesia Catholica clerici ordinari."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p279","CANON LVII. (Greek lxi.)","SINCE in the former council it was decreed, as your unanimity remembers as well as I do, that those who as children were baptized by the Donatists, and not yet being able to know the pernicious character of their error, and afterward when they had come to the use of reason, had received the knowledge of the truth, abhorred their former error, and were received, (in accordance with the ancient order) by the imposition of the hand, into the Catholic Church of God spread throughout the world, that to such the remembrance of the error ought to be no impediment to the reception of the clerical office. For in coming to faith they thought the true Church to be their own and there they believed in Christ, and received the sacraments of the Trinity. And that all these sacraments are altogether true and holy and divine is most certain, and in them the whole hope of the soul is placed, although the presumptuous audacity of heretics, taking to itself the name of the truth, dares to administer them. They are but one after all, as the blessed Apostle tells us, saying: \"One God, one faith, one baptism,\" and it is not lawful to reiterate what once only ought to be administered. [Those therefore who have been so baptized] having anathematized their error may be received by the imposition of the hand into the one Church, the pillar as it is called, and the one mother of all Christians, where all these Sacraments are received unto salvation and everlasting life; even the same sacraments which obtain for those persevering in heresy the heavy penalty of damnation. So that which to those who are in the truth lighteneth to the obtaining of eternal life, the same to them who are in error tends but to darkness and damnation. With regard then to those who, having fled from error, acknowledge the breasts of their mother the Catholic Church, who believe and receive all these holy mysteries with the love of the truth, and besides the Sacraments have the testimony of a good life, there is no one who would not grant that without doubt such persons may be raised to the clerical office, especially in such necessity as the present. But there are others of this sect, who being already clergymen, desire to pass to us with their peoples and also with their honours, such as for the sake of office are converts to life, and that they may retain them seek for salvation [i.e., enter the Church]. I think that the question concerning such may be left to the graver consideration of our aforesaid brothers, and that when they have considered by their more prudent counsel the matter referred to them, they may vouchsafe to advise us what approves itself to them with regard to this question. Only concerning those who as children were baptized by heretics we decree that they consent, if it seems good, to our decision concerning the ordination of the same. All things, therefore, which we have set forth above with the holy bishops, let your honourable fraternity with me adjudge to be done.","QUONIAM in praeterito concilio decretum est, sicut vestra unanimitas meminit et ego, ut hi qui ab infantia a Donatistis baptizati sunt, et nondum valentes nosse perniciosam qualitatem erroris sui, et postea cum ad rationis usum pervenerunt, veritatis notitiam acceperunt, pristinum errorem abhorruerunt, et (secundum antiquum ordinem) per manus impositionem recepti sunt in Catholicam Dei Ecclesiam per orbem diffusam, ut talibus commemoratio erroris non sit impedimento ad suscipiendum clericale officium. In fide enim venientes putaverunt veram Ecclesiam esse suam et ibi crediderunt in Christum, et sacramenta Trinitatis acceperunt. Et quod haec omnia sacramenta vere sancta et divina sunt certissimum est, et in eis tota spes animae collocatur, licet praesumptuosa audacia haereticorum, nomen veritatis sibi assumens, ea ministrare audeat. Unum sunt tamen, sicut beatus Apostolus dicit: \"Unus Deus, una fides, unum baptisma,\" et non licet iterare quod semel tantum ministrari debet. [Qui ergo sic baptizati sunt] anathematizato errore suo recipi possunt per manus impositionem in unam Ecclesiam, columna quae dicitur, et unam matrem omnium Christianorum, ubi omnia haec Sacramenta ad salutem et vitam aeternam suscipiuntur; eadem sacramenta quae perseverantibus in haeresi gravem damnationis poenam acquirent. Itaque illud quod in veritate positos illuminat ad consequendam vitam aeternam, hoc ipsum eos qui in errore sunt obscurat et damnat. De his igitur qui, ab errore fugientes, matris suae Catholicae Ecclesiae ubera agnoscunt, qui credunt et suscipiunt omnia haec sancta mysteria cum amore veritatis, et praeter Sacramenta testimonium bonae vitae habent, nullus est qui non concedat quin tales sine dubio ad clericale officium possint promoveri, praesertim in tanta necessitate qualis praesens est. Sunt autem alii de hac secta, qui cum iam clerici sint, transire ad nos volunt cum populis suis et etiam cum honoribus suis, qui propter officium ad vitam convertuntur, et ut eos retineant salutem quaerunt [i.e., Ecclesiam intrant]. Puto quaestionem de talibus graviori considerationi praedictorum fratrum nostrorum relinquendam esse, et cum ipsi consilio prudentiore causam ad se relatam consideraverint, dignentur nos admonere quid sibi in hac quaestione placeat. Solummodo de his qui ab infantia ab haereticis baptizati sunt decernimus ut consentiant, si videtur, sententiae nostrae de ordinatione eorundem. Omnia igitur quae superius cum sanctis episcopis proposuimus, vestra honorabilis fraternitas mecum facienda iudicet."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p280","Ancient Epitome of Canon LVII.","Such as have been while children baptized by the Donatists may be ordained should they repent, anathematize their heresy, and be otherwise worthy.","Tales qui ab infantia a Donatistis baptizati sunt, si paenituerint, haeresim suam anathematizaverint, et alias digni fuerint, ordinari possunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p281","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LVII.","Of the three Introductions to Carthaginian Councils which precede this canon, the first refers to the synod held June 26, A.D. 397; the second to that held April 27, A.D. 399; and the third to that of June 15 (or 16), A.D. 401. The canon is Canon j. of the Synod of Carthage of June 15 (or 16), A.D. 401.","De tribus Introductionibus ad Concilia Carthaginensia quae hunc canonem praecedunt, prima refertur ad synodum habitam VI. Kalendas Iulias, anno 397; secunda ad eam habitam V. Kalendas Maias, anno 399; et tertia ad eam habita Idibus Iuniis (vel XVI. Kalendas Iulias), anno 401. Canon est Canon I. Synodi Carthaginensis, Idibus Iuniis (vel XVI. Kalendas Iulias), anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p282","CANON LVIII. (Greek lxii.)","Of the remaining idols or temples which should be done away by the Emperors.","De reliquiis idolorum vel templorum quae ab Imperatoribus tolli debent."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p283","CANON LVIII. (Greek lxii.)","WHEREFORE the most religious Emperors should be asked that they order the remaining idols to be taken entirely away throughout all Africa; for in many maritime places and in divers possessions the iniquity of this error still flourishes: that they command them to be taken away and their temples, (such as are no ornament, being set up in fields or out of the way places) be ordered to be altogether destroyed.","Quapropter religiosissimi Imperatores rogandi sunt ut reliqua idola per totam Africam penitus tolli iubeant; in multis enim maritimis locis et in diversis possessionibus adhuc huius erroris iniquitas virescit: ut ea tolli et templa eorum, (quae non sunt ornamenta, in agris vel in remotis locis posita) penitus destrui iubeant."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p284","Ancient Epitome of Canon LVIII.","The remains of the idols should be abolished altogether.","Reliquiae idolorum penitus aboleantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p285","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LVIII.","This is Canon ij. of the Synod of Carthage of June 15 (16), A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon II. Synodi Carthaginensis, Idibus Iuniis (XVI. Kalendas Iulias), anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p286","CANON LIX. (Greek lxiii.)","That clerics be not compelled to give testimony in public concerning the cognizance of their own judgment.","Ut clerici non compellantur publice testimonium dicere de cognitione proprii iudicii."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p287","CANON LIX. (Greek lxiii.)","IT should be petitioned also that they deign to decree, that if perchance any shall have been willing to plead their cause in any church according to the Apostolic law imposed upon the Churches, and it happens that the decision of the clergy does not satisfy one of the parties, it be not lawful to summon that clergyman who had been cognitor or present, into judgment as a witness, and that no person attached to any ecclesiastic be compelled to give testimony.","Petendum est etiam ut dignentur decernere, ut si forte aliqui voluerint causam suam in aliqua ecclesia secundum Apostolicam legem ecclesiis impositam agere, et contigerit ut clericorum iudicium uni parti non satisfaciat, non liceat illum clericum qui cognitor vel praesens fuerat, in iudicium vocare quasi testem, et ut nulla persona cuiquam ecclesiastico adhaerens compellatur testimonium dicere."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p288","Ancient Epitome of Canon LIX.","A cleric who has decided a case shall not, if it be displeasing, be summoned to a tribunal to give evidence concerning it; and no ecclesiastical person shall be forced to give testimony.","Clericus qui causam iudicavit non, si displicuerit, ad tribunal vocetur ut testimonium de ea dicat; et nulla persona ecclesiastica cogatur testimonium dicere."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p289","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LIX.","This is Canon iij. of the Synod of Carthage, June 15 (or 16). A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon III. Synodi Carthaginensis, Idibus Iuniis (XVI. Kalendas Iulias), anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p290","CANON LX. (Greek lxiii.)","Of heathen feasts.","De ethnicorum conviviis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p291","CANON LX. (Greek lxiii.)","THIS also must be sought, that (since contrary to the divine precepts feasts are held in many places, which have been induced by the heathen error, so that now Christians are forced to celebrate these by heathens, from which state of things it happens that in the times of the Christian Emperors a new persecution seems to have secretly arisen:) they order such things to be forbidden and prohibit them from cities and possessions under pain of punishment; especially should this be done since they do not fear to commit such iniquities in some cities even upon the natal days of most blessed martyrs, and in the very sacred places themselves. For upon these days, shame to say, they perform the most wicked leapings throughout the fields and open places, so that matronal honour and the modesty of innumerable women who have come out of devotion for the most holy day are assaulted by lascivious injuries, so that all approach to holy religion itself is almost fled from.","Hoc quoque petendum est, ut (cum contra divina praecepta convivia in multis locis habeantur, quae gentili errore inducta sunt, ita ut nunc Christiani ab ethnicis haec celebrare cogantur, ex quo fit ut temporibus Christianorum Imperatorum nova persecutio latenter orta videatur:) iubeant haec prohiberi et sub poenae interminatione ab urbibus et possessionibus ea arcere; praesertim cum in quibusdam civitatibus etiam in natalitiis beatissimorum martyrum et in ipsis sacratissimis locis talia facinora committere non vereantur. His enim diebus, pudet dicere, per agros et plateas nefandissimas saltationes exercent, ut matronalis honor et innumerabilium feminarum quae devotione ad sanctissimum diem convenerunt modestia lascivis iniuriis infestetur, ita ut omnis accessus ad ipsam sanctam religionem paene fugiatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p292","Ancient Epitome of Canon LX.","The Greek feasts must cease to be kept, because of their impropriety, and because they seduce many Christians, moreover they are celebrated on the commemorations of the martyrs.","Convivio gentilium cessare debent propter turpitudinem, et quia multos Christianos seducunt; praeterea in commemorationibus martyrum celebrantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p293","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LX.","This is Canon iv. of the Synod of Carthage, Aug. 15 (or 16), A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon IV. Synodi Carthaginensis, Idibus Augustis (XVII. Kalendas Septembres), anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p294","CANON LXI. (Greek lxiv.)","Of spectacles, that they be not celebrated on Lord's days nor on the festivals of the Saints.","De spectaculis, ut non celebrentur diebus Dominicis nec in festivitatibus Sanctorum."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p295","CANON LXI. (Greek lxiv.)","FURTHERMORE, it must be sought that theatrical spectacles and the exhibition of other plays be removed from the Lord's day and the other most sacred days of the Christian religion, especially because on the octave day of the holy Easter [i.e., Low Sunday] the people assemble rather at the circus than at church, and they should be transferred to some other day when they happen to fall upon a day of devotion, nor shall any Christian be compelled to witness these spectacles, especially because in the performance of things contrary to the precepts of God there should be no persecution made by anyone, but (as is right) a man should exercise the free will given him by God. Especially also should be considered the peril of the cooperators who, contrary to the precepts of God, are forced by great fear to attend the shews.","Petendum etiam est ut spectacula theatralia et aliorum ludorum exhibitio die Dominica et ceteris sacratissimis diebus religionis Christianae removeantur, praesertim quia in octava sancti Paschae [id est, Dominica in Albis] magis ad circum quam ad ecclesiam populus convenit, et ad alium diem transferantur quando in diem devotionis inciderint, nec quisquam Christianus ad haec spectacula cogatur, maxime quia in exhibitione eorum quae contra Dei praecepta sunt nulla persecutio ab aliquo fieri debet, sed (sicut decet) homo libero arbitrio a Deo sibi dato utatur. Maxime etiam considerandum est periculum cooperatorum qui, contra Dei praecepta, magno timore coguntur spectaculis interesse."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p296","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXI.","There shall be no theatrical representations upon Lord's days or feast days.","Nulla spectacula theatralia fiant diebus Dominicis vel festis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p297","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXI.","This is Canon V. of the Synod of Carthage, June 15th (16), A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon V. Synodi Carthaginensis, Idibus Iuniis (XVI. Kalendas Iulias), anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p298","CANON LXII. (Greek lxv.)","Of condemned clerics.","De clericis damnatis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p299","CANON LXII. (Greek lxv.)","AND this should be sought, that they deign to decree that if any clergyman of whatever rank shall have been condemned by the judgment of the bishops for any crime, he may not be defended either by the churches over which he presided, nor by anyone whatever, under pain of loss both of money and office, and let them order that neither age nor sex be received as an excuse.","Et hoc petendum est, ut dignentur decernere ut si quis clericus cuiuscumque gradus fuerit iudicio episcoporum pro aliquo crimine damnatus fuerit, nec ab ecclesiis quibus praefuit nec ab aliquo defendatur, sub periculo amittendi et pecuniam et officium, et iubeant ut nec aetas nec sexus excusationem recipiatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p300","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXII.","No one shall justify a clergyman condemned by his own bishop.","Nullus iustificet clericum a proprio episcopo condemnatum."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p301","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXII.","This is Canon vj. of the Synod of Carthage, June 15 (or 16), A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon VI. Synodi Carthaginensis, Idibus Iuniis (XVI. Kalendas Iulias), anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p302","CANON LXIII. (Greek lxvi.)","Of players who have become Christians.","De histrionibus qui Christiani facti sunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p303","CANON LXIII. (Greek lxvi.)","AND of them also it must be sought that if anyone wishes to come to the grace of Christianity from any ludicrous art and to remain free of that stain, it be not lawful for anyone to induce him or compel him to return to the performance of the same things again.","Et de his quoque petendum est ut si quis a qualibet ludicra arte ad Christianae gratiam venire voluerit et ab illa macula liber permanere, non liceat cuiquam eum inducere vel compellere ut ad eorundem operum exhibitionem redeat iterum."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p304","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXIII.","Whoever has turned away from the stage to adopt an honest life, shall not be led back thereto.","Quicumque a theatro ad honestam vitam convertitur, non reducatur illuc."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p305","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXIII.","This is Canon vij. of the Synod of Carthage, June 15 (or 16), A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon VII. Synodi Carthaginensis, Idibus Iuniis (XVI. Kalendas Iulias), anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p306","CANON LXIV. (Greek lxvii.)","Of celebrating manumissions in church, that permission be asked from the Emperor.","De celebrandis manumissionibus in ecclesia, ut ab Imperatore permissio petatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p307","CANON LXIV. (Greek lxvii.)","CONCERNING the publishing of manumissions in church, if our fellow bishops throughout Italy shall be found to do this, it will be a mark of our confidence to follow their order [of proceedings], full power being given to the legate we send, that whatever he can accomplish worthy of the faith, for the state of the Church and the salvation of souls, we shall laudably accept in the sight of the Lord. All which things, if they please your sanctity, pray set forth, that I may be assured that my suggestion has been ratified by you and that their sincerity may freely accept our unanimous action. And all the bishops said: The things which have been enjoined to be done and have been wisely set forth by your holiness are pleasing to all.","De publicandis manumissionibus in ecclesia, si confratres nostri per totam Italiam hoc facere inventi fuerint, signum erit fiduciae nostrae ut ordinem eorum sequamur, data plena potestate legato quem mittimus, ut quidquid fide dignum pro statu Ecclesiae et salute animarum efficere potuerit, laudabiliter in conspectu Domini accipiamus. Quae omnia, si sanctitati vestrae placent, rogo ut proponatis, ut certus sim suggestionem meam a vobis ratificatam esse et sinceritas eorum unanimem actionem nostram libere acceptare possit. Et omnes episcopi dixerunt: Quae a sanctitate vestra sapienter proposita et agenda iniuncta sunt omnibus placent."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p308","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXIV.","The Emperor's permission should be sought to allow the public manumission of slaves in church.","Imperatoris permissio petenda est ut publica manumissio servorum in ecclesia fiat."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p309","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXIV.","This is Canon viij. of the Synod of Carthage, June 15 (or 16), A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon VIII. Synodi Carthaginensis, Idibus Iuniis (XVI. Kalendas Iulias), anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p310","CANON LXV. (Greek lxviii.)","Concerning the condemned bishop Equitius.","De damnato episcopo Equitio."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p311","CANON LXV. (Greek lxviii.)","AURELIUS, the bishop, said: I do not think that the case of Equitius should be passed over in the legation, who some time ago for his crimes was condemned by an Episcopal sentence; that if by any chance our legate should meet him in those parts, our brother should take care for the state of the Church, as opportunity offered or where he could, to act against him. And all the bishops said: This prosecution is exceedingly agreeable to us, especially as Equitius was condemned some time ago, his impudent unrest ought to be repelled everywhere more and more for the good estate and health of the Church. And they subscribed, I, Aurelius, the bishop of the Church of Carthage, have consented to this decree, and after having read it have signed my name. Likewise also signed all the other bishops.","AURELIUS episcopus dixit: Non puto causam Equitii praetereundam esse in legatione, qui olim propter sua crimina episcopali sententia damnatus est; ut si forte legatus noster in illis partibus ei occurrerit, frater noster pro statu Ecclesiae curet, sicut opportunitas se dederit vel ubi potuerit, contra eum agere. Et omnes episcopi dixerunt: Ista prosecutio valde nobis placet, praesertim cum Equitius olim damnatus sit, impudens eius inquietudo magis magisque repellenda est pro bono statu et sanitate Ecclesiae. Et subscriptionibus confirmarunt: Ego, Aurelius, episcopus Ecclesiae Carthaginensis, huic decreto consensi, et postquam legi, nomen meum subscripsi. Similiter et omnes alii episcopi subscripserunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p312","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXV.","Equitius, who had been condemned by the judgment of the bishops, and had behaved impudently against the ecclesiastical authority, ought to be opposed.","Equitius, qui iudicio episcoporum damnatus fuerat, et impudenter contra auctoritatem ecclesiasticam se gesserat, opponi debet."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p313","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXV.","This is Canon ix. of the Synod of Carthage, June 15 (or 16), A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon IX. Synodi Carthaginensis, Idibus Iuniis (XVI. Kalendas Iulias), anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p314","CANON LXVI. (Greek lxix.)","That the Donatists are to be treated leniently.","Ut Donatistae leniter tractentur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p315","CANON LXVI. (Greek lxix.)","THEN when all things had been considered and treated of which seem to conduce to the advantage of the church, the Spirit of God suggesting and admonishing us, we determined to act leniently and pacifically with the before-mentioned men, although they were cut off from the unity of the Lord's body by an unruly dissent, so that (as much as in us lies) to all those who have been caught in the net of their communion and society, it might be known throughout all the provinces of Africa, how they have been overcome by miserable error, holding different opinions, \"that perchance,\" as the Apostle says, when we have corrected them with gentleness, \"God should grant them repentance for the acknowledging of the truth, and that they might be snatched out of the snares of the devil, who are led captive of him at his will.\"","Tunc, cum omnia considerata et tractata fuissent quae ad utilitatem ecclesiae conducere videbantur, Spiritu Dei suggerente et admonente, decrevimus leniter et pacifice agere cum praedictis hominibus, quamvis a unitate dominici corporis discissione praecipiti praecisi essent, ut (quantum in nobis est) omnibus illis qui in retia communionis et societatis eorum irretiti sunt, per omnes Africae provincias innotescat quomodo misero errore superati sint, diversa sentientes, \"ne forte,\" sicut Apostolus dicit, cum eos leniter correxerimus, \"det illis Deus paenitentiam ad cognoscendam veritatem, et resipiscant a diaboli laqueis, a quo captivi tenentur ad ipsius voluntatem.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p316","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXVI.","It seemed good that the Donatists should be treated kindly and with leniency, even if they should separate themselves from the Church, so that perchance through their respect for our great gentleness they may be loosed from their captivity.","Placuit ut Donatistae benigne et leniter tractentur, etiamsi se ab Ecclesia separaverint, ut forte per reverentiam magnae mansuetudinis nostrae a captivitate sua liberentur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p317","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXVI.","The introduction refers to the Synod of Carthage of September 13, 401, and this canon is part of Canon j. of that Synod.","Introductio refertur ad Synodum Carthaginensem Idibus Septembribus, anno 401, et hic canon est pars Canonis I. illius Synodi."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p318","CANON LXVII. (Greek lxx.)","Of the letters to be sent to the judges, that they may take note of the things done between the Donatists and the Maximianists.","De epistolis ad iudices mittendis, ut ea quae inter Donatistas et Maximianistas gesta sunt animadvertant."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p319","CANON LXVII. (Greek lxx.)","THEREFORE it seemed good that letters should be given from our council to the African judges, from whom it would seem suitable that this should be sought, that in this matter they would aid the common mother, the Catholic Church, that the episcopal authority may be fortified in the cities; that is to say that by their judicial power and with diligence out of their Christian faith, they enquire and record in the public acts, that all may have a firm notion of it, what has taken place in all those places in which the Maximianists, who made a schism from them, have obtained basilicas.","Ideo placuit ut epistolae darentur ex nostro concilio ad Africanos iudices, a quibus petendum hoc dignum videretur, ut in hac re adiuvarent communem matrem Catholicam Ecclesiam, ut auctoritas episcopalis muniatur in civitatibus; id est, ut sua potestate iudiciaria et diligentia ex Christiana fide sua inquirant et publicis actis conscribant, ut omnes firmam notionem eius rei habeant, quid actum sit in omnibus illis locis in quibus Maximianistae, qui schisma ab eis fecerunt, basilicas obtinuerunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p320","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXVII.","The secular arm must be implored by synodal letters to assist our common Mother the Catholic Church against those by whom the authority of the bishop is despised.","Brachium saeculare per epistolas synodicas implorandum est ut adiuvet communem Matrem Catholicam Ecclesiam contra illos a quibus auctoritas episcopi contemnitur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p321","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXVII.","This canon is the other half of Canon j. of the Synod of Carthage, September 13, A.D. 401.","Hic canon est altera pars Canonis I. Synodi Carthaginensis, Idibus Septembribus, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p322","CANON LXVIII. (Greek lxxi.)","That the Donatist clergy are to be received into the Catholic Church as clergymen.","Ut Donatistarum clerici in Ecclesiam Catholicam sicut clerici recipiantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p323","CANON LXVIII. (Greek lxxi.)","IT moreover seemed good that letters be sent to our brethren and fellow-bishops, and especially to the Apostolic See, over which our aforesaid venerable brother and colleague Anastasius presides, that he may know that Africa is in great need, for the peace and prosperity of the Church, that those Donatists who were clergymen and who by good advice had desired to return to Catholic unity, should be treated according to the will and judgment of each Catholic bishop who governs the Church in that place; and, if it seem good for Christian peace, be received with their honours, as it is clear was done in the former times of this same division. And that this was the case the example of the majority, yea, of nearly all the African Churches in which this error had sprung up, testify; not that the Council which met about this matter in foreign parts should be done away, but that it may remain in force with regard to those who so will to come over to the Catholic Church that there be procured by them no breaking of unity. But those through whom Catholic unity was seen to have been altogether perfected or assisted by the manifest winning of the souls of their brethren in the places where they live, there shall not be objected to them the decree contrary to their honour adopted by a foreign council, for salvation is shut off to no one, that is to say, that those ordained by the Donatist party, if having been corrected they have been willing to return to the Catholic Church, are not to be received in their grades, according to the foreign council; but they are to be excepted through whom they received the advice to return to Catholic unity.","Item placuit ut epistolae mittantur ad fratres et coepiscopos nostros, et praecipue ad Sedem Apostolicam, cui praefatus venerabilis frater et collega noster Anastasius praesidet, ut sciat Africam in magna necessitate esse, propter pacem et prosperitatem Ecclesiae, ut illi Donatistae qui clerici fuerunt et qui bono consilio ad Catholicam unitatem redire desideraverunt, secundum voluntatem et iudicium uniuscuiusque episcopi Catholici qui in illo loco Ecclesiam regit tractentur; et, si Christianae paci videtur expedire, cum honoribus suis recipiantur, sicut prioribus temporibus eiusdem divisionis factum esse constat. Et quod ita se res haberet, maiorum, immo omnium fere Africarum Ecclesiarum in quibus hic error exortus est, exempla testantur; non ut Concilium quod de hac re transmarinis partibus habitum est evacuetur, sed ut maneat in sua firmitate erga eos qui ita ad Ecclesiam Catholicam transire voluerint ut nulla per eos unitatis facta sit ruptio. Illi autem per quos unitas Catholica vel perfecta vel adiuta esse conspicitur, manifesta lucratione animarum fratrum suorum in locis ubi commorantur, non eis obicietur decretum ab exteris concilio contra honorem eorum constitutum, quia salus nulli neganda est, id est, ut qui a parte Donatistarum ordinati sunt, si correcti ad Ecclesiam Catholicam redire voluerint, non recipiantur in gradibus suis, secundum transmarinum concilium; sed excipiantur illi per quos ad Catholicam unitatem redeundi consilium acceperunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p324","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXVIII.","Those ordained by the Donatists, even though their reception has been forbidden by a foreign synod, since it is truly good that all should be saved, if they correct themselves, let them be received.","Hi qui a Donatistis ordinati sunt, etiamsi eorum receptio prohibita sit a transmarina synodo, quia vere bonum est ut omnes salvi fiant, si se correxerint, recipiantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p325","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXVIII.","This canon is Canon ij. of Carthage, Sept., A.D. 401.","Hic canon est Canon II. Carthaginensis, Septembri, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p326","CANON LXIX. (Greek lxxii.)","That a legation be sent to the Donatists for the sake of making peace.","Ut legatio ad Donatistas pro pace facienda mittatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p327","CANON LXIX. (Greek lxxii.)","IT further seemed good, that when these things were done, legates should be sent from our number to those of the Donatists whom they hold as bishops, or to the people, for the sake of preaching peace and unity, without which Christian salvation cannot be attained; and that these legates should direct the attention of all to the fact that they have no just objection to urge against the Catholic Church. And especially that this be made manifest to all by the municipal acts (on account of the weight of their documents) what they themselves had done in the case of the Maximianists, their own schismatics. For in this case it is shown them by divine grace, if they will but heed it, that their separation from the unity of the Church is as iniquitous as they now proclaim the schism of the Maximianists from themselves to be. Nevertheless from the number, those whom they condemned by the authority of their plenary council, they received back with their honours, and accepted the baptism which they had given while condemned and cut off. And thus let them see how with stupid heart they resist the peace of the Church scattered throughout the whole world, when they do these things on the part of Donatus, neither do they say that they are contaminated by communion with those whom they so receive for the making of peace, and yet they despise us, that is the Catholic Church, which is established even in the extreme parts of the earth, as being defiled by the communion of those whom the accusers have not been able to win over to themselves.","Item placuit ut, his gestis, legati de nostro numero mittantur ad eos Donatistarum quos episcopos habent, vel ad plebes, propter praedicandam pacem et unitatem, sine qua salus Christiana haberi non potest; et ut hi legati omnium animadvertant animum ad id quod non habent iustam obiectionem contra Ecclesiam Catholicam. Et maxime ut hoc omnibus manifestetur per acta municipalia (propter documentorum pondus) quid ipsi fecerint in causa Maximianistarum, suorum schismaticorum. In hac enim causa ostenditur eis divina gratia, si velint attendere, quod separatio eorum ab unitate Ecclesiae tam iniqua est quam nunc ipsi schisma Maximianistarum a se esse praedicant. Tamen de numero eos quos auctoritate plenarii concilii sui damnaverunt, cum honoribus suis receperunt, et baptismum quem damnati et abscisi dederant acceperunt. Et sic videant quam stulto corde resistant paci Ecclesiae per totum orbem diffusae, cum haec faciant pro parte Donati, nec dicunt se contaminari communione eorum quos sic ad pacem faciendam recipiunt, et tamen nos contemnunt, id est Catholicam Ecclesiam, quae usque in extremas terrae partes constituta est, quasi contaminatam communione eorum quos accusatores ad se transducere non potuerunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p328","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXIX.","It seemed good that legates be sent to preach peace and unity to the Donatists who had been converted to the orthodox faith.","Placuit ut legati mittantur ad praedicandam pacem et unitatem Donatistis qui ad orthodoxam fidem conversi sunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p329","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXIX.","This canon is Canon iij. of Carthage, September, A.D. 401.","Hic canon est Canon III. Carthaginensis, Septembri, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p330","CANON LXX. (Greek lxxiii.)","What clerics should abstain from their wives.","Qui clerici abstinere debeant ab uxoribus suis."],["vol3/african-code/part-1/index.html","p331","CANON LXX. (Greek lxxiii.)","MOREOVER since incontinence has been charged against some clergymen with regard to their own wives it has seemed good that bishops, presbyters, and deacons should according to the statutes already made abstain even from their own wives; and unless they do so that they should be removed from the clerical office. But the rest of the clergy shall not be forced to this but the custom of each church in this matter shall be followed.","Porro cum incontinentia quibusdam clericis erga proprias uxores obiecta sit, placuit ut episcopi, presbyteri et diacones secundum iam statuta etiam ab uxoribus suis abstineant; et nisi hoc fecerint, ab officio clericatus removeantur. Ceteri autem clerici ad hoc non cogantur, sed consuetudo uniuscuiusque ecclesiae in hac re servetur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p332","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXX.","Bishops, presbyters and deacons shall abstain from their wives or else be removed from the ecclesiastical order. But the rest of the clergy shall not be forced to the same: but let the custom be observed.","Episcopi, presbyteri et diacones abstineant ab uxoribus suis aut ab ordine ecclesiastico removeantur. Ceteri autem clerici ad idem non cogantur: sed consuetudo servetur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p333","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXX.","This is Canon iv. of Carthage, September, A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon IV. Carthaginensis, Septembri, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p334","CANON LXXI. (Greek lxxiv.)","Of those who leave in neglect their own people.","De his qui neglectui habent populum suum."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p335","CANON LXXI. (Greek lxxiv.)","MOREOVER it seemed good that no one should be allowed to leave his chief cathedral and go to another church built in the diocese, or to neglect the care and frequent attendance upon his own cathedral by reason of too great care for his own affairs.","Item placuit ut nulli liceat principalem cathedram suam deserere et ad aliam ecclesiam in dioecesi aedificatam transire, aut curam et frequentem visitationem propriae cathedrae nimia suarum rerum cura neglegere."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p336","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXXI.","It seemed good that no bishop shall translate himself to another see, leaving his own, nor that through a care for his own affairs he should neglect his diocese.","Placuit ut nullus episcopus ad aliam sedem se transferat, propria relicta, nec ut per curam suarum rerum dioecesim suam negligat."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p337","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXI.","This is Canon vj. of Carthage, September, A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon VI. Carthaginensis, Septembri, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p338","CANON LXXII. (Greek lxxv.)","Of the baptism of infants when there is some doubt of their being already baptized.","De baptismo infantum cum aliqua dubitatio est eos iam baptizatos esse."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p339","CANON LXXII. (Greek lxxv.)","ITEM, it seemed good that whenever there were not found reliable witnesses who could testify that without any doubt they were baptized and when the children themselves were not, on account of their tender age, able to answer concerning the giving of the sacraments to them, all such children should be baptized without scruple, lest a hesitation should deprive them of the cleansing of the sacraments. This was urged by the Moorish Legates, our brethren, since they redeem many such from the barbarians.","Item placuit ut quotienscumque non inveniuntur idonei testes qui testificari possint sine ulla dubitatione eos baptizatos esse, et ipsi infantes propter aetatem teneram non possint respondere de sacramentorum sibi datione, omnes tales infantes sine scrupulo baptizentur, ne haesitatio eos ablutione sacramentorum privet. Hoc a Mauritanis Legatis, fratribus nostris, suggestum est, cum multos tales de barbaris redimant."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p340","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXXII.","It seemed good that they should be baptized about whom there was an ambiguity whether they had been baptized or no; lest they might through that doubt lose the divine ablution.","Placuit ut baptizentur illi de quibus ambigebatur utrum baptizati fuissent necne; ne per illam dubitationem divinam ablutionem amitterent."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p341","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXII.","This is Canon vij. of Carthage, September, A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon VII. Carthaginensis, Septembri, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p342","CANON LXXIII. (Greek lxxvi.)","The date of Easter and the date of the Council should be announced.","Dies Paschae et dies Concilii annuntiari debent."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p343","CANON LXXIII. (Greek lxxvi.)","ITEM, it seemed good that the day of the venerable Easter should be intimated to all by the subscription of formed letters; and that the same should be observed with regard to the date of the Council, according to the decree of the Council of Hippo, that is to say the X. Calends of September, and that it should be written to the primates of each province so that when they summon their councils they do not impede this day.","Item placuit ut dies venerabilis Paschae per subscriptionem formatarum litterarum omnibus intimetur; et ut idem servetur de die Concilii, secundum decretum Concilii Hipponensis, id est, X. Kalendas Septembres, et ut scribatur ad primates uniuscuiusque provinciae ut cum sua concilia convocant, hunc diem non impediant."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p344","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXXIII.","It seemed good that the day of the Holy Easter should be announced on the day of the annual Synod, or on the tenth day before the calends of September.","Placuit ut dies Sancti Paschae in die annuae Synodi annuntietur, vel X. Kalendas Septembres."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p345","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXIII.","This is Canon viij. of Carthage, September, A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon VIII. Carthaginensis, Septembri, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p346","CANON LXXIV. (Greek lxxvii.)","That no bishop who is an intercessor is to hold the see where he is intercessor.","Ut nullus episcopus qui intercessor est teneat sedem ubi intercessor est."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p347","CANON LXXIV. (Greek lxxvii.)","ITEM, it has been decreed that it is not lawful to any intercessor to retain the see to which he has been appointed as intercessor, by any popular movements and seditions; but let him take care that within a year he provide them with a bishop: but if he shall neglect to do so, when the year is done, another intercessor shall be appointed.","Item decretum est non licere ulli intercessori per motus et seditiones popularium retinere sedem ad quam intercessor datus est; sed curet ut intra annum eis episcopum provideat: quod si neglexerit, anno expleto, alius intercessor constituatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p348","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXXIV.","It seemed good that the bishop who had been called in as an intercessor, by the zeal and dissensions of the people, should not be allowed to become the occupant of its throne: but let a bishop be provided within a year, or else in the next year let another intercessor be appointed.","Placuit ut episcopus qui intercessor vocatus fuerit studio et dissensionibus populi, non permittatur fieri possessor eiusdem cathedrae: sed provideatur episcopus intra annum, aut alioquin sequenti anno alius intercessor constituatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p349","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXIV.","This is Canon IX. of Carthage, September, A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon IX. Carthaginensis, Septembri, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p350","CANON LXXV. (Greek lxxviii.)","Of asking from the Emperors defenders of the Churches.","De petendis ab Imperatoribus defensoribus Ecclesiarum."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p351","CANON LXXV. (Greek lxxviii.)","ON account of the afflictions of the poor by whose troubles the Church is worn out without any intermission, it seemed good to all that the Emperors be asked to allow defenders for them against the power of the rich to be chosen under the supervision of the bishops.","Propter afflictiones pauperum quorum molestiis Ecclesia sine ulla intermissione fatigatur, placuit omnibus ut Imperatores rogentur ut defensores eis contra potentiam divitum sub episcoporum examinatione eligi permittant."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p352","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXXV.","That the bishop be not annoyed, let Defensors be appointed.","Ut episcopus non vexetur, Defensores constituantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p353","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXV.","This is Canon X. of Carthage, September, 401.","Hic est Canon X. Carthaginensis, Septembri, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p354","CANON LXXVI. (Greek lxxix.)","Of bishops who do not put in an appearance at Council.","De episcopis qui in Concilium non occurrunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p355","CANON LXXVI. (Greek lxxix.)","ITEM, it seemed good that as often as the council is to be assembled, the bishops who are impeded neither by age, sickness, or other grave necessity, come together, and that notice be given to the primates of their several provinces, that from all the bishops there be made two or three squads, and of each of these squads there be elected some who shall be promptly ready on the council day: but should they not be able to attend, let them write their excuses in the tractory, or if after the coming of the tractory certain necessities suddenly arise by chance, unless they send to their own primate an account of their impediment, they ought to be content with the communion of their own Church.","Item placuit ut quoties concilium congregandum est, episcopi qui nec aetate nec languore nec aliqua gravi necessitate impediuntur, conveniant, et primatibus singularum provinciarum intimetur ut de omnibus episcopis duae vel tres turmae fiant, et de singulis turmis aliqui eligantur qui in die concilii prompte parati sint: quod si occurrere non potuerint, excusationes suas in tractoria scribant; aut si post tractoriae adventum aliquae necessitates ex improviso evenerint, nisi ad proprium primatem de impedimento suo miserint, contenti esse debebunt communione suae Ecclesiae."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p356","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXXVI.","Those who do not attend the annual synod, unless they be involuntarily prevented, must be satisfied with the communion of their own churches.","Qui annuam synodum non frequentant, nisi inviti impediantur, contenti esse debebunt communione suarum ecclesiarum."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p357","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXVI.","This is Canon xj., of Carthage, September, 401.","Hic est Canon XI. Carthaginensis, Septembri, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p358","CANON LXXVII. (Greek lxxx.)","Of Cresconius.","De Cresconio."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p359","CANON LXXVII. (Greek lxxx.)","CONCERNING Cresconius of Villa Regis this seemed good to all, that the Primate of Numidia should be informed on this matter so that he should by his letters summon the aforementioned Cresconius in order that at the next plenary Council of Africa he should not put off making an appearance. But if he contemns the summons and does not come, let him recognize the fact that sentence should be pronounced against him.","De Cresconio Villae Regis hoc placuit omnibus, ut Primas Numidiae de hac re informetur ut per epistolas suas praefatum Cresconium convocet ut in proximo plenario Concilio Africae non differat occurrere. Quod si contempserit et non venerit, sciat sententiam in se proferendam."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p360","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXXVII.","Unless Cresconius who has been summoned by letter to the Synod, shall appear, let him know that he will have sentence given against him.","Nisi Cresconius, qui per epistolam ad Synodum vocatus est, occurrerit, sciat sententiam in se proferendam."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p361","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXVII.","This canon was probably formerly an appendix (so Hefele thinks) to Canon xj., of the Synod of Carthage of September 13, 401.","Hic canon fuit probabiliter olim appendix (sic Hefele censet) ad Canonem XI. Synodi Carthaginensis, Idibus Septembribus, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p362","CANON LXXVIII. (Greek lxxxi.)","Of the Church of Hippo-Diarrhytus.","De Ecclesia Hippo-Diarrhyta."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p363","CANON LXXVIII. (Greek lxxxi.)","IT further seemed good that since the destitution of the Church of Hippo-Diarrhytus should no longer be neglected, and the churches there are retained by those who have declined the infamous communion of Equitius, that certain bishops be sent from the present council, viz.: Reginus, Alypius, Augustine, Maternus, Theasius, Evodius, Placian, Urban, Valerius, Ambivius, Fortunatus, Quodvultdeus, Honoratus, Januarius, Aptus, Honoratus, Ampelius, Victorian, Evangelus and Rogation; and when those had been gathered together, and those had been corrected who with culpable pertinacity were of opinion that this flight of the same Equitius should be waited for, let a bishop be ordained for them by the vote of all. But if these should not be willing to consider peace, let them not prevent the choosing for ordination of a bishop, for the advantage of the church which has been so long destitute.","Item placuit quia destitutio Ecclesiae Hippo-Diarrhytae iam diutius negligi non debet, et ecclesiae ibi retinentur ab his qui infamem communionem Equitii declinaverunt, ut quidam episcopi de praesenti concilio mittantur, videlicet: Reginus, Alypius, Augustinus, Maternus, Theasius, Evodius, Placianus, Urbanus, Valerius, Ambivius, Fortunatus, Quodvultdeus, Honoratus, Ianuarius, Aptus, Honoratus, Ampelius, Victorianus, Evangelus et Rogatianus; et cum illi congregati fuerint, et illi correcti fuerint qui culpabili pertinacia huius eiusdem Equitii fugam expectandam esse censebant, ordinetur eis episcopus omnium suffragio. Quod si hi pacem considerare noluerint, non impediant quominus episcopus ad utilitatem tamdiu destitutae ecclesiae ordinandus eligatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p364","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXXVIII.","It seemed good that, after Equitius had been condemned by the universal vote, a bishop of Hippo should be elected, and that they should in no way impede the ordination of a prelate for that church.","Placuit ut, postquam Equitius universali suffragio damnatus fuerit, episcopus Hipponensis eligatur, et ut nullo modo impediant ordinationem praelati pro illa ecclesia."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p365","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXVIII.","This canon was likewise probably an appendix, to Canon xiiij, of the Synod of Carthage of September 13th, 401, according to Hefele.","Hic canon similiter fuit probabiliter appendix ad Canonem XIV. Synodi Carthaginensis, Idibus Septembribus, anno 401, secundum Hefele."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p366","CANON LXXIX. (Greek lxxxii.)","Of clerics who do not take care to have their causes argued within a year.","De clericis qui non curant causas suas intra annum agere."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p367","CANON LXXIX. (Greek lxxxii.)","IT was further decreed that as often as clergymen convicted and confessed of any crime either on account of those whose shame is spared, or on account of the opprobrium to the Church, and of the insolent glorying of heretics and Gentiles, if perchance they are willing to be present at their cause and to assert their innocence, let them do so within one year of their excommunication; if in truth they neglect during a year to purge their cause, their voice shall not be heard afterwards.","Item decretum est ut quotienscumque clerici convicti et confessi de aliquo crimine vel propter eorum quorum verecundiae parcitur, vel propter opprobrium Ecclesiae, et propter insolentem gloriae haereticorum et gentilium, si forte voluerint causae suae adesse et innocentiam suam asserere, intra unum annum excommunicationis suae faciant; si vero intra annum purgare causam suam neglexerint, vox eorum postea non audiatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p368","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXXIX.","When a cleric has been convicted of a crime, if he says his cause should be heard upon appeal, let the appeal be made within a year; after that the appeal shall not be admitted.","Cum clericus de crimine convictus fuerit, si dixerit causam suam per appellationem audiri debere, intra annum appelletur; postea appellatio non admittatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p369","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXIX.","This is Canon xiv. of Carthage, September, A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon XIV. Carthaginensis, Septembri, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p370","CANON LXXX. (Greek lxxxiii.)","That it is not permitted to make superiors of monasteries nor to ordain as clerics those who are received from a monastery not one's own.","Ut non liceat facere superiores monasteriorum nec ordinare ut clericos eos qui de monasterio non proprio recipiuntur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p371","CANON LXXX. (Greek lxxxiii.)","ITEM, it seemed good that if any bishop wished to advance to the clericature a monk received from a monastery not under his jurisdiction, or shall have appointed him superior of a monastery of his own, the bishop who shall have thus acted shall be separated from the communion of others and shall rest content with the communion of his own people alone, but the monk shall continue neither as cleric nor superior.","Item placuit ut si quis episcopus monachum de monasterio non sub sua iurisdictione susceptum ad clericatum promovere voluerit, vel eum superiorem monasterii sui constituerit, episcopus qui sic egerit ab aliorum communione separetur et sola suorum communione contentus sit, monachus autem nec clericus nec superior permaneat."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p372","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXXX.","Whoever shall receive a monk from a monastery not subject to his jurisdiction, and if he shall ordain him to the clerical estate or shall appoint him prior of his monastery, such an one shall be cut off from communion.","Quicumque susceperit monachum de monasterio suae iurisdictioni non subiecto, et si eum ad clericatum ordinaverit vel priorem monasterii sui constituerit, talis a communione abscindatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p373","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXX.","This is Canon xv. of Carthage, September, A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon XV. Carthaginensis, Septembri, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p374","CANON LXXXI. (Greek lxxxiv.)","Of bishops who appoint heretics or heathens as their heirs.","De episcopis qui haereticos vel gentiles sibi heredes instituunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p375","CANON LXXXI. (Greek lxxxiv.)","ITEM, it was ordained that if any bishop should prefer to his Church strangers to blood relationship with him, or his heretical relatives, or pagans as his heirs, he shall be anathematized even after his death, and his name shall by no means be recited among those of the priests of God. Nor can he be excused if he die intestate, because being a bishop he was bound not to postpone making such a disposition of his goods as was befitting his profession.","Item constitutum est ut si quis episcopus ecclesiae suae praeferat extraneos a consanguinitate sua, vel haereticos propinquos, vel paganos, sibi heredes, anathematizetur etiam post mortem suam, et nomen eius nullo modo inter sacerdotum Dei nomina recitetur. Nec excusari potest si intestatus decesserit, quia episcopus constitutus non debebat differre talem dispositionem bonorum suorum qualem professionem suam decebat."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p376","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXXXI.","Let a bishop be anathema if he make heretics and heathen his heirs.","Anathema sit episcopus si haereticos et gentiles sibi heredes fecerit."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p377","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXXI.","This is Canon xvi. of Carthage, September, A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon XVI. Carthaginensis, Septembri, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p378","CANON LXXXII. (Greek lxxxv.)","Of manumissions.","De manumissionibus."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p379","CANON LXXXII. (Greek lxxxv.)","ITEM, it seemed good that the Emperor be petitioned with regard to announcing manumissions in church.","Item placuit ut Imperator petatur de publicandis manumissionibus in ecclesia."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p380","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXXXII.","The imperial permission must be asked for the making of the manumission of slaves in churches.","Imperatoria permissio petenda est pro facienda manumissione servorum in ecclesiis."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p381","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXXII.","This is Canon xvij. of Carthage, September, A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon XVII. Carthaginensis, Septembri, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p382","CANON LXXXIII. (Greek lxxxvi.)","Of false Memories of Martyrs.","De falsis Martyrum Memoriis."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p383","CANON LXXXIII. (Greek lxxxvi.)","ITEM, it seemed good that the altars which have been set up here and there, in fields and by the wayside as Memories of Martyrs, in which no body nor reliques of martyrs can be proved to have been laid up, should be overturned by the bishops who rule over such places, if such a thing can be done. But should this be impossible on account of the popular tumult it would arouse, the people should none the less be admonished not to frequent such places, and that those who believe rightly should be held bound by no superstition of the place. And no memory of martyrs should at all be accepted, unless where there is found the body or some reliques, on which is declared traditionally and by good authority to have been originally his habitation, or possession, or the scene of his passion. For altars which have been erected anywhere on account of dreams or inane quasi-revelations of certain people, should be in every way disapproved of.","Item placuit ut altaria quae passim in agris et per vias quasi Martyrum Memoriae constituta sunt, in quibus nullum corpus aut reliquiae martyrum conditae probari possunt, ab episcopis qui illis locis praesunt, si fieri potest, everterentur. Quod si hoc propter tumultum popularem fieri non potest, populus tamen admonendus est ne illa loca frequentet, et ut qui recte credunt nulla loci superstitione teneantur. Et nulla Martyrum memoria omnino accipiatur, nisi ubi corpus aut aliquae reliquiae inveniuntur, de quibus traditoria et bona auctoritate declaratur quod originaliter eius habitatio, vel possessio, vel passio fuerit. Altaria enim quae ubicumque per somnia vel inanes quasi revelationes quorundam hominum erecta sunt, omnimodis reprobentur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p384","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXXXIII.","An altar in the fields or in a vineyard which lacks the reliques of the martyrs should be thrown down unless it would cause a public tumult to do so: and the same is the case with such as have been set up on account of dreams and false revelations.","Altare in agris vel in vinea quod reliquias martyrum non habet, destruatur nisi publicus inde tumultus oriatur: et similiter de his quae per somnia et falsas revelationes erecta sunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p385","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXXIII.","This is Canon xviij. of Carthage, September, A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon XVIII. Carthaginensis, Septembri, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p386","CANON LXXXIV. (Greek lxxxvii.)","Of extirpating the remains of the idols.","De extirpandis reliquiis idolorum."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p387","CANON LXXXIV. (Greek lxxxvii.)","ITEM, it seemed good to petition the most glorious Emperors that the remains of idolatry not only in images, but in any places whatever or groves or trees, should altogether be taken away.","Item placuit ut gloriosissimi Imperatores petantur ut reliquiae idololatriae non solum in imaginibus, sed in quibuscumque locis vel lucis vel arboribus, penitus tollantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p388","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXXXIV.","Let all remains of idolatry be abolished whether in statues, or in places, or groves or trees.","Omnes reliquiae idololatriae aboleantur sive in statuis, sive in locis, sive in lucis, sive in arboribus."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p389","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXXIV.","This is Canon xix. of Carthage, September, A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon XIX. Carthaginensis, Septembri, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p390","CANON LXXXV. (Greek lxxxviii.)","That by the bishop of Carthage, when there shall be need, letters shall be written and subscribed in the name of all the bishops.","Ut a Carthaginensi episcopo, cum opus fuerit, epistolae scribantur et subscribantur in omnium episcoporum nomine."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p391","CANON LXXXV. (Greek lxxxviii.)","IT was said by all the bishops: If any letters are to be composed in the name of the council it seemed good that the venerable bishop who presides over this See should vouchsafe to dictate and sign them in the name of all, among which also are those to the episcopal legates, who are to be sent throughout the African provinces, in the matter of the Donatists; and it seemed good that the letters given them should contain the tenor of the mandate which they are not to go beyond. And they subscribed: I, Aurelius, bishop of the church of Carthage have consented to this decree and having read it have signed it. Likewise all the rest of the bishops subscribed.","Ab omnibus episcopis dictum est: Si quae epistolae in nomine concilii conscribendae sunt, placuit ut venerabilis episcopus qui huic Sedi praesidet, dignetur eas in omnium nomine dictare et subscribere, inter quas sunt et illae ad legatos episcopales, qui per Africanas provincias mittendi sunt in causa Donatistarum; et placuit ut epistolae eis datae mandati tenorem contineant quem non excedant. Et subscriptionibus confirmaverunt: Ego, Aurelius, episcopus ecclesiae Carthaginensis, huic decreto consensi et legi et subscripsi. Similiter et omnes reliqui episcopi subscripserunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p392","Ancient Epitome of Canon LXXXV.","It seemed good that whatever letters were to be sent from the Synod should be written and subscribed by the bishop of Carthage in the name of all.","Placuit ut quaecumque epistolae a Synodo mittendae essent, a Carthaginensi episcopo in omnium nomine scribantur et subscribantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p393","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXXV.","This is Canon xx. of Carthage, September, A.D. 401.","Hic est Canon XX. Carthaginensis, Septembri, anno 401."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p394","CANON LXXXVI. (Greek lxxxix.)","Of the order of bishops, that those ordained more recently do not dare to take precedence of those ordained before them.","De ordine episcoporum, ut recentius ordinati non praesumant se anteponere ante se ordinatis."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p395","CANON LXXXVI. (Greek lxxxix.)","VALENTINE, the bishop, said: If your good patience will permit, I follow the things which were done in time past in the Church of Carthage, and which were illustrious having been confirmed by the subscriptions of the brethren, and I profess that we intend to preserve this. For this we know, that ecclesiastical discipline has always remained inviolate: therefore let none of the brethren dare to place himself before those ordained earlier than himself; but by the offices of charity this has always been shewn to those ordained earlier, which always should be accepted joyfully by those ordained more recently. Let your holiness give command that this order be strengthened by your interlocutions. Aurelius, the bishop, said: It would not be fitting that we should repeat these things, were it not for the existence of certain inconsiderate minds, which would induce us to making such statutes; but this is a common cause about which our brother and fellow bishop has spoken, that each one of us should recognize the order decreed to him by God, and that the more recent should defer to the earlier ordained, and they should presume to do nothing when these have not been consulted. Wherefore I say, now that I think of it, that they who think they may presume to take precedence over those ordained before them, should be coerced suitably by the great council.","VALENTINUS episcopus dixit: Si bona patientia vestra permittit, sequor ea quae retro acta sunt in Ecclesia Carthaginensi, et quae clara fuerunt fratrum subscriptionibus confirmata, et profiteor nos hoc servare intendere. Hoc enim scimus, disciplinam ecclesiasticam semper inviolatam mansisse: nullus ergo fratrum praesumat se anteponere ante se ordinatis; sed per officia caritatis hoc semper exhibitum est ante ordinatis, quod semper a recentius ordinatis laetanter accipi debet. Iubeat sanctitas vestra hunc ordinem vestris interlocutionibus roborari. Aurelius episcopus dixit: Non deceret ut haec repeteremus, nisi essent quaedam inconsideratae mentes, quae nos ad huiusmodi statuta facienda inducerent; sed haec causa communis est de qua frater et coepiscopus noster locutus est, ut unusquisque nostrum agnoscat ordinem sibi a Deo decretum, et ut recentiores ante ordinatis deferant, et nihil praesumant facere non consultis his. Quapropter dico, nunc ut considero, ut qui sibi praesumere putant ut se anteponant ante se ordinatis, a magno concilio convenienter coerceantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p396","CANON LXXXVI. (Greek lxxxix.)","Xantippus, bishop of the first see of Numidia, said: All the brethren present have heard what our brother and fellow bishop Aurelius has said, what answer do we make? Datian, the bishop, said: The decrees made by our ancestors should be strengthened by our assent, so that the action taken by the Church of Carthage in past synods should hold fast, being confirmed by the full assent of all of us. And all the bishops said: This order has been preserved by our fathers and by our ancestors, and shall be preserved by us through the help of God, the rights of the primacy of Numidia and of Mauritania being kept intact.","Xantippus, primae sedis episcopus Numidiae, dixit: Audierunt omnes fratres praesentes quid frater et coepiscopus noster Aurelius dixerit, quid respondemus? Datianus episcopus dixit: Decreta a maioribus nostris nostro consensu roboranda sunt, ut actio quae a Carthaginensi Ecclesia in praeteritis synodis facta est firma permaneat, omnium nostrum pleno consensu confirmata. Et omnes episcopi dixerunt: Hic ordo a patribus nostris et a maioribus nostris servatus est, et a nobis per Dei auxilium servabitur, salvis primatiarum iuribus Numidiae et Mauritaniae."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p397","CANON LXXXVI. (Greek lxxxix.)","Of the archives and matricula of Numidia.",""],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p398","CANON LXXXVI. (Greek lxxxix.)","Moreover it seemed good to all the bishops who subscribed in this council that the matricula and the archives of Numidia should be at the first see and in the Metropolis, that is Constantina.","De archivis et matricula Numidiae."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p399","CANON LXXXVI. (Greek lxxxix.)","Thou shalt not prefer thyself to thine elders, but shalt follow them. For he that spurns those who were before him should be frowned down upon.","Item placuit omnibus episcopis qui in hoc concilio subscripserunt ut matricula et archiva Numidiae apud primam sedem et in Metropoli, hoc est Constantina, sint."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p400","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXXVI.","This canon (lxxxvj.) is Canon j., of the above named Synod of Milevis, August 27, A.D. 402.","Non praeponas te senioribus tuis, sed sequaris eos. Nam qui spernit eos qui ante se fuerunt, debet reprobari."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p401","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXXVI.","Concerning Quodvultdeus, the bishop.","Hic canon (lxxxvj.) est Canon I. praedictae Synodi Milevitanae, VI. Kalendas Septembres, anno 402."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p402","CANON LXXXVII. (Greek xc.)","IN the case of Quodvultdeus of Centuria, it pleased all the bishops that no one should communicate with him until his cause should be brought to a conclusion, for his accuser when he sought to bring the cause before our council, upon being asked whether he was willing with him to be tried before the bishops, at first said that he was, but on another day answered that he was not willing, and went away. Under these circumstances to deprive him of his bishoprick, before the conclusion of his cause was known, could commend itself to no Christian as a just act.","De Quodvultdeo episcopo."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p403","CANON LXXXVII. (Greek xc.)","Since Quodvultdeus at first promised to come to our synod when his opposer had asked that he be admitted, and afterwards withdrew, saying that that was displeasing to him, he should be excommunicated, until the cause is finished. But it is not just that he be deposed before sentence is given.","In causa Quodvultdei Centurionis placuit omnibus episcopis ut nemo cum eo communicet donec causa eius ad finem perducatur, nam accusator eius cum causam ad concilium nostrum deferre vellet, interrogatus utrum vellet cum eo coram episcopis iudicari, primo dixit se velle, alia autem die respondit nolle, et abscessit. His ita se habentibus, antequam finis causae eius cognitus esset, privare eum episcopatu nulli Christiano iustum videri potest."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p404","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXXVII.","This canon is part of Canon ij. of Synod of Milevis, A.D. 402.","Cum Quodvultdeus primo promiserit se ad synodum nostram venturum, postulante adversario ut admitteretur, et postea se subtraxerit dicens hoc sibi displicere, excommunicetur donec causa finiatur. Non est autem iustum ut ante sententiam deponatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p405","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXXVII.","Of Maximian, the bishop.","Hic canon est pars Canonis II. Synodi Milevitanae, anno 402."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p406","CANON LXXXVIII. (Greek xci.)","BUT in the case of Maximian of Vagai it seemed good that letters be sent from the council both to him and to his people; that he should vacate the bishoprick, and that they should request another to be appointed for them.","De Maximiano episcopo."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p407","CANON LXXXVIII. (Greek xci.)","Let Maximian of Bagai be expelled from his church, and another be set in his room.","In causa autem Maximiani Vagaiensis placuit ut epistolae de concilio et ad ipsum et ad populum eius mittantur; ut ipse episcopatu cedat, et illi alium sibi provideri petant."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p408","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXXVIII.","This canon is remaining part of Canon ij., of the Synod of Milevis, A.D. 402.","Maximianus Bagaiensis ab ecclesia sua expellatur, et alius in loco eius constituatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p409","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXXVIII.","That bishops who are ordained shall receive letters from their ordainers bearing the date and the name of the consul.","Hic canon est reliqua pars Canonis II. Synodi Milevitanae, anno 402."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p410","CANON LXXXIX. (Greek xcii.)","IT further seemed good that whoever thereafter should be ordained by the bishops throughout the African provinces, should receive from their ordainers letters, written in their own hands, containing the name of the consul and the date, that no altercation might arise concerning which were ordained first and which afterwards.","Ut episcopi qui ordinantur epistolas ab ordinatoribus suis accipiant tempus et nomen consulis continentes."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p411","CANON LXXXIX. (Greek xcii.)","Whoever is ordained in Africa let him have letters signed by the proper hand of him that ordained him, containing the date and the name of the Consul.","Item placuit ut quicumque deinceps per Africanas provincias ab episcopis ordinantur, epistolas ab ordinatoribus suis, manibus eorum scriptas, consulis nomen et tempus continentes accipiant, ne qua altercatio oriatur de his qui prius et qui postea ordinati sunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p412","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXXIX.","This is Canon iij. of Milevis, A.D. 402.","Quicumque in Africa ordinatur, habeat epistolas propria manu eius qui eum ordinavit subscriptas, tempus et nomen Consulis continentes."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p413","Epitome Antiqua Canonis LXXXIX.","Of those who have once read in church, that they cannot be advanced by others.","Hic est Canon III. Milevitanae, anno 402."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p414","CANON XC. (Greek xciii.)","ITEM, it seemed good that whoever in church even once had read should not be admitted to the ministry by another church.","De his qui semel in ecclesia legerunt, ut ab aliis promoveri non possint."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p415","CANON XC. (Greek xciii.)","And they subscribed: I, Aurelius, bishop of the Church of Carthage, have consented to this decree, and, having read it, have signed it. Likewise also the rest of the bishops signed.","Item placuit ut quicumque in ecclesia vel semel legit, ab altera ecclesia non admittatur ad clericatum."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p416","CANON XC. (Greek xciii.)","He who has only once read in a Church [i.e., diocese] shall not be admitted into the clergy by another Church.","Et subscriptionibus confirmaverunt: Ego, Aurelius, episcopus Ecclesiae Carthaginensis, huic decreto consensi, et legi, et subscripsi. Similiter et ceteri episcopi subscripserunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p417","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XC.","This is Canon iv. of Milevis, 402.","Qui vel semel legit in Ecclesia [id est, dioecesi], ab altera Ecclesia non admittatur in clerum."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p418","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XC.","Of holding meetings with the Donatists.","Hic est Canon IV. Milevitanae, anno 402."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p419","CANON XCI. (Greek xciv.)","AURELIUS, the bishop, said: What has come out in the handling of your charity, I think this should be confirmed by ecclesiastical acts. For the profession of all of you shews that each one of us should call together in his city the chiefs of the Donatists either alone and with one of his neighbour bishops, so that in like manner in the different cities and places there should be meetings of them assembled by the magistrates or seniors of the places. And let this be made an edict if it seems good to all. And all the bishops said: It seems good to all, and we all have confirmed this with our subscription. Also we desire that your holiness sign the letters to be sent from the council to the judges. Aurelius, the bishop, said: If it seems good to your charity, let the form of summoning them be read, in order that we all may hold the same tenour of proceeding. All the bishops said: Let it be read.","De habendis collationibus cum Donatistis."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p420","CANON XCI. (Greek xciv.)","This introduction together with the propositions of the different bishops belongs to the Synod of Carthage of August, 403. This canon (xci.) is Canon j. of that synod.","AURELIUS episcopus dixit: Quod in tractatu caritatis vestrae processit, hoc ecclesiasticis actis confirmari debere existimo. Omnium enim vestrum professio ostendit ut unusquisque nostrum in civitate sua principes Donatistarum convocet vel solus et cum uno vicino episcopo suo, ita ut similiter in diversis civitatibus et locis fiant eorum conventus magistratibus vel loci senioribus convocantibus. Et hoc edictum fiat si omnibus placet. Et omnes episcopi dixerunt: Omnibus placet, et omnes hoc subscriptione nostra confirmavimus. Etiam volumus ut epistolas ad iudices de concilio mittendas sanctitas vestra subscribat. Aurelius episcopus dixit: Si caritati vestrae placet, forma convocandi eos legatur, ut omnes eundem procedendi tenorem teneamus. Omnes episcopi dixerunt: Legatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p421","CANON XCI. (Greek xciv.)","Form of convening the Donatists.","Haec introductio una cum propositionibus diversorum episcoporum pertinet ad Synodum Carthaginensem Augusti, anno 403. Hic canon (xci.) est Canon I. illius synodi."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p422","CANON XCII. (Greek xcv.)","Formula convocandi Donatistas.",""],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p423","CANON XCII. (Greek xcv.)","THAT bishop of that church said: What by the authority of that most ample see we shall have impetrated, we ask your gravity to have read, and that you order it to be joined to the acts and carried into effect. When the jussio had been read and joined to the acts, the bishop of the Catholic Church said: Vouchsafe to listen to the mandate to be sent through your gravity to the Donatists, and to insert it in the acts, and to carry it to them, and informs us in your acts of their answer. \"We, sent by the authority of our Catholic Council, have called you together, desiring to rejoice in your correction, bearing in mind the charity of the Lord who said: Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called the children of God; and moreover he admonished through the prophet those who say they are not our brothers, that we ought to say: Ye are our brethren. Therefore you ought not to despise this pacific commonitory coming of love, so that if ye think we have any part of the truth, ye do not hesitate to say so: that is, when your council is gathered together, ye delegate of your number certain to whom you intrust the statement of your case; so that we may be able to do this also, that there shall be delegated from our Council who with them delegated by you may discuss peacefully, at a determined place and time, whatever question there is which separates your communion from us; and that at length the old error may receive an end through the assistance of our Lord God, lest through the animosity of men, weak souls, and ignorant people should perish by sacrilegious dissension. But if ye shall accept this proposition in a fraternal spirit, the truth will easily shine forth, but if ye are not willing to do this, your distrust will be easily known.\" And when this had been read, all the bishops said: This pleases us well, so let it be. And they subscribed: I, Aurelius, bishop of the Carthaginian Church, have consented to this decree, and having read it, have subscribed it. Likewise also the rest of the bishops signed.","Ille episcopus illius ecclesiae dixit: Quod auctoritate amplissimae sedis impetraverimus, rogamus gravitatem vestram ut legi iubeatis, et ut illud actis iungi et effectui mancipari iubeatis. Cum iussio lecta fuisset et actis iuncta, episcopus Ecclesiae Catholicae dixit: Audire dignemini mandatum per gravitatem vestram Donatistis mittendum, et actis inserendum, et ad eos perferendum, et responsum eorum nobis per acta vestra indicate. \"Nos, auctoritate Concilii Catholici nostri missi, convocavimus vos, desiderantes vestra correctione gaudere, memores caritatis Domini qui dixit: Beati pacifici, quoniam filii Dei vocabuntur; et insuper per prophetam admonuit eos qui dicunt se non esse fratres nostros, ut dicere debeamus: Fratres nostri estis. Non ergo debetis contemnere hoc pacificum commonitorium caritatis venientis, ut si putatis nos aliquam partem veritatis habere, non dubitetis dicere: id est, cum concilium vestrum collectum fuerit, delegatis ex numero vestro aliquos quibus causae vestrae insinuationem creditis; ut et nos hoc facere possimus, ut delegentur de Concilio nostro qui cum eis a vobis delegatis, placito loco et tempore, quidquid quaestionis est quae communionem vestram a nobis separat, pacifice disceptent; et ut tandem error antiquus per Domini Dei nostri adiutorium finem accipiat, ne per hominum animositates animae infirmae et populi ignorantes sacrilega dissensione pereant. Quod si fraterno animo hanc propositionem susceperitis, veritas facile elucescet; si autem hoc facere nolueritis, diffidentia vestra facile cognoscetur.\" Quo lecto, omnes episcopi dixerunt: Hoc valde nobis placet, ita fiat. Et subscriptionibus confirmaverunt: Ego, Aurelius, episcopus Carthaginensis Ecclesiae, huic decreto consensi, et legi, et subscripsi. Similiter et ceteri episcopi subscripserunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p424","CANON XCII. (Greek xcv.)","This synod sent a legation to the Princes against the Donatists. The most glorious emperor Honorius Augustus, being consul for the sixth time, on the Calends of July, at Carthage in the basilica of the second region. In this council Theasius and Euodius received a legation against the Donatists. In this council was inserted the commonitorium which follows.","Haec synodus legationem ad Principes contra Donatistas misit. Gloriosissimo imperatore Honorio Augusto VI consule, Kalendis Iuliis, Carthagine in basilica regionis secundae. In hoc concilio Theasius et Euodius legationem contra Donatistas acceperunt. In hoc concilio insertum est commonitorium quod sequitur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p425","Ancient Epitome of Canon XCII.","What things should be said to the Donatists are these: \"We greatly desire to rejoice in your conversion; for we have been commanded to say even to those not desiring to be our brethren, 'Ye are our brothers.' We come therefore to you and we exhort you that if you have any defence to make, ye should appoint certain persons to whom this should be entrusted, who, at a fixed time and place, shall urge your case; otherwise your distrust will be thenceforward patent.\"","Quae dicenda sunt Donatistis haec sunt: \"Magnopere cupimus conversione vestra gaudere; nam iussi sumus dicere etiam nolentibus esse fratribus nostris: Fratres nostri estis. Venimus ergo ad vos et exhortamur vos ut si quid habetis quod pro vobis dicatis, constituatis aliquas personas quibus hoc committendum sit, quae statuto tempore et loco causam vestram agant; alioquin diffidentia vestra exinde patebit.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p426","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XCII.","This canon is Canon ij. of the Synod of Carthage of August 25, A.D. 403.","Hic canon est Canon II. Synodi Carthaginensis, VIII. Kalendas Septembres, anno 403."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p427","CANON XCIII. (Greek xcvi.)","The character of the Commonitory which the legates received against the Donatists.","Quale sit Commonitorium quod legati contra Donatistas acceperunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p428","CANON XCIII. (Greek xcvi.)","THE Commonitorium for our brothers Theasius and Evodius, sent as legates from the Council of Carthage to the most glorious and most religious princes. When by the help of the Lord they are come into the presence of the most pious princes, they shall declare to them with what fulness of confidence, according to the direction of the council of the year before, the prelates of the Donatists had been urged by the municipal authority to assemble, in order that if they really meant their professions, they might by fit persons chosen from their number, enter into a peaceful conference with us in Christian meekness, and whatever they held as truth they might not hesitate to declare it frankly; so that from such conference the sincerity of the Catholic position, which has been conspicuous for so long a time, might be perceived even by those who from ignorance or obstinacy were opposing themselves to it. But deterred by their want of confidence they scarcely ventured to reply. And forsooth, because we had discharged toward them the offices which become bishops and peacemakers, and they had no answer to make to the truth, they betook themselves to unreasonable acts of brute force, and treacherously oppressed many of the bishops and clergy, to say nothing of the laity. And some of the churches they actually invaded, and tried to assault still others.","Commonitorium fratribus nostris Theasio et Evodio, legatis de Concilio Carthaginensi ad gloriosissimos et religiosissimos principes missis. Cum Domino adiuvante in conspectum piissimorum principum venerint, indicabunt eis quanta plenitudine fiduciae, secundum praecedentis anni concilii ordinationem, praepositi Donatistarum auctoritate municipali convocati fuerint, ut si revera proposita sua vellent implere, idoneis ex numero suo electis personis, pacificam conferentiam nobiscum in Christiana mansuetudine inirent, et quidquid pro veritate haberent aperte profiteri non dubitarent; ita ut ex tali conferentia sinceritas Catholicae positionis, quae tam diu conspicua fuerat, etiam ab his qui per ignorantiam vel pertinaciam adversabantur, agnosceretur. Sed diffidentia deterriti vix respondere ausi sunt. Et videlicet, quia erga illos episcopis et pacificis digna officia impenderamus, et nihil veritati respondere habebant, ad irrationabiles vires et violentiam confugerunt, et multos episcoporum et clericorum (ut de laicis nihil dicatur) dolose oppresserunt. Et quasdam ecclesias invaserunt, aliasque invadere conati sunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p429","CANON XCIII. (Greek xcvi.)","And now, it behoves the gracious clemency of their Majesties to take measures that the Catholic Church, which has begotten them as worshippers of Christ in her womb, and has nourished them with the strong meat of the faith, should by their forethought, be defended, lest violent men, taking advantage of the times of religious excitement, should by fear overcome a weak people, whom by argument they were not able to pervert. It is well known how often the vile gatherings of the Circumcelliones have been forbidden by the laws, and also condemned by many decrees of the Emperors, their majesties most religious predecessors. Against the madness of these people it is not unusual nor contrary to the holy Scriptures to ask for secular protection, since Paul the Apostle, as is related in the authentic Acts of the Apostles, warded off a conspiracy of certain lawless men by the help of the military. Now then we ask that there be extended to the Catholic Churches, without any dissimulation, the protection of the companies of soldiers stationed in each city, and of the holders of the suburban estates in the various places. At the same time it will be necessary to ask that they give commandment that the law, set forth by their father Theodosius, of pious memory, which imposed a fine of ten pounds of gold upon both the ordainers and the ordained among heretics, and which was also directed against proprietors at whose houses conventicles were held, be confirmed anew; so that it may be effective with persons of this sort when Catholics, provoked by their wiles, shall lay complaint against them; so that through fear at least, they may cease from making schisms and from the wickedness of the heretics, since they refuse to be cleansed and corrected by the thought of the eternal punishment.","Et nunc oportet clementiam gratiosam Maiestatum eorum decernere ut Catholica Ecclesia, quae eos ut Christi cultores in utero suo genuit et fidei robore nutrivit, eorum providentia defendatur, ne violenti homines, temporibus religiosi fervoris abutentes, populum infirmum quem disputatione pervertere non potuerunt, timore superent. Notum est quam saepe detestabiles Circumcellionum manus legibus prohibita fuerint, et etiam multis decretis Imperatorum, religiosissimorum praedecessorum Maiestatum eorum, damnata. Contra horum insaniam non insolens est nec sanctis Scripturis contrarium saeculare auxilium postulare, cum Paulus Apostolus, sicut in authenticis Actis Apostolorum legitur, conspirationem quorundam sceleratorum militari auxilio repulerit. Nunc ergo petimus ut Catholicis Ecclesiis, sine ulla dissimulatione, praebeatur protectio ordinum in unaquaque civitate, et possessorum suburbanorum in singulis locis. Simul petendum erit ut legem a patre eorum Theodosio piae memoriae promulgatam, quae decem libras auri tam ordinatoribus quam ordinatis haereticis irrogavit, et etiam contra dominos apud quos conventicula habebantur directa, denuo confirmari iubeant; ut vigeat erga huiusmodi personas cum Catholici eorum insidiis provocati adversus eos conquesti fuerint; ut vel timore desistant a schismatibus faciendis et ab haereticorum impietate, cum aeternae poenae consideratione emundari et corrigi nolunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p430","CANON XCIII. (Greek xcvi.)","Let request be also made that the law depriving heretics of the power of being able to receive or bequeath by gift or by will, be straightway renewed by their Piety, so that all right of giving or receiving may be taken away from those who, blinded by the madness of obstinacy, are determined to continue in the error of the Donatists. With regard to those who by considerations of unity and peace are willing to correct themselves, let permission be granted to them to receive their inheritance, the law notwithstanding, even though the bequest by gift or inheritance was made while they were yet living in the error of the heretics; those of course being excepted, who under the stress of legal proceedings have sought to enter the Catholic Church; for it may well be supposed, that persons of this latter sort desired Catholic unity, not so much from fear of the judgment of heaven, as from the greed of earthly gain.","Petatur etiam ut lex haereticos donationis vel testamenti faciendi vel capiendi iure privans, denuo ab eorum Pietate renovetur, ut omne ius dandi vel accipiendi auferatur ab his qui, obstinationis insania excaecati, in errore Donatistarum perseverare decreverunt. De his autem qui unitatis et pacis consideratione se corrigere volunt, permittatur eis hereditatem suam lege non obstante recipere, etiamsi donatio vel hereditas dum adhuc in haereticorum errore viverent facta sit; exceptis videlicet his qui instantia iudiciorum Catholicam Ecclesiam intrare quaesiverunt; de talibus enim merito creditur non tam iudicii coelestis metu quam terrenae cupiditatis aviditate Catholicam unitatem desiderasse."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p431","CANON XCIII. (Greek xcvi.)","For the furtherance of all these things the help of the Powers of each one of the provinces is needed. With regard to other matters, whatever they shall perceive is for the Church's interests, this we have resolved that the legation have full authority to do and to carry into effect. Moreover it seemed good to us all, that letters from our assembly should be sent to the most glorious Emperors and most Excellent Worthinesses, whereby they may be assured of the agreement of us all that the legates should be sent by us to their most blessed court. Since it is a very slow business for us all to set our names to these letters, and in order that they may not be burdened with the signature of each one of us, we desire thee, brother Aurelius, that thy charity be good enough to sign them in the name of us all. And to this they all agreed.","Ad haec omnia exsequenda, auxilium Potestatum uniuscuiusque provinciarum necessarium est. De ceteris autem, quidquid ecclesiae utilitatibus congruere perspexerint, id legationem liberam potestatem habere faciendi et exsequendi decrevimus. Item placuit omnibus nobis ut epistolae de conventu nostro ad gloriosissimos Imperatores et Excellentissimas Potestates mittantur, quibus certiores fiant de consensu omnium nostrum legatos a nobis ad beatissimam curiam eorum mittendos. Quia autem valde longum est ut omnes epistolis subscribamus, et ne sigillis singulorum onerentur, volumus, frater Aureli, ut caritas tua dignetur eas in omnium nostrum nomine subscribere. Huic omnes consenserunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p432","CANON XCIII. (Greek xcvi.)","I, Aurelius, Bishop of the Church of Carthage have consented to this decree and have subscribed my name. And so all the other bishops subscribed.","Ego, Aurelius, Episcopus Ecclesiae Carthaginensis, huic decreto consensi et nomen meum subscripsi. Et ita omnes alii episcopi subscripserunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p433","CANON XCIII. (Greek xcvi.)","Letters ought likewise to be sent to the judges that, until the lord permit the legates to return to us, they give protection through the soldiers of the cities, and through the holders of the farms of the Catholic Church. It ought also to be added concerning the dishonest Equitius, which he had shewn by laying claim to the jus sacerdotum, that he be rejected from the diocese of Hippo according to the statutes of the Emperors. Letters ought also to be sent to the Bishop of the Church of Rome in commendation of the legates, and to the other Bishops who may be where the Emperor is. To this they assented.","Epistolae quoque ad iudices mittendae sunt ut, donec legatos ad nos redire Dominus permiserit, per milites urbium et per possessores fundorum Ecclesiae Catholicae praesidium praebeant. Addendum etiam de improbo Equitio, quod exhibuerit sibi vendicando ius sacerdotum, ut secundum statuta Imperatorum a dioecesi Hipponensi repellatur. Epistolae quoque ad Episcopum Ecclesiae Romanae pro commendatione legatorum mittendae sunt, et ad ceteros Episcopos qui ubi Imperator est fuerint. Huic consenserunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p434","CANON XCIII. (Greek xcvi.)","Likewise I, Aurelius, Bishop of the Church of Carthage, have consented to this decree, and having read it, have set my name to it. And all the other bishops likewise subscribed.","Similiter ego, Aurelius, Episcopus Ecclesiae Carthaginensis, huic decreto consensi, et legi, et nomen meum subscripsi. Et omnes alii episcopi similiter subscripserunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p435","Ancient Epitome of Canon XCIII.","The Emperors who were born in the true religion and were educated in the faith, ought to stretch forth a helping hand to the Churches. For the military band overthrew the dire conspiracy which was threatening Paul.","Imperatores qui in vera religione nati sunt et in fide educati, porrigere manum adiutricem Ecclesiis debent. Cohors enim militaris diremptionem direxit quae imminebat Paulo."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p436","CANON XCIV. (Greek xcvii.)","Summary of Chapters.","Summarium Capitulorum."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p437","CANON XCIV. (Greek xcvii.)","THAT a free delegation be sent to the council from all the provinces to Mizoneum. Legates and letters were ordered to be sent for the purpose of directing the free legation: that because the unity had been made only at Carthage, letters should also be given to the judges, that they might order in the other provinces and cities the work of union to be proceeded with, and the thanksgivings of the Church of Carthage for the whole of Africa concerning the exclusion of the Donatists should be sent with the letters of the bishops to Court. The letters of Pope Innocent were read: that bishops ought not readily to carry causes across seas, and this very thing was confirmed by the judgment of the bishops themselves; that on account of thanksgiving and the exclusion of the Donatists, two clerics of the Church of Carthage should be sent to Court.","Ut libera legatio ad concilium de omnibus provinciis ad Mizoneum mittatur. Legati et epistolae mitti iussi sunt ad dirigendam liberam legationem: quia unitas tantum Carthagine facta fuerat, darentur etiam epistolae ad iudices, ut in ceteris provinciis et urbibus opus unionis procedere iuberent, et gratiarum actiones Ecclesiae Carthaginensis pro tota Africa de exclusione Donatistarum cum epistolis episcoporum ad Comitatum mittantur. Litterae Papae Innocentii lectae sunt: ut episcopi non facile causas trans mare perferant, et hoc ipsum iudicio episcoporum confirmatum est; ut propter gratiarum actionem et exclusionem Donatistarum duo clerici Ecclesiae Carthaginensis ad Comitatum mittantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p438","Ancient Epitome of Canon XCIV.","It seemed good that letters be sent to the Magistrates that the Donatists be expelled.","Placuit ut epistolae ad Magistratus mittantur ut Donatistae expellantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p439","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XCIV.","This introduction is taken from the Synod of Carthage of August 23, 405. There is also added the introduction of the Synod of Carthage of June 13, 407.","Haec introductio sumpta est ex Synodo Carthaginensi, X. Kalendas Septembres, anno 405. Addita est etiam introductio Synodi Carthaginensis, Idibus Iuniis, anno 407."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p440","CANON XCV. (Greek xcviii.)","An universal council to be held only when necessary.","Universale concilium tantum cum necesse fuerit habendum."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p441","CANON XCV. (Greek xcviii.)","IT seemed good that there should be no more the yearly necessity of fatiguing the brethren; but as often as common cause, that is of the whole of Africa, demands, that letters shall be given on every side to that see in this matter, that a synod should be gathered in that province, where the desirability of it induces; but let the causes which are not of general interest be judged in their own provinces.","Placuit ut non sit annua necessitas fatigandi fratres; sed quotiens causa communis, id est totius Africae, exegerit, ut undique ad illam sedem de hac re epistolae dentur, ut synodus in ea provincia, ubi opportunitas suaserit, congregetur; causae autem quae non sunt generales in suis provinciis iudicentur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p442","Ancient Epitome of Canon XCV.","When general necessity so urges, letters are to be sent to the chief see, and a synod held in a convenient place. But let ordinary causes be settled in their own provinces.","Cum generalis necessitas ita suaserit, epistolae ad primam sedem mittendae sunt, et synodus in loco opportuno habenda. Causae autem ordinariae in suis provinciis terminandae sunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p443","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XCV.","This canon is Canon j. of the Synod of Carthage, A.D. 407.","Hic canon est Canon I. Synodi Carthaginensis, anno 407."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p444","CANON XCVI. (Greek xcix.)","That from judges who have been chosen, no appeals may be taken.","Ut a iudicibus qui electi fuerint, appellari non possit."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p445","CANON XCVI. (Greek xcix.)","IF an appeal be taken, let him who makes it choose the judges, and with him he also against whom the appeal is taken; and from their decision no appeal may be made.","Si appellatum fuerit, eligat iudices qui appellat, et cum eo ille adversus quem appellatum est; et ab eorum iudicio appellari non licebit."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p446","CANON XCVI. (Greek xcix.)","Concerning the delegates of the different provinces. When all the delegates of the different provinces came together, they have been most graciously received, that is those of the Numidians, Byzacenes, Stifensian Moors, as well as Cæsarians and Tripolitans.","De delegatis diversarum provinciarum. Cum omnes delegati diversarum provinciarum convenerunt, benignissime suscepti sunt, id est Numidiarum, Byzacenorum, Stifensium Maurorum, necnon Caesariensium et Tripolitanorum."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p447","CANON XCVI. (Greek xcix.)","Concerning the executors of Churches. It has seemed good moreover that the appointment of five executors should be asked for in all matters pertaining to the necessities of the Church, who shall be portioned off in the different provinces.","De executoribus Ecclesiarum. Placuit autem ut constitutio quinque executorum in omnibus quae ad necessitates Ecclesiae pertinent petatur, qui per diversas provincias dividendi sunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p448","Ancient Epitome of Canon XCVI.","If one party to a suit takes an appeal, and if both choose together a judge, no further appeal shall be allowed.","Si una pars iudicii appellaverit, et si ambo simul elegerint iudicem, non liceat ulterius appellare."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p449","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XCVI.","This canon is Canon ij. of Carthage, A.D. 407.","Hic canon est Canon II. Carthaginensis, anno 407."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p450","CANON XCVII. (Greek c.)","That there be sought from the Emperor the protection of Advocates in causes ecclesiastical.","Ut ab Imperatore petatur patrocinium Advocatorum in causis ecclesiasticis."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p451","CANON XCVII. (Greek c.)","IT seemed good that the legates who were about leaving, viz., Vincent and Fortunatian, should in the name of all the provinces ask from the most glorious Emperors to give a faculty for the establishment of scholastic defensors, whose shall be the care of this very kind of business: so that as the priests of the province, they who have received the faculty as defensors of the Churches in ecclesiastical affairs, as often as necessity arises, may be able to enter the private apartments of the judges, so as to resist what is urged on the other side, or to make necessary explanations.","Placuit ut legati qui exituri erant, videlicet Vincentius et Fortunatianus, in omnium provinciarum nomine a gloriosissimis Imperatoribus petant ut facultatem pro constituendis defensoribus scholasticis tribuant, quorum sit cura huius generis negotiorum: ut sicut sacerdotes provinciae, qui facultatem defensorum Ecclesiarum in causis ecclesiasticis acceperint, quotiens necessitas exegerit, possint in secreta iudicum introire, ut his quae ex adverso aguntur resistant, vel quae necessaria sunt exponant."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p452","Ancient Epitome of Canon XCVII.","That there be asked of the Emperor the appointment of Patrons for ecclesiastical heads, whose care it should be to defend the Church in its affairs, and who as priests could easily refer what things were urgent.","Ut ab Imperatore petatur constitutio Patronorum pro ecclesiasticis capitibus, quorum cura sit Ecclesiam in negotiis suis defendere, et qui ut sacerdotes possint quae necessaria sunt referre."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p453","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XCVII.","(Greek ci.) That the legation be free.","(Gr. ci.) Ut legatio libera sit."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p454","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XCVII.","IT seemed good that the chosen legates should have at the meeting freedom of action.","Placuit ut legati electi in conventu liberam habeant actionem."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p455","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XCVII.","This canon is Canon iij. of Carthage, A.D. 407.","Hic canon est Canon III. Carthaginensis, anno 407."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p456","CANON XCVIII. (Greek cii.)","Of the peoples which never had bishops.","De plebibus quae numquam episcopos habuerunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p457","CANON XCVIII. (Greek cii.)","IT seemed good that such peoples as had never had bishops of their own should in no way receive such unless it had been decreed in a plenary council of each province and by the primates, and with the consent of the bishop of that diocese to which the church belonged.","Placuit ut eae plebes quae numquam proprios episcopos habuerunt nullo modo tales accipiant nisi in plenario concilio uniuscuiusque provinciae et a primatibus decretum fuerit, et cum consensu episcopi illius dioecesis ad quam ecclesia pertinebat."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p458","Ancient Epitome of Canon XCVIII.","Whoso never heretofore had a bishop of their own, unless the general synod of the Province shall agree to it, and the Primate, in agreement with him to whom the province in which the Church is, is subject, shall not have bishops of their own.","Qui numquam antea proprium episcopum habuerunt, nisi generalis Synodus Provinciae consenserit, et Primas, cum eo cui provincia in qua ecclesia est subiecta est, concordante, non habeant proprios episcopos."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p459","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XCVIII.","This canon is Canon iv. of the Synod of Carthage, A.D. 407.","Hic canon est Canon IV. Synodi Carthaginensis, anno 407."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p460","CANON XCIX. (Greek ciii.)","Of people or dioceses returned from the Donatists.","De plebibus vel dioecesibus a Donatistis reversis."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p461","CANON XCIX. (Greek ciii.)","SUCH communities as have returned from the Donatists and have had bishops, without doubt may continue to have them even without any action of the councils, but such a community as had a bishop and when he dies wish no longer to have a bishop of their own, but to belong to the diocese of some other bishop, this is not to be denied them. Also such bishops as before the promulgation of the imperial law concerning unity as brought back their people to the Catholic Church, they ought to be allowed still to rule them: but from the time of that law of unity, all the Churches, and their dioceses, and if perchance there be any instruments of the Church or things pertaining to its rights should belong to the Catholic bishops of those places to whom the places pertained while under the heretics, whether they be converted to the Catholic Church or remain unconverted heretics. Whoever after this law shall make any such usurpation, shall restore as is meet the usurped possessions.","Eae plebes quae a Donatistis reversae sunt et episcopos habuerunt, procul dubio eos retinere possunt etiam sine ulla actione conciliorum; plebs autem quae episcopum habuit et eo mortuo non vult ultra proprium episcopum habere, sed ad alterius episcopi dioecesim pertinere, non est ei negandum. Item episcopi qui ante promulgationem legis imperialis de unitate plebes suas ad Ecclesiam Catholicam reduxerunt, eis adhuc regere permittantur: a tempore autem illius legis unitatis, omnes Ecclesiae, et dioeceses eorum, et si qua forte instrumenta Ecclesiae vel ad iura eius pertinentia, ad Catholicos episcoporum illorum locorum pertinebunt ad quos loca dum sub haereticis erant pertinebant, sive ad Ecclesiam Catholicam convertantur sive haeretici inconversi maneant. Quicumque post hanc legem talem usurpationem fecerit, usurpata possidenda pro condigno restituet."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p462","Ancient Epitome of Canon XCIX.","Whoever are converted from the Donatists may retain their own bishops, although they had them without the consent of the synod; and when the bishop is dead, if they do not wish another to be substituted in his room, but desire to place themselves under some other bishop, they shall be allowed to do so. And such bishops as before the union have brought back the people they ruled, let them still rule them. After the imperial Edict on Unity every church must defend its own rights.","Quicumque a Donatistis convertuntur, retinere possunt proprios episcopos, etiamsi sine synodi consensu eos habuerunt; et mortuo episcopo, si non volunt alium in loco eius substitui, sed sub alio episcopo se constituere, permittantur. Et episcopi qui ante unionem plebes quas regebant reduxerunt, adhuc eas regant. Post Imperiale Edictum de Unitate, unaquaeque ecclesia sua iura defendere debet."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p463","Epitome Antiqua Canonis XCIX.","This canon is Canon v. of Carthage, A.D. 407.","Hic canon est Canon V. Carthaginensis, anno 407."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p464","CANON C. (Greek civ.)","Of the suggestion of Bishop Maurentius.","De suggestione Episcopi Maurentii."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p465","CANON C. (Greek civ.)","[Hefele says \"The text of this canon is much corrupted and very difficult to be understood.\" He gives as a synopsis, \"The council appoints judges in the affair of Bishop Maurentius.\"]","[Hefele dicit: \"Textus huius canonis multum corruptus est et valde difficilis ad intellegendum.\" Dat synopsim: \"Concilium constituit iudices in negotio Episcopi Maurentii.\"]"],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p466","Ancient Epitome of Canon C.","It is right that sentence be given on the subdeacons who are said to be present from Nova Germania, who have thrice been sought and not found. But out of regard to ecclesiastical gentleness, let some be sent to look into the matter.","Iustum est ut sententia detur de subdiaconibus qui de Nova Germania praesentes esse dicuntur, qui ter quaesiti et non inventi sunt. Sed propter ecclesiasticam mansuetudinem, mittantur aliqui ad inquirendum."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p467","Epitome Antiqua Canonis C.","This canon is Canon vj. of Carthage, A.D. 407.","Hic canon est Canon VI. Carthaginensis, anno 407."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p468","CANON CI. (Greek civ. bis)","Of making peace between the Churches of Rome and Alexandria.","De pace facienda inter Ecclesias Romae et Alexandriae."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p469","CANON CI. (Greek civ. bis)","IT seemed good that a letter be written to the holy Pope Innocent concerning the dissension between the Churches of Rome and Alexandria, so that each Church might keep peace with the other as the Lord commanded.","Placuit ut epistola ad sanctum Papam Innocentium scribatur de dissensione inter Ecclesias Romae et Alexandriae, ut unaquaeque Ecclesia pacem cum altera servet sicut Dominus praecepit."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p470","Ancient Epitome of Canon CI.","It seemed good to write to Innocent that the Roman and Alexandrian churches might be at peace between themselves.","Placuit scribere ad Innocentium ut Romana et Alexandrina ecclesiae pacem inter se habeant."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p471","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CI.","This canon is Canon vij. of Carthage, A.D. 407.","Hic canon est Canon VII. Carthaginensis, anno 407."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p472","CANON CII. (Greek cv.)","Of those who put away their wives or husbands, that so they remain.","De his qui dimittunt uxores vel maritos, ut ita permaneant."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p473","CANON CII. (Greek cv.)","IT seemed good that according to evangelical and apostolical discipline a man who had been put away from his wife, and a woman put away from her husband should not be married to another, but so should remain, or else be reconciled the one to the other; but if they spurn this law, they shall be forced to do penance, covering which case we must petition that an imperial law be promulgated.","Placuit ut secundum evangelicam et apostolicam disciplinam vir qui ab uxore dimissus est, et mulier quae a viro dimissa est, non alteri nubant, sed ita permaneant, aut sibi invicem reconcilientur; quod si hanc legem spreverint, ad paenitentiam cogantur; de qua re petendum est ut lex imperialis promulgari iubeatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p474","Ancient Epitome of Canon CII.","Married people who are loosed must remain unmarried or else be reconciled, otherwise they shall be forced to do penance.","Coniugati qui solvuntur debent permanere innupti aut reconcilari, alioquin ad paenitentiam cogentur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p475","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CII.","This canon is Canon viij. of Carthage, A.D. 407.","Hic canon est Canon VIII. Carthaginensis, anno 407."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p476","CANON CIII. (Greek cvi.)","Of the prayers to be said at the Altar.","De orationibus ad Altare dicendis."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p477","CANON CIII. (Greek cvi.)","THIS also seemed good, that the prayers which had been approved in synod should be used by all, whether prefaces, commendations, or laying on of the hand, and that others contrary to the faith should not be used by any means, but that those only should be said which had been collected by the learned.","Hoc quoque placuit, ut orationes quae in synodo approbatae sunt ab omnibus adhibeantur, sive praefationes, sive commendationes, sive manus impositiones, et ut aliae fidei contrariae nullo modo adhibeantur, sed solae dicantur quae a prudentioribus collectae sunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p478","Ancient Epitome of Canon CIII.","[The same as the canon, but omits the last phrase.]","[Idem quod canon, sed ultimam clausulam omittit.]"],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p479","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CIII.","This canon is Canon ix. of Carthage, A.D. 407.","Hic canon est Canon IX. Carthaginensis, anno 407."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p480","CANON CIV. (Greek cvii.)","Of these who ask from the Emperor that secular judges may take cognizance of their causes.","De his qui ab Imperatore petunt ut iudices saeculares cognoscant causas eorum."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p481","CANON CIV. (Greek cvii.)","IT seemed good that whoever should seek from the Emperor, that secular judges should take cognizance of his business, should be deprived of his office; if however, he had asked from the Emperor an episcopal trial, no objection should be made.","Placuit ut quicumque ab Imperatore petierit ut iudices saeculares causas eius cognoscant, officio suo privetur; si autem ab Imperatore iudicium episcopale petierit, nulla fiat obiectio."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p482","Ancient Epitome of Canon CIV.","Let not him be a bishop who from the Emperor seeks a public judgment.","Non sit episcopus qui ab Imperatore publicum iudicium petit."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p483","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CIV.","This canon is Canon X. of Carthage, A.D. 407.","Hic canon est Canon X. Carthaginensis, anno 407."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p484","CANON CV. (Greek cviii.)","Of those who do not communicate in Africa and would go across seas.","De his qui in Africa non communicant et trans mare ire volunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p485","CANON CV. (Greek cviii.)","WHOEVER does not communicate in Africa, and goes to communicate across seas, let him be cast out of the clergy.","Quicumque in Africa non communicat, et trans mare communicatum vadit, de clero abiciatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p486","Ancient Epitome of Canon CV.","Whoever is cut off from communion in Africa, and goes to parts across seas that he may there communicate, is to be cast out of the clergy.","Quicumque in Africa communione abscissus est, et ad transmarinas partes vadit ut ibi communicet, de clero abiciatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p487","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CV.","This canon is Canon j. of Carthage, A.D. 407.","Hic canon est Canon I. Carthaginensis, anno 407."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p488","CANON CVI. (Greek cix.)","That those who are going to carry their case to court should be careful to inform either the bishop of Carthage or the bishop of Rome.","Ut qui causam suam ad comitatum perlaturi sunt, diligenter informare debeant vel episcopum Carthaginensem vel episcopum Romanum."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p489","CANON CVI. (Greek cix.)","IT seemed good that whoever wished to go to court, should give notice in the form which is sent to the Church of the city of Rome, that from thence also he should receive a formed letter to court. But if receiving only a formed letter to Rome, and saying nothing about the necessity which he had of going to court, he willed immediately to go thither, let him be cut off from communion. But if while at Rome the necessity of going to court suddenly arose, let him state his necessity to the bishop of Rome and let him carry with him a rescript of the same Roman bishop. But let the formed letters which are issued by primates and by certain bishops to their own clergy have the date of Easter; but if it be yet uncertain what is the date of Easter of that year, let the preceding Easter's date be set down, as it is customary to date public acts after the consulship. It further seemed good that those who were sent as delegates from this glorious council should ask of the most glorious princes whatever they saw would be useful against the Donatists and Pagans, and their superstitions. It also seemed good to all the bishops that all conciliar letters be signed by your holiness alone. And they subscribed: I, Aurelius, Bishop of Carthage, have consented to this decree, and having read it, now subscribe my name. Likewise also the rest of the bishops subscribed.","Placuit ut quicumque ad comitatum ire voluerit, in forma quae ad Ecclesiam urbis Romae mittitur, se debeat insinuare, ut inde quoque accipiat formatam epistolam ad comitatum. Quod si tantummodo formatam Romam acceperit, nihil dicens de necessitate quam habebat ad comitatum pergendi, statim illuc ire voluerit, a communione abscindatur. Si autem Romae necessitas ad comitatum pergendi subito evenerit, indicet necessitatem suam episcopo Romano, et rescriptum eiusdem episcopi Romani secum perferat. Formatae autem epistolae quae a primatibus et a quibusdam episcopis suis clericis dantur, diem Paschae habeant; si autem incertum est adhuc quis sit dies Paschae illius anni, praecedentis Paschae dies ponatur, sicut acta publica post consulatum subscribi solent. Item placuit ut qui legati de hoc glorioso concilio missi sunt, quaecumque contra Donatistas et Paganos et superstitiosa eorum utilia esse perspexerint, a gloriosissimis principibus postulent. Item placuit omnibus episcopis ut omnes conciliares epistolae a sola sanctitate vestra subscribantur. Et subscriptionibus confirmaverunt: Ego, Aurelius, Episcopus Carthaginensis, huic decreto consensi, et legi, et nomen meum subscripsi. Similiter et ceteri episcopi subscripserunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p490","Ancient Epitome of Canon CVI.","Whoever from any necessity was going to court, must declare his intention to the bishop of Carthage and to the bishop of Rome, and receive a letter dimissory, and otherwise he shall be excommunicated. Whatever shall seem to the legates useful against the Donatists and Greeks, and their superstitions, that shall be sought from the Emperor.","Quicumque ex aliqua necessitate ad comitatum pergebat, insinuare debet propositum suum episcopo Carthaginensi et episcopo Romano, et accipere litteras dimissorias; alioquin excommunicetur. Quidquid legatis contra Donatistas et Graecos et superstitiosa eorum utile visum fuerit, ab Imperatore petatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p491","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CVI.","This canon is Canon xij. of Carthage, A.D. 407.","Hic canon est Canon XII. Carthaginensis, anno 407."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p492","CANON CVII. (Greek cx. continued.)","A Council concerning a bishop taking cognizance.","Concilium de episcopo cognoscente."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p493","CANON CVII. (Greek cx. continued.)","IN the consulate of the most glorious Emperors Honorius for the VIIth time and Theodosius for the IIIrd, Augusti, xvii. Calends of July, a synod was held at Carthage in the basilica of the second region. In this council it seemed good that no one bishop should claim the right to take cognizance of a cause. The acts of this council I have not here written down, because it was only provincial and not general.","Honorio VII et Theodosio III, Augustis, consulibus, xvii. Kalendas Iulias, synodus habita est Carthagine in basilica regionis secundae. In hoc concilio placuit ut nullus episcopus sibi vindicet ius cognoscendae causae. Acta huius concilii non descripsi hic, quia tantum provinciale fuit et non generale."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p494","Ancient Epitome of Canon CVII.","One bishop shall not claim for himself to take cognizance of a cause alone.","Unus episcopus non sibi vindicet causam solus cognoscere."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p495","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CVII.","(Greek cxi.) Synod against the Donatists.","(Gr. cxi.) Synodus contra Donatistas."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p496","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CVII.","After the consulate of the most illustrious Emperors Honorius for the VIIIth time and Theodosius for the IVth time, Augusti, xviii. Calends of July, at Carthage in the basilica of the second region. In this council the bishops, Florentius, Possidius, Præsidius and Benenatus received legation against the Donatists, at that time at which a law was given that anyone might practice the Christian worship at his own will.","Post consulatum Honorii VIII et Theodosii IIII, Augustorum, xviii. Kalendas Iulias, Carthagine in basilica regionis secundae. In hoc concilio episcopi Florentius, Possidius, Praesidius et Benenatus legationem contra Donatistas acceperunt, eo tempore quo lex data est ut quisque Christianam religionem sua voluntate exerceret."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p497","Ancient Epitome of Canon CVII.","Let each one receive the practice of piety of his own free will.","Unusquisque suae voluntatis arbitrio pietatis cultum recipiat."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p498","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CVII.","The two first introductions belong respectively to the Synods of Carthage of June 16 and of October 13, A.D. 408. Canon cvij. of the African code and that which follows it are the introductions to the Synods of Carthage of June 15, A.D. 409, and of June 14, A.D. 410.","Duae primae introductiones pertinent respective ad Synodos Carthaginenses, xvi. Kalendas Iulias, anno 408, et iii. Idus Octobres, anno 408. Canon cvij. Codicis Africani et id quod eum sequitur sunt introductiones ad Synodos Carthaginenses, Idibus Iuniis, anno 409, et xviii. Kalendas Iulias, anno 410."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p499","CANON CVIII. (Greek cxii.)","Synod against the heresy of Pelagius and Celestius.","Synodus contra haeresim Pelagii et Caelestii."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p500","CANON CVIII. (Greek cxii.)","IN the consulate of the most glorious Emperors, Honorius for the XIIth time and Theodosius for the VIIIth, Augusti most exalted, on the Calends of May, at Carthage in the secretarium of the Basilica of Faustus. When Aurelius the bishop presided over the whole council, the deacons standing by, it pleased all the bishops, whose names and subscriptions are indicated, met together in the holy synod of the Church of Carthage to define—","Honorio XII et Theodosio VIII, Augustis excellentissimis, consulibus, Kalendis Maiis, Carthagine in secretario Basilicae Fausti. Cum Aurelius episcopus universali concilio praesideret, diaconibus assistentibus, placuit omnibus episcopis, quorum nomina et subscriptiones indicantur, in sancta synodo Ecclesiae Carthaginensis congregatis definire—"],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p501","CANON CIX. (Greek cxii. continued.)","That Adam was not created by God subject to death.","Ut Adam non sit a Deo creatus morti obnoxius."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p502","CANON CIX. (Greek cxii. continued.)","THAT whosoever says that Adam, the first man, was created mortal, so that whether he had sinned or not, he would have died in body—that is, he would have gone forth of the body, not because his sin merited this, but by natural necessity, let him be anathema.","Ut quicumque dicit quod Adam, primus homo, mortalis creatus sit, ita ut sive peccasset sive non peccasset, corpore moreretur—id est, de corpore exiret, non quia peccatum eius hoc meruit, sed naturali necessitate, anathema sit."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p503","Ancient Epitome of Canon CIX.","Whoso shall assert that the protoplast would have died without sin and through natural necessity, let him be anathema.","Quicumque asseruerit protoplastum sine peccato et per naturalem necessitatem fuisse moriturum, anathema sit."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p504","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CIX.","Canon CVIII. is the introduction to the Synod of Carthage of May 1, A.D. 418; and Canon CIX. is Canon j. of that synod.","Canon CVIII. est introductio ad Synodum Carthaginensem, Kalendis Maiis, anno 418; et Canon CIX. est Canon I. illius synodi."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p505","CANON CX. (Greek cxii. bis)","That infants are baptized for the remission of sins.","Ut infantes baptizentur in remissionem peccatorum."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p506","CANON CX. (Greek cxii. bis)","LIKEWISE it seemed good that whosoever denies that infants newly from their mother's wombs should be baptized, or says that baptism is for remission of sins, but that they derive from Adam no original sin, which needs to be removed by the laver of regeneration, from whence the conclusion follows, that in them the form of baptism for the remission of sins, is to be understood as false and not true, let him be anathema. For no otherwise can be understood what the Apostle says, \"By one man sin is come into the world, and death through sin, and so death passed upon all men in that all have sinned,\" than the Catholic Church everywhere diffused has always understood it. For on account of this rule of faith even infants, who could have committed as yet no sin themselves, therefore are truly baptized for the remission of sins, in order that what in them is the result of generation may be cleansed by regeneration.","Item placuit ut quicumque negat infantes recentes ab uteris matrum baptizandos esse, aut dicit baptismum in remissionem peccatorum esse, sed nihil ex Adam trahere originalis peccati, quod regenerationis lavacro expiandum sit, unde sequitur ut in eis forma baptismatis in remissionem peccatorum non vera sed falsa intelligatur, anathema sit. Non enim aliter intelligendum est quod Apostolus dicit: \"Per unum hominem peccatum intravit in mundum, et per peccatum mors, et ita in omnes homines mors pertransiit, in quo omnes peccaverunt,\" nisi quemadmodum Catholica Ecclesia ubique diffusa semper intellexit. Propter hanc enim regulam fidei et infantes, qui nullum peccatum ipsi committere potuerunt, ideo in remissionem peccatorum veraciter baptizantur, ut in eis per regenerationem mundetur quod generatione traxerunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p507","Ancient Epitome of Canon CX.","Whoso affirms that those newly born and baptized contract nothing from Adam's transgression, which needs to be washed away by baptism, is to be execrated: for through one both death and sin invaded the whole world.","Quicumque affirmat eos qui recenter nati et baptizati sunt nihil ex Adae praevaricatione contrahere, quod ablui debeat baptismo, execrandus est: per unum enim et mors et peccatum in totum mundum invasit."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p508","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CX.","This is Canon ij. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon II. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p509","CANON CXI. (Greek cxiii.)","That the grace of God not only gives remission of sins, but also affords aid that we sin no more.","Ut Dei gratia non solum remissionem peccatorum tribuit, sed etiam auxilium praestat ne amplius peccemus."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p510","CANON CXI. (Greek cxiii.)","LIKEWISE it seemed good, that whoever should say that the grace of God, by which a man is justified through Jesus Christ our Lord, avails only for the remission of past sins, and not for assistance against committing sins in the future, let him be anathema.","Item placuit, ut quicumque dixerit gratiam Dei, qua iustificatur homo per Iesum Christum Dominum nostrum, tantum prodesse ad remissionem praeteritorum peccatorum, et non ad adiutorium contra futura peccata, anathema sit."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p511","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXI.","Whoever is of opinion that the grace of God only gives remission of those sins we have already committed, and does not afford aid against sin in the future, is to be twice execrated.","Quicumque opinatur gratiam Dei tantum dare remissionem peccatorum quae iam commisimus, et non praestare adiutorium contra peccatum in futurum, est execrandus."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p512","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXI.","This is Canon iij. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon III. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p513","CANON CXII. (Greek cxiii. continued.)","That the grace of Christ gives not only the knowledge of our duty, but also inspires us with a desire that we may be able to accomplish what we know.","Ut gratia Christi non solum scientiam officii nostri tribuit, sed etiam inspirat nobis desiderium ut possimus efficere quod scimus."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p514","CANON CXII. (Greek cxiii. continued.)","ALSO, whoever shall say that the same grace of God through Jesus Christ our Lord helps us only in not sinning by revealing to us and opening to our understanding the commandments, so that we may know what to seek, what we ought to avoid, and also that we should love to do so, but that through it we are not helped so that we are able to do what we know we should do, let him be anathema. For when the Apostle says: \"Wisdom puffeth up, but charity edifieth\" it were truly infamous were we to believe that we have the grace of Christ for that which puffeth us up, but have it not for that which edifieth, since in each case it is the gift of God, both to know what we ought to do, and to love to do it; so that wisdom cannot puff us up while charity is edifying us. For as of God it is written, \"Who teacheth man knowledge,\" so also it is written, \"Love is of God.\"","Item, quicumque dixerit eandem gratiam Dei per Iesum Christum Dominum nostrum tantum adiuvare nos ad non peccandum, revelando nobis et aperiendo intelligentiae mandata, ut sciamus quid appetere, quid vitare debeamus, et quod id diligamus, sed per eam non adiuvari ut possimus facere quod nosse debemus, anathema sit. Cum enim Apostolus dicat: \"Scientia inflat, caritas vero aedificat,\" valde infame esset ut crederemus nos habere gratiam Christi ad id quod inflat, sed non ad id quod aedificat, cum in utroque donum Dei sit, et scire quid facere debeamus, et diligere ut faciamus; ut scientia non possit inflare nos, caritate aedificante. Sicut enim de Deo scriptum est: \"Qui docet hominem scientiam,\" ita etiam scriptum est: \"Caritas ex Deo est.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p515","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXII.","Whoever says that the grace of God is given to us only that we may know what we ought to do and what to flee from, but not also that we may love the thing known, and be able to accomplish it, let him be anathema.","Quicumque dicit gratiam Dei dari nobis tantum ut sciamus quid facere debeamus et quid vitare, sed non et ut amemus cognitum, et possimus efficere, anathema sit."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p516","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXII.","This is Canon iv. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon IV. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p517","CANON CXIII. (Greek cxiiii.)","That without the grace of God we can do no good thing.","Ut sine Dei gratia nihil boni facere possimus."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p518","CANON CXIII. (Greek cxiiii.)","IT seemed good that whosoever should say that the grace of justification was given to us only that we might be able more readily by grace to perform what we were ordered to do through our free will; as if though grace was not given, although not easily, yet nevertheless we could even without grace fulfil the divine commandments, let him be anathema. For the Lord spake concerning the fruits of the commandments, when he said: \"Without me ye can do nothing,\" and not \"Without me ye could do it but with difficulty.\"","Placuit ut quicumque dixerit gratiam iustificationis ideo datam esse nobis ut quod per liberum arbitrium iuberemur facere, facilius per gratiam possemus implere; quasi etsi gratia non daretur, non quidem facile, sed tamen possemus etiam sine gratia divina mandata implere, anathema sit. De fructibus enim mandatorum Dominus loquebatur, cum diceret: \"Sine me nihil potestis facere,\" non \"Sine me difficulter potestis.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p519","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXIII.","Whoso preaches that without grace we could keep the commandments although with difficulty, is to be thrice execrated. For the Lord says, \"Without me ye can do nothing.\"","Quicumque praedicat quod sine gratia possemus servare mandata etiam cum difficultate, ter execrandus est. Dominus enim dicit: \"Sine me nihil potestis facere.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p520","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXIII.","This is Canon V. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon V. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p521","CANON CXIV. (Greek cxv.)","That not only humble but also true is that voice of the Saints: \"If we say that we have no sin we deceive ourselves.\"","Ut non solum humilis sed etiam vera sit illa vox Sanctorum: \"Si dixerimus quia peccatum non habemus, nos ipsos decipimus.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p522","CANON CXIV. (Greek cxv.)","IT also seemed good that as St. John the Apostle says, \"If we shall say that we have no sin we deceive ourselves and the truth is not in us,\" whosoever thinks that this should be so understood as to mean that out of humility, we ought to say that we have sin, and not because it is really so, let him be anathema. For the Apostle goes on to add, \"But if we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins and to cleanse us from all iniquity,\" where it is sufficiently clear that this is said not only of humility but also truly. For the Apostle might have said, \"If we shall say we have no sins we shall extoll ourselves, and humility shall have no place in us;\" but when he says, \"we deceive ourselves and the truth is not in us\" he sufficiently intimates that he who affirmed that he had no sin would speak not that which is true but that which is false.","Item placuit ut sicut beatus Ioannes Apostolus dicit: \"Si dixerimus quia peccatum non habemus, nos ipsos decipimus et veritas in nobis non est,\" quicumque hoc ita intellegendum putat ut humilitatis causa nos dicere oporteat peccatum nos habere, non quia vere ita est, anathema sit. Subiungit enim Apostolus: \"Si autem confiteamur peccata nostra, fidelis est et iustus ut dimittat nobis peccata nostra et emundet nos ab omni iniquitate,\" ubi satis apparet hoc non solum humiliter sed etiam veraciter dici. Poterat enim Apostolus dicere: \"Si dixerimus quia peccata non habemus, extollemur, et humilitas locum in nobis non habebit;\" cum vero dicit: \"nos ipsos decipimus et veritas in nobis non est,\" satis insinuat eum qui diceret se peccatum non habere non verum sed falsum dicere."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p523","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXIV.","Whosoever shall interpret the saying of the Divine [i.e. St. John]: \"If we shall say that we have no sin, we deceive ourselves\" as not being really true but as spoken out of humility, let him be anathema.","Quicumque interpretatus fuerit sermonem Divini [id est S. Ioannis]: \"Si dixerimus quia peccatum non habemus, nos ipsos decipimus\" non vere esse verum sed humiliter dictum, anathema sit."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p524","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXIV.","This is Canon vj. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon VI. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p525","CANON CXV. (Greek cxvi.)","That in the Lord's Prayer the Saints say for themselves: \"Forgive us our trespasses.\"","Ut in Oratione Dominica Sancti pro se dicant: \"Dimitte nobis debita nostra.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p526","CANON CXV. (Greek cxvi.)","IT has seemed good that whoever should say that when in the Lord's prayer, the saints say, \"forgive us our trespasses,\" they say this not for themselves, because they have no need of this petition, but for the rest who are sinners of the people; and that therefore no one of the saints can say, \"Forgive me my trespasses,\" but \"Forgive us our trespasses;\" so that the just is understood to seek this for others rather than for himself; let him be anathema. For holy and just was the Apostle James, when he said, \"For in many things we offend all.\" For why was it added \"all,\" unless that this sentence might agree also with the psalm, where we read, \"Enter not into judgment with thy servant, O Lord, for in thy sight shall no man living be justified;\" and in the prayer of the most wise Solomon: \"There is no man that sinneth not;\" and in the book of the holy Job: \"He sealeth in the hand of every man, that every man may know his own infirmity;\" wherefore even the holy and just Daniel when in prayer said several times: \"We have sinned, we have done iniquity,\" and other things which there truly and humbly he confessed; nor let it be thought (as some have thought) that this was said not of his own but rather of the people's sins, for he said further on: \"When I shall pray and confess my sins and the sins of my people to the Lord my God;\" he did not wish to say our sins, but he said the sins of his people and his own sins, since he as a prophet foresaw that those who were to come would thus misunderstand his words.","Placuit ut quicumque dixerit quod in Oratione Dominica sancti cum dicunt: \"Dimitte nobis debita nostra,\" non pro se dicant, quia non iam necesse est hanc petitionem, sed pro ceteris qui peccatores sunt in populo; et ideo neminem sanctorum posse dicere: \"Dimitte mihi debita mea,\" sed \"Dimitte nobis debita nostra;\" ut iustus pro aliis magis hoc petere intellegatur quam pro se; anathema sit. Sanctus enim et iustus erat Iacobus Apostolus cum diceret: \"In multis enim offendimus omnes.\" Cur autem additum est \"omnes,\" nisi ut haec sententia etiam cum psalmo concordet, ubi legimus: \"Ne intres in iudicium cum servo tuo, Domine, quia non iustificabitur in conspectu tuo omnis vivens;\" et in oratione sapientissimi Salomonis: \"Non est homo qui non peccat;\" et in libro sancti Iob: \"Signavit in manu omnium hominum, ut sciat omnis homo infirmitatem suam;\" unde et sanctus et iustus Daniel in oratione dicebat pluries: \"Peccavimus, inique egimus,\" et cetera quae ibi veraciter et humiliter confitebatur; nec putandum est (sicut quidam putaverunt) quod hoc non de suis sed potius de populi peccatis dixerit; dixit enim infra: \"Cum orarem et confiterer peccata mea et peccata populi mei Domino Deo meo;\" noluit dicere peccata nostra, sed dixit peccata populi sui et peccata sua, quia propheta futurus praevidebat eos qui postea venturi essent sic verba eius male intellecturos."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p527","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXV.","Whoso expounds this, \"forgive us our trespasses\" as speaking only of the multitude and not of individuals let him be anathema: Since Daniel even he can behold saying with the multitude \"I confessed my sins and the sins of my people.\"","Quicumque exponit illud, \"dimitte nobis debita nostra\" quasi de multitudine tantum dicatur et non de singulis, anathema sit: Cum Daniel etiam ipse cernatur dicens cum multitudine \"Confitebar peccata mea et peccata populi mei.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p528","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXV.","This is Canon vij. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon VII. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p529","CANON CXVI. (Greek cxvii.)","That the Saints say with accuracy, \"Forgive us our trespasses.\"","Ut Sancti veraciter dicant, \"Dimitte nobis debita nostra.\""],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p530","CANON CXVI. (Greek cxvii.)","LIKEWISE also it seemed good, that whoever wished that these words of the Lord's prayer, when we say, \"Forgive us our trespasses\" are said by the saints out of humility and not in truth let them be anathema. For who would make a lying prayer, not to men but to God? Who would say with his lips that he wished his sins forgiven him, but in his heart that he had no sins to be forgiven.","Item placuit, ut quicumque voluerit haec verba Dominicae orationis, cum dicimus, \"Dimitte nobis debita nostra,\" a sanctis humiliter dici et non veraciter, anathema sint. Quis enim faceret orationem mendacem, non hominibus sed Deo? Quis diceret labiis velle sibi dimitti peccata, corde autem se peccata non habere quae dimitterentur?"],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p531","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXVI.","(Lacking.)","(Deest.)"],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p532","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXVI.","This is Canon viij. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon VIII. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p533","CANON CXVII. (Greek cxviii.)","Of peoples converted from the Donatists.","De plebibus a Donatistis conversis."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p534","CANON CXVII. (Greek cxviii.)","ITEM, it seemed good, since it was so decreed some years ago by a plenary council, that whatever churches were erected in a diocese before the laws were made concerning Donatists when they became Catholic, should pertain to the sees of those bishops through whom their return to Catholic unity was brought about; but after the laws whatever churches communicated were to belong there where they belonged when they were Donatists. But because many controversies afterward arose and are still springing up between bishops concerning dioceses, which were not then at all in prospect, now it has seemed good to this council, that wherever there was a Catholic and a Donatist party, pertaining to different sees, at whatever time unity has been or shall be made, whether before or after the laws, the churches shall belong to that see to which the Catholic church which was already there belonged.","Item placuit, cum iam ante aliquot annos in plenario concilio decretum sit, ut quaecumque ecclesiae in dioecesi constitutae ante leges de Donatistis factas, cum Catholicae factae sunt, ad sedes episcoporum pertineant per quorum operam in Catholicam unitatem redierunt; post leges autem, quaecumque ecclesiae communicaverunt, ibi pertineant ubi cum Donatistae essent pertinebant. Sed quia multae controversiae postea ortae sunt et adhuc oriuntur inter episcopos de dioecesibus, quae tunc minime prospectae sunt, nunc placuit huic concilio ut ubicunque Catholica et Donatistica pars, ad diversas sedes pertinentes, quocumque tempore unitas facta est vel fiet, sive ante sive post leges, ecclesiae ad eam sedem pertineant ad quam Catholica ecclesia quae iam ibi erat pertinebat."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p535","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXVII.","Whenever conversions and unions of Donatists are effected, let them be subject to that throne to which the Catholic Church which was formerly there was subject.","Ubicunque conversiones et unitates Donatistarum factae sunt, subiaceant illi cathedrae cui Catholica ecclesia quae prius ibi erat subiacebat."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p536","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXVII.","This is Canon ix. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon IX. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p537","CANON CXVIII. (Greek cxix.)","How bishops as well Catholic as those who have been converted from the Donatists are to divide between themselves the dioceses.","Quomodo episcopi tam Catholici quam qui a Donatistis conversi sunt dividant inter se dioeceses."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p538","CANON CXVIII. (Greek cxix.)","SO, too, it has seemed good that if a bishop has been converted from the Donatists to Catholic unity, that equally there should be divided what shall have been so found where there were two parties; that is, that some places should pertain to one and some to the other; and let the division be made by him who has been the longest time in the episcopate, and let the younger choose. But should there be only one place let it belong to him who is found to be the nearer. But should the distance be equal to each of the two cathedrals let it belong to the one the people may choose. But should the old Catholics wish their own bishop, and if the same be the case with the converted Donatists, let the will of the greater number prevail, but should the parties be equal, let it belong to him who has been longest bishop. But if so many places be found in which there were both parties, that an equal division is impossible, as for example, if they are unequal in number, after those places have been distributed which have an equal number, the place that remains over shall be disposed of as is provided above in the case where there is but one place to be treated.","Item placuit ut si episcopus a Donatistis ad Catholicam unitatem conversus fuerit, aequaliter dividatur quod sic inventum fuerit ubi duae partes erant; id est, ut alia loca ad unum pertineant, alia ad alterum; et divisio fiat ab eo qui diutius in episcopatu fuit, et minor eligat. Quod si unum tantum locum fuerit, pertineat ad eum qui propinquior inventus fuerit. Quod si aequaliter distet ab utraque cathedrali, pertineat ad eum quem populus elegerit. Quod si veteres Catholici suum episcopum voluerint, et similiter conversi Donatistae, maioris numeri voluntas praevaleat; quod si partes aequales fuerint, pertineat ad eum qui diutius episcopus fuit. Quod si tot loca inventa fuerint in quibus utraeque partes erant ut aequalis divisio fieri non potest, verbi gratia, si inaequalia sunt numero, distributis primum quae aequalem numerum habent, locus qui superaverit disponatur sicut supra dictum est in casu ubi unus locus tractandus est."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p539","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXVIII.","Those who have been converted from Donatus, let them divide the dioceses; and let the senior bishop make the division, and the junior choose which he will.","Qui a Donato conversi sunt, dividant dioeceses; et senior episcopus faciat divisionem, et iunior eligat quam voluerit."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p540","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXVIII.","This is Canon x. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon X. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p541","CANON CXIX. (Greek cxx.)","That if a bishop shall possess a diocese which he has snatched from heresy for three years, no one may take it from him.","Ut si episcopus possederit dioecesim quam ab haeresi eripuit per tres annos, nullus eam ei auferat."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p542","CANON CXIX. (Greek cxx.)","ITEM, it seemed good that if anyone after the laws should convert any place to Catholic unity and retain it for three years without opposition, it should not be taken away from him afterwards. If however there was during those three years a bishop who could claim it and was silent, he shall lose the opportunity. But if there was no bishop, no prejudice shall happen to the see, but it shall be lawful when the place that had none shall receive a bishop, to make the claim within three years of that day. Item, if a Donatist bishop shall be converted to the Catholic party, the time that has elapsed shall not count against him, but from the day of his conversion for three years he shall have the right of making a claim on the places which belonged to his See.","Item placuit ut si quis post leges converterit aliquem locum ad Catholicam unitatem et per tres annos sine contradictione retinuerit, non ei postea auferatur. Si autem fuit per illos tres annos episcopus qui eum vindicare posset et siluit, perdat occasionem. Si autem non fuit episcopus, nulla fiat sedi praeiudicium, sed liceat, cum locus qui nullum habuit episcopum receperit, intra tres annos eius diei vindicare. Item, si episcopus Donatista ad Catholicam partem conversus fuerit, tempus quod praeteriit non ei noceat, sed a die conversionis eius per tres annos ius habeat vindicandi loca quae ad Sedem eius pertinebant."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p543","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXIX.","Whosoever shall convert a region to Orthodoxy and shall keep it converted for three years, let him be without blame. But if the bishop converted from Donatus within three years of its conversion seeks his diocese again, let it be returned to him.","Quicumque converterit regionem ad Orthodoxiam et conversam per tres annos retinuerit, sine vituperio sit. Si autem episcopus ex Donato conversus intra tres annos conversionis eius suam dioecesim repetierit, reddatur ei."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p544","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXIX.","This is Canon xj. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon XI. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p545","CANON CXX. (Greek cxxi.)","Of those who intrude upon peoples which they think belong to them, without the consent of those by whom they are held.","De his qui plebes quas ad se pertinere existimant, sine consensu eorum a quibus tenentur, invadunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p546","CANON CXX. (Greek cxxi.)","ITEM, it seemed good that whatever bishops seek the peoples whom they consider to pertain to their see, not by bringing their causes before the episcopal judges, but rush in while another is holding the place, all such, (whether said people are willing to receive them or no) shall lose their case. And whoever have done this, if the contention between the two bishops is not yet finished but still going on, let him depart who intruded without the decree of the ecclesiastical judges; nor let anyone flatter himself that he will retain [what he has seized] if he shall obtain letters from the primate, but whether he has such letters or has them not, it is suitable that he who holds and receives his letters should make it appear then that he has held the church pertaining to him peaceably. But if he has referred any question, let the cause be decided by the episcopal judges, whether those whom the primates have appointed for them, or the neighbouring bishops whom they have chosen by common consent.","Item placuit ut quicumque episcopi plebes quas ad suam sedem pertinere existimant, non per causarum discussionem apud episcopales iudices, sed alio tenente loca irruunt, omnes tales (sive plebs illos recipere velit sive nolit) causam perdant. Et quicumque hoc fecerunt, si adhuc contentio inter duos episcopos non finita sed adhuc pendet, discedat ille qui sine decretis ecclesiasticorum iudicum invasit; nec se quisquam blandiatur quod retinebit, si a primate litteras acceperit; sed sive tales litteras habeat sive non habeat, oportet ut qui tenet et litteras suas accipiat, tunc ostendat se tenuisse ecclesiam ad se pertinentem pacifice. Quod si aliquam quaestionem detulerit, causa a iudicibus episcopalibus terminetur, sive ab his quos primates eis dederint, sive a vicinis episcopis quos communi consensu elegerint."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p547","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXX.","Let no one seize for himself what he thinks belongs to him: but let the bishops judge or whom the Primate will give, or whom the neighbouring bishops shall give with his consent. But whosoever has received letters from the primate concerning the keeping [of such regions and churches] merely deceives himself.","Nemo sibi vindicet quod ad se putat pertinere: sed iudicent episcopi vel quos Primas dederit, vel quos vicini episcopi cum eius consensu dederint. Quicumque autem litteras a primate de retinendis [tali regionibus et ecclesiis] acceperit, ipse se decipit."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p548","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXX.","This is Canon xij. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon XII. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p549","CANON CXXI. (Greek cxxii.)","Of those who neglect the peoples belonging to them.","De his qui negligunt plebes ad se pertinentes."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p550","CANON CXXI. (Greek cxxii.)","ITEM, it seemed good that whoever neglect to bring the places belonging to their see into Catholic unity should be admonished by the neighbouring diligent bishops, that they delay no longer to do this; but if within six months from the day of the convention they do nothing, let them pertain to him who can win them: but with this proviso however, that if he to whom it seemed they naturally belonged can prove that this neglect was intentional and more efficacious in winning them than the greater apparent diligence of others; when the episcopal judges shall be convinced that this is the case, they shall restore the places to his see. If the bishops between whom the cause lies are of different provinces, let the Primate in whose province the place is situated about which there is the dispute, appoint judges; but if by mutual consent they have chosen as judges the neighbouring bishops, let one or three be chosen: so that if they choose three they may follow the sentence of all or of two.","Item placuit ut quicumque loca ad sedem suam pertinentia negligunt ad Catholicam unitatem adducere, ab episcopis vicinis diligentioribus admoneantur ut hoc facere non differant; si autem intra sex menses a die conventionis nihil egerint, pertineant ad eum qui potuerit ea lucrari: ita tamen ut si ille ad quem naturaliter pertinere videbantur probare potuerit hanc neglegentiam voluntariam et plus in lucrandis eis profuisse quam aliorum maior apparens diligentia; cum episcopales iudices hoc ita esse convicerint, restituant loca sedi eius. Si episcopi inter quos causa est diversarum provinciarum sunt, Primas in cuius provincia locus de quo controversia est situs est, iudices det; si autem communi consensu vicinos episcopos iudices elegerint, unus vel tres eligantur: ut si tres elegerint, sententiam omnium vel duorum sequantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p551","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXI.","If any neglect what belongs to their jurisdiction, let them be admonished; and if they shall do nothing within a six month, let them be adjudged to him who can win them. But if they have committed the neglect out of policy so as not to irritate the heretics, and this shall appear to have been the case, their sees shall be restored to them, by the judgment of the bishops either appointed or elected.","Si aliqui negligunt quae ad suam iurisdictionem pertinent, admoneantur; et si intra sex menses nihil egerint, adjudicentur ei qui potuerit ea lucrari. Quod si ex consilio neglexerunt ut haereticos non irritarent, et hoc apparuerit, sedes eis restituantur iudicio episcoporum sive datorum sive electorum."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p552","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXXI.","This is Canon xiij. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon XIII. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p553","CANON CXXII. (Greek cxxiii.)","The sentence of the elected judges ought not to be spurned.","Sententia electorum iudicum non est spernenda."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p554","CANON CXXII. (Greek cxxiii.)","FROM the judges chosen by common consent of the parties, no appeal can be taken; and whoever shall be found to have carried such an appeal and contumaciously to be unwilling to submit to the judges, when this has been proved to the primate, let him give letters, that no one of the bishops should communicate with him until he yield.","A iudicibus communi consensu partium electis, appellari non potest; et quicumque inventus fuerit talem appellationem interposuisse et contumaciter nolle iudicibus subiacere, cum hoc primati fuerit probatum, det litteras ut nemo episcoporum cum eo communicet donec cedat."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p555","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXII.","A judge chosen by both parties cannot be repudiated.","Iudex ab utraque parte electus repudiari non potest."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p556","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXXII.","This is Canon xiv. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon XIV. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p557","CANON CXXIII. (Greek cxxiv.)","That if a bishop neglects his diocese he is to be deprived of communion.","Ut si episcopus dioecesim suam neglexerit, communione privetur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p558","CANON CXXIII. (Greek cxxiv.)","IF in the mother cathedrals a bishop should have been negligent against the heretics, let a meeting be held of the neighbouring diligent bishops, and let his negligence be pointed out to him, so that he can have no excuse. But if within six months after this meeting, if an execution was in his own province, and he had taken no care to convert them to Catholic unity, no one shall communicate with him till he does his duty. But if no executor shall have come to the places, then the fault shall not be laid to the bishop.","Si in matricibus cathedralibus episcopus contra haereticos neglegens fuerit, conveniant vicini episcopi diligentiores, et ostendatur ei neglegentia eius, ut excusationem non habeat. Quod si intra sex menses post hanc conventionem, si executor in provincia sua fuit, et non curaverit eos ad Catholicam unitatem convertere, nemo cum eo communicet donec satisfaciat. Si autem executor ad loca non venerit, tunc episcopo vitium non imputetur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p559","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXIII.","A bishop who spurns the care of heretics, and if after being warned he continues for six months in his contempt, and has no care for their conversion, is to be excommunicated.","Episcopus qui curam haereticorum spernit, et si post admonitionem per sex menses in contemptu perseverat, nec curam habet eorum conversionis, excommunicetur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p560","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXXIII.","This is Canon xv. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon XV. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p561","CANON CXXIV. (Greek cxxv.)","Of bishops who shall lie with regard to Donatists' communions.","De episcopis qui mentientur de communione Donatistarum."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p562","CANON CXXIV. (Greek cxxv.)","IF it shall be proven that any bishop has lied concerning the communion of those [who had been Donatists], and had said that they had communicated when he knew it was an established fact that they had not done so, let him lose his bishoprick.","Si probatum fuerit aliquem episcopum mentitum esse de communione illorum [qui Donatistae fuerant], et dixisse eos communicasse cum sciret firmum esse quod non fecerint, episcopatu suo privetur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p563","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXIV.","Whoso says that a man, whom he knows does not communicate, does communicate is to be deprived of his episcopate.","Quicumque dicit hominem, quem scit non communicare, communicare, episcopatu privetur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p564","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXXIV.","This is Canon xvj. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon XVI. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p565","CANON CXXV. (Greek cxxvi.)","That presbyters and clerics are not to appeal except to African Synods.","Ut presbyteri et clerici non appellent nisi ad Africanas Synodos."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p566","CANON CXXV. (Greek cxxvi.)","ITEM, it seemed good that presbyters, deacons, or other of the lower clergy who are to be tried, if they question the decision of their bishops, the neighbouring bishops having been invited by them with the consent of their bishops, shall hear them and determine whatever separates them. But should they think an appeal should be carried from them, let them not carry the appeal except to African councils or to the primates of their provinces. But whoso shall think of carrying an appeal across seas he shall be admitted to communion by no one in Africa.","Item placuit ut presbyteri, diacones, vel ceteri inferioris gradus clerici qui iudicandi sunt, si de sententia episcoporum suorum quaestionem moverint, vicinis episcopis ab eis cum consensu episcoporum suorum rogatis, audiant eos et quidquid eos separat definiant. Quod si ab his putaverint appellandum, non ad transmarina iudicia appellent, sed ad Africana concilia vel ad primatum provinciarum suarum. Quicumque autem trans mare appellandum putaverit, in Africa a nullo ad communionem admittatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p567","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXV.","A presbyter and deacons, who has been condemned by his own bishop, let him appeal to the neighbouring bishops: but let them not cross the sea. In Africa they shall be excommunicated.","Presbyter et diacones qui damnatus est ab episcopo suo, appellent ad vicinos episcopos: sed non trans mare. In Africa excommunicentur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p568","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXXV.","This is Canon xvij. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon XVII. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p569","CANON CXXVI. (Greek cxxvii.)","That Virgins, even when minors, should be given the veil.","Ut Virgines, etiam minores, velentur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p570","CANON CXXVI. (Greek cxxvii.)","ITEM, it seemed good that whatever bishop, by the necessity of the dangers of virginal purity, when either a powerful suitor or some ravisher is feared, or if she shall be pricked with some scruple of death that she might die unveiled, at the demand either of her parents or of those to whose care she has been entrusted, shall give the veil to a virgin, or shall have given it while she was under twenty-five years of age, the council which has appointed that number of years shall not oppose him.","Item placuit ut quicumque episcopus, necessitate periculorum virginalis pudicitiae, cum aut potens sponsus aut raptor aliquis timetur, aut si aliqua morte urgetur ne velata non moriatur, rogantibus sive parentibus sive eis ad quorum curam pertinet, virginem velare voluerit aut celaverit infra viginti quinque annos aetatis, concilium quod illum numerum annorum constituit non ei obsistat."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p571","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXVI.","Whosoever has veiled or shall veil a virgin before she is twenty-five years of age (that is give her the habit, or clothe her), being forced thereto on account of a powerful lover, or a ravisher, or deadly disease, provided those who have the charge of her so exhort, shall receive no damage from the synod concerning that age.","Quicumque velavit vel celaverit virginem ante viginti quinque annos aetatis (id est, dederit ei habitum seu vestem), coactus propter potentem amatorem vel raptorem vel morbum mortiferum, dummodo qui curam eius habent suadeant, nihil damni a synodo de illa aetate patiatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p572","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXXVI.","This is Canon xviij. of Carthage, A.D. 418. The reference to a former canon is to Canon j. of the second series of the canons of the Synod of Hippo in A.D. 393.","Hic est Canon XVIII. Carthaginensis, anno 418. Relatio ad priorem canonem est ad Canonem I. secundae seriei canonum Synodi Hipponensis anno 393."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p573","CANON CXXVII. (Greek cxxviii.)","That bishops be not detained too long in council, let them choose three judges from themselves of the singular provinces.","Ut episcopi non diutius in concilio teneantur, eligant tres iudices de singulis provinciis."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p574","CANON CXXVII. (Greek cxxviii.)","ITEM, it seemed good, lest all the bishops who are assembled at a council be kept too long, that the whole synod should choose three judges of the several provinces; and they elected for the province of Carthage Vincent, Fortunatian, and Clarus; for the province of Numidia Alypius, Augustine, and Restitutus; for the province of Byzacena, with the holy Senex Donatian the Primate, Cresconius, Jocundus, and Æmilian; for Mauritania Sitephensis Severian, Asiaticus, and Donatus; for the Tripolitan province Plautius, who alone was sent as legate according to custom; all these were to take cognizance of all things with the holy senex Aurelius, from whom the whole council sought that he should subscribe all things done by the council whether acts or letters. And they subscribed: I, Aurelius, bishop of the church of Carthage consent to this decree and having read it sign my name. Likewise also signed they all.","Item placuit, ne omnes episcopi qui in concilio congregantur diutius teneantur, ut tota synodus tres iudices de singulis provinciis eligat; et elegerunt pro provincia Carthaginensi Vincentium, Fortunatianum et Clarum; pro provincia Numidiae Alypium, Augustinum et Restitutum; pro provincia Byzacena cum sancto sene Donatiano primate, Cresconium, Iocundum et Aemilianum; pro Mauritania Sitephensi Severianum, Asiaticum et Donatum; pro Tripolitana provincia Plautium, qui solus secundum consuetudinem legatus missus est; hi omnes cum sancto sene Aurelio omnia cognoscerent, a quo tota synodus petivit ut omnia quae a concilio gesta sunt sive acta sive epistolae subscriberet. Et subscriptionibus confirmaverunt: Ego, Aurelius, episcopus ecclesiae Carthaginensis, huic decreto consentio et legi et nomen meum subscripsi. Similiter et omnes subscripserunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p575","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXVII.","Whenever the bishops who come to synod can remain no longer in attendance, let three be chosen from each province.","Quotienscumque episcopi qui ad synodum veniunt non possunt diutius interesse, tres de unaquaque provincia eligantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p576","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXXVII.","This is Canon xix. of Carthage, A.D. 418.","Hic est Canon XIX. Carthaginensis, anno 418."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p577","CANON CXXVIII. (Greek cxxix.)","That those out of communion should not be allowed to bring accusation.","Ut hi qui extra communionem sunt non admittantur ad accusationem."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p578","CANON CXXVIII. (Greek cxxix.)","IT seemed good to all, as it had been decreed by the former councils, concerning what persons were to be admitted to bring accusations against clerics; and since it had not been expressed what persons should not be admitted, therefore we define, that he cannot properly be admitted to bring an accusation, who had been already excommunicated, and was still lying under that censure, whether he that wished to be the accuser were cleric or layman.","Placuit omnibus, sicut prioribus conciliis decretum est, de quibus personis admittendis ad accusationem clericorum; et quia non fuerat expressum quae personae non admittendae essent, ideo definimus, non posse proprie admitti ad accusationem, qui iam excommunicatus fuerit, et adhuc sub illa censura iacet, sive clericus sive laicus sit qui accusator esse voluerit."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p579","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXVIII.","One excommunicated is not to give witness.","Excommunicatus non est testis."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p580","Epitome Antiqua Canonis CXXVIII.","The Council of Carthage of 419 had at its first session on May 25th done thus much. But when it met again on the 30th of the same month, it continued the code. The introduction in regard to this new session is this introduction. The Canons then enacted were original, viz. numbers 128, 129, 130, 131, 132 and 133.","Concilium Carthaginense anni 419 in prima sessione sua V. Kalendas Iunias haec egit. Cum autem iterum convenit III. Kalendas Iunias eiusdem mensis, codicem continuavit. Introductio de hac nova sessione haec introductio est. Canones tunc constituti originales erant, scilicet numeri 128, 129, 130, 131, 132 et 133."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p581","CANON CXXIX. (Greek cxxx.)","That slaves and freedmen and all infamous persons ought not to bring accusation.","Ut servi et liberti et omnes infames personae accusationem non debeant inferre."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p582","CANON CXXIX. (Greek cxxx.)","TO all it seemed good that no slaves or freedmen, properly so called, be admitted to accusation nor any of those who by the public laws are debarred from bringing accusation in criminal proceedings. This also is the case with all those who have the stain of infamy, that is actors, and persons subject to turpitudes, also heretics, or heathen, or Jews; but even all those to whom the right of bringing accusation is denied, are not forbidden to bring accusation in their own suits.","Omnibus placuit ut nulli servi vel liberti, proprie sic dicti, admittantur ad accusationem nec ulli ex his qui legibus publicis in causis criminalibus accusationem inferre prohibentur. Hoc etiam de omnibus qui notam infamiae habent, id est histrionibus, et personis turpitudinibus subiectis, item haereticis, vel paganis, vel Iudaeis; sed et omnes illi quibus ius accusationis negatur, non prohibentur in suis causis accusationem inferre."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p583","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXIX.","A slave, and a freedman, and he who before was accused of any of these crimes on account of which he is not admitted in court, and a player, and a heathen, and a heretic, and a Jew [are not to give witness].","Servus, et libertus, et qui antea de aliquo horum criminum accusatus fuit propter quae in foro non admittitur, et histrio, et paganus, et haereticus, et Iudaeus [testes non sint]."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p584","CANON CXXX. (Greek cxxxi.)","That he who has failed to prove one charge shall not be allowed to give evidence to another.","Ut qui unum crimen probare non potuit, ad aliud testimonium dicere non permittatur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p585","CANON CXXX. (Greek cxxxi.)","SO, too, it seemed good that as often as many crimes were laid to clerics by their accusers, and one of the first examined could not be proved, they should not be allowed to go on giving evidence on the other counts.","Item placuit ut quotiens multa crimina ab accusatoribus clericis obiecta fuerint, et unum de primo examinatum probari non potuerit, non permittantur in aliis capitibus testimonium dicere."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p586","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXX.","He who makes many accusations and proves nothing [is not to give witness].","Qui multa accusat et nihil probat [testis non sit]."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p587","CANON CXXXI. (Greek CXXXII.)","Who should be allowed to give evidence.","Qui admittendi sint ad testimonium."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p588","CANON CXXXI. (Greek CXXXII.)","THEY who are forbidden to be admitted as accusers are not to be allowed to appear as witnesses, nor any that the accuser may bring from his own household. And none shall be admitted to give witness under fourteen years of age.","Qui prohibentur admitti ut accusatores, non admittantur ut testes, nec ulli quos accusator de domo sua produxerit. Et nemo infra quattuordecim annos aetatis admittatur ad testimonium."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p589","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXXI.","And whoso is not past fourteen years of age [is not to give witness]. An accuser is not to produce witnesses from his own house.","Et qui non est maior quattuordecim annis [testis non sit]. Accusatores non producant testes de domo sua."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p590","CANON CXXXII. (Greek cxxxiii.)","Concerning a bishop who removes a man from communion who says he has confessed to the bishop alone his crime.","De episcopo qui removet a communione hominem qui dicit se soli episcopo crimen suum confessum fuisse."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p591","CANON CXXXII. (Greek cxxxiii.)","IT also seemed good that if on any occasion a bishop said that someone had confessed to him alone a personal crime, and that the man now denies it; let not the bishop think that any slight is laid upon him if he is not believed on his own word alone, although he says he is not willing to communicate with the man so denying through a scruple of his own conscience.","Item placuit ut si qua occasione episcopus dixerit aliquem sibi soli crimen proprium confessum fuisse, et ille homo modo negat; non existimet episcopus aliquam contumeliam sibi fieri si verbis eius soli non creditur, licet dicat se nolle propter conscientiae suae scrupulum cum eo negante communicare."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p592","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXXII.","If a bishop says \"someone has confessed to me alone a crime,\" if the someone denies it, he [i.e. the bishop] is not easily to be believed.","Si episcopus dixerit \"aliquis mihi soli crimen confessus est,\" si ille negaverit, non est facile credendum."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p593","CANON CXXXIII. (Greek cxxxiv.)","That a bishop should not rashly deprive anyone of communion.","Ut episcopus non temere quemquam communione privet."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p594","CANON CXXXIII. (Greek cxxxiv.)","AS long as his own bishop will not communicate with one excommunicated, the other bishops should have no communion with that bishop, that the bishop may be more careful not to charge anyone with what he cannot prove by documentary evidence to others.","Quamdiu proprius episcopus cum excommunicato non communicat, ceteri episcopi cum illo episcopo non communicent, ut episcopus cautior sit ne quem accuset quod aliis per documenta probare non potest."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p595","CANON CXXXIII. (Greek cxxxiv.)","(Greek cxxxv.) BISHOP AURELIUS said: According to the statutes of this whole assembled council, and the opinion of my littleness, it seems good to make an end of all the matters of the whole of the before-manifested title, and let the ecclesiastical acts receive the discussion of the present day's constitution. And what things have not yet been expressed we shall write on the next day through our brethren, Bishop Faustinus and the Presbyters Philip and Asellus to our venerable brother and fellow-bishop Boniface; and they gave their assent in writing.","(Gr. cxxxv.) AURELIUS episcopus dixit: Secundum statuta totius congregati concilii, et sententiam parvitatis meae, placet ut omnium rerum totius praemonstrati tituli finis fiat, et acta ecclesiastica hodiernae constitutionis discussionem accipiant. Et quae nondum expressa sunt, crastino die per fratres nostros Faustinum episcopum et Philippum et Asellum presbyteros ad venerabilem fratrem et coepiscopum nostrum Bonifacium scribemus; et consensum suum subscriptione firmaverunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p596","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXXIII.","If a bishop deprives of communion an unconvicted man, he shall likewise be deprived of communion with his fellows.","Si episcopus communione privat non convictum, similiter privetur communione cum suis collegis."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p597","CANON CXXXIV. (Continuation of cxxxv. in the Greek.)","Here beginneth the letter directed from the whole African Council to Boniface, bishop of the City of Rome, by Faustinus the bishop, and Philip and Asellus the presbyters, legates of the Roman Church.","Incipit epistola directa ab universo Concilio Africano ad Bonifacium, episcopum urbis Romae, per Faustinum episcopum, et Philippum et Asellum presbyteros, legatos Ecclesiae Romanae."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p598","CANON CXXXIV. (Continuation of cxxxv. in the Greek.)","TO the most blessed lord, and our honourable brother Boniface, Aurelius, Valentine of the primatial See of Numidia, and others present with us to the number of 217 from the whole council in Africa.","Domino beatissimo, et honorabili fratri Bonifacio, Aurelius, Valentinus primae sedis Numidiae, et ceteri nobiscum praesentes ad numerum CCXVII de universo concilio Africano."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p599","CANON CXXXIV. (Continuation of cxxxv. in the Greek.)","Since it has pleased the Lord that our humility should write concerning those things which with us our holy brethren, Faustinus a fellow-bishop and Philip and Asellus, fellow presbyters, have done, not to the bishop Zosimus of blessed memory, from whom they brought commands and letters to us, but to your holiness, who art constituted in his room by divine authority, we ought briefly to set forth what has been determined upon by mutual consent; not indeed those things which are contained in the prolix volumes of the acts, in which, while charity was preserved, yet we loitered not without some little labour of altercation, deliberating those things in the acts which now pertain to the cause. However the more gratefully would he have received this news as he would have seen a more peaceful ending of the matter, my lord and brother, had he been still in the body! Apiarius the presbyter, concerning whose ordination, excommunication, and appeal no small scandal arose not only at Sicca but also in the whole African Church, has been restored to communion upon his seeking pardon for all his sins. First our fellow bishop Urban of Sicca doubtless corrected whatever in him seemed to need correction. For there should have been kept in mind the peace and quiet of the Church not only in the present but also in the future, since so many evils of such a kind had gone before, that it was incumbent to take care that like or even graver evils should be prevented thereafter. It seemed good to us that the presbyter Apiarius should be removed from the church of Sicca, retaining only the honour of his grade, and that he should exercise the office of the presbyterate wherever else he wished and could, having received a letter to this effect. This we granted without difficulty at his own petition made in a letter. But truly before this case should be thus closed, among other things which we were treating of in daily discussions, the nature of the case demanded that we should ask our brothers, Faustinus our fellow bishop, and Philip and Asellus our fellow presbyters, to set forth what they had been enjoined to treat of with us that they might be inserted in the ecclesiastical acts. And they proceeded to make a verbal statement, but when we earnestly asked that they would present it rather in writing, then they produced the Commonitory. This was read to us and also set down in the acts, which they are bringing with them to you. In this they were bidden to treat of four things with us, first concerning the appeal of bishops to the Pontiff of the Roman Church, second that bishops should not unbecomingly be sailing to court, thirdly concerning the treating the causes of presbyters and deacons by contiguous bishops, if they had been wrongly excommunicated by their own, and fourthly concerning the bishop Urban who should be excommunicated or even sent to Rome, unless he should have corrected what seemed to need correction.","Quoniam Domino placuit ut de his quae cum nobis sancti fratres nostri Faustinus coepiscopus et Philippus et Asellus consacerdotes egerunt, non ad Zosimum beatae memoriae episcopum, a quo mandata et epistolas ad nos detulerunt, sed ad sanctitatem vestram, qui eius loco divina auctoritate constitutus est, nostra humilitas scriberet, breviter exponere debemus quae communi consensu statuta sunt; non ea quae prolixis voluminibus actuum continentur, in quibus, salva caritate, tamen non sine aliquo altercandi labore immorati sumus, ea in actis deliberantes quae nunc ad causam pertinent. Quanto autem gratius haec ille suscepisset, quanto pacatiorem causae exitum vidisset, dominus et frater, si adhuc in corpore esset! Apiarius presbyter, de cuius ordinatione, excommunicatione et appellatione non parvum scandalum non solum apud Siccam sed et in universa Africana Ecclesia exortum est, veniam pro omnibus suis peccatis postulans communioni restitutus est. Primus frater noster Urbanus Siccensis episcopus correxit quidquid in eo videbatur esse corrigendum. Servari enim debuit pax et quies Ecclesiae non solum in praesenti sed et in futuro, cum tot et tanta mala praecesserint, ut cavendum fuerit ne similia vel etiam graviora posthac evenirent. Placuit nobis ut Apiarius presbyter ab ecclesia Siccensi removeretur, tantum honoris sui gradum retinens, et ubicumque vellet et posset presbyterii officium exerceret, accepta ad hoc epistola. Hoc ei sine difficultate ad suam petitionem per epistolam factam concessimus. Sed vere antequam haec causa sic clauderetur, inter cetera quae in cotidianis tractatibus agebamus, postulabat ipsius causae natura ut fratres nostros Faustinum coepiscopum, et Philippum et Asellum consacerdotes rogaremus ut proponerent quidquid mandatum habebant nobiscum tractandi ut ecclesiasticis actis insereretur. Et verbis facere coeperunt, sed cum instantissime peteremus ut scripto potius ederent, tunc Commonitorium protulerunt. Hoc nobis lectum est et etiam actis insertum, quod vobiscum perferunt. In hoc quattuor capitula nobiscum tractare iussi sunt: primum de appellationibus episcoporum ad Pontificem Romanae Ecclesiae, secundum ne episcopi indecenter ad comitatum navigent, tertium de tractandis causis presbyterorum et diaconorum a vicinis episcopis si iniuste excommunicati a suis essent, quartum de Urbano episcopo qui excommunicari vel etiam Romam mitti deberet, nisi correxisset quae corrigenda videbantur."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p600","CANON CXXXIV. (Continuation of cxxxv. in the Greek.)","Of all which things concerning the first and third, that is that it is allowed to bishops to appeal to Rome and that the causes of clerics should be settled by the bishops of their own provinces, already last year we have taken pains to insinuate, in our letter to the same bishop Zosimus of venerable memory, that we were willing to observe these provisions for a little while without any injury to him, until the search for the statutes of the Council of Nice had been finished. And now we ask of your holiness that you would cause to be observed by us the acts and constitutions of our fathers at the Council of Nice, and that you cause to be exercised by you there, those things which they brought in the commonitory: that is to say, If a bishop shall have been accused, etc. [Here follows Canon vii. of Sardica.]","De quibus omnibus de primo et tertio, id est de licentia episcopis Romam appellandi et de causis clericorum ab episcopis suarum provinciarum terminandis, iam praeterito anno curavimus insinuare, in epistola nostra ad eundem Zosimum venerabilis memoriae episcopum, quod vellemus haec sine aliquo eius praeiudicio ad modicum observare, donec investigatio statutorum Concilii Nicaeni finem acciperet. Et nunc petimus a sanctitate vestra ut observari a nobis faciatis acta et constitutiones patrum nostrorum in Concilio Nicaeno, et ut exerceri a vobis illic faciatis quae in commonitorio detulerunt: id est, Si episcopus accusatus fuerit, etc. [Hic sequitur Canon VII. Sardicensis.]"],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p601","CANON CXXXIV. (Continuation of cxxxv. in the Greek.)","Item concerning presbyters and deacons. If any bishop has been quickly angered, etc. [Here follows Canon xvii. of Sardica.]","Item de presbyteris et diaconibus. Si episcopus quis forte iracundus, etc. [Hic sequitur Canon XVII. Sardicensis.]"],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p602","CANON CXXXIV. (Continuation of cxxxv. in the Greek.)","These are the things which have been inserted in the acts until the arrival of the most accurate copies of the Nicene Council, which things, if they are contained there (as in the Commonitory, which our brethren directed to us from the Apostolic See alleged) and be even kept according to that order by you in Italy, in no way could we be compelled either to endure such treatment as we are unwilling to mention or could suffer what is unbearable: but we believe, through the mercy of our Lord God, while your holiness presides over the Roman Church, we shall not have to suffer that pride. And there will be kept toward us, what should be kept with brotherly love to us who are making no dispute. You will also perceive according to the wisdom and the justice which the most Highest has given thee, what should be observed, if perchance the canons of the Council of Nice are other [than you suppose]. For although we have read very many copies, yet never have we read in the Latin copies that there were any such decrees as are contained in the commonitory before mentioned. So too, because we can find them in no Greek text here, we have desired that there should be brought to us from the Eastern Churches copies of the decrees, for it is said that there correct copies of the decrees are to be found. For which end we beg your reverence, that you would deign yourself also to write to the pontiffs of these parts, that is of the churches of Antioch, Alexandria, and Constantinople, and to any others also if it shall please your holiness, that thence there may come to us the same canons decreed by the Fathers in the city of Nice, and thus you would confer by the help of the Lord this most great benefit upon all the churches of the West. For who can doubt that the copies of the Nicene Council gathered in the Greek empire are most accurate, which although brought together from so diverse and from such noble Greek churches are found to agree when compared together? And until this be done, the provisions laid down to us in the Commonitory aforesaid, concerning the appeals of bishops to the pontiff of the Roman Church and concerning the causes of clerics which should be terminated by the bishops of their own provinces, we are willing to allow to be observed until the proof arrives and we trust your blessedness will help us in this according to the will of God. The rest of the matters treated and defined in our synod, since the aforesaid brethren, our fellow bishop Faustinus, and the presbyters Philip and Asellus are carrying the acts with them, if you deign to receive them, will make known to your holiness. And they signed. Our Lord keep thee to us for many years, most blessed brother. Alypius, Augustine, Possidius, Marinus and the rest of the bishops (217) also signed.","Haec sunt quae actis inserta sunt usque ad aduentum exactissimorum exemplarium Concilii Nicaeni, quae, si ibi continentur (sicut in Commonitorio quod fratres nostri a Sede Apostolica ad nos direxerunt allegatum est), et etiam serventur secundum illum ordinem a vobis in Italia, nullo modo compelli possemus aut talia pati quae nolumus dicere aut ferre quae intolerabilia sunt: sed credimus, per misericordiam Domini Dei nostri, sanctitate vestra Romanae Ecclesiae praesidente, non passuros nos istam superbiam. Et servabitur erga nos quod servari debet fraterna caritate nobis non contendentes. Perpendetis etiam secundum sapientiam et iustitiam quam tibi Altissimus dedit, quid observari debeat, si forte aliter se habent canones Concilii Nicaeni. Nam cum legimus plurima exemplaria, numquam tamen in Latinis exemplaribus legimus talia decreta qualia in praedicto Commonitorio continentur. Item, quia in nullis Graecis hic invenire potuimus, desideravimus ut nobis de Orientalibus Ecclesiis exemplaria decretorum adferantur, nam dicitur ibi exemplaria decretorum emendatissima inveniri. Ad quod finem petimus reverentiam vestram, ut dignemini etiam vos scribere ad pontifices illarum partium, id est ecclesiarum Antiochenae, Alexandrinae et Constantinopolitanae, et ad quoslibet alios si placuerit sanctitati vestrae, ut inde ad nos eidem canones a Patribus in urbe Nicaena decreti perveniant, et sic maximum beneficium per Domini adiutorium omnibus Occidentis ecclesiis conferatis. Quis enim dubitet exemplaria Concilii Nicaeni in Graeco imperio collecta esse emendatissima, quae tam ex diversis et tam nobilibus Graecis ecclesiis collecta inter se collata concordant? Et donec hoc fiat, ea quae nobis in Commonitorio supra dicto praescripta sunt de appellationibus episcoporum ad Romanae Ecclesiae pontificem et de causis clericorum quae a suarum provinciarum episcopis terminari debent, observari permittere volumus donec probatio veniat, et confidimus beatitudinem vestram nos in hoc adiuvaturam secundum Dei voluntatem. Cetera autem quae in synodo nostra tractata et definita sunt, cum praefati fratres nostri Faustinus coepiscopus, et Philippus et Asellus presbyteri acta vobiscum perferant, si dignemini accipere, sanctitati vestrae nota facient. Et subscripserunt. Dominus nos tibi per multos annos conservet, beatissime frater. Alypius, Augustinus, Possidius, Marinus et ceteri episcopi (CCXVII) etiam subscripserunt."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p603","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXXIV.","Urban, the bishop of Siccas, is either to be excommunicated or else summoned to Rome unless he corrects what should be corrected by him.","Urbanus, episcopus Siccensis, aut excommunicetur aut Romam vocetur nisi correxerit quae corrigenda sunt ab eo."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p604","CANON CXXXV. (Not numbered in the Greek.)","Here begin the rescripts to the African Council from Cyril bishop of Alexandria in which he sends the authentic proceedings of the Nicene Council, translated from the Greek by Innocent the presbyter: these letters with the same Nicene council were also sent through the aforementioned presbyter Innocent and by Marcellus a subdeacon of the Church of Carthage, to the holy Boniface, bishop of the Roman Church, on the sixth day before the calends of December in the year 419.","Incipiunt rescripta ad Concilium Africanum a Cyrillo episcopo Alexandrino in quibus mittit authentica gesta Concilii Nicaeni, translata de Graeco per Innocentium presbyterum: hae epistolae cum eodem Nicaeno concilio missae sunt etiam per praefatum Innocentium presbyterum et per Marcellum subdiaconum Ecclesiae Carthaginensis ad sanctum Bonifacium, episcopum Romanae Ecclesiae, VI Kalendas Decembres anno 419."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p605","CANON CXXXV. (Not numbered in the Greek.)","TO the most honourable lords, our holy brethren and fellow bishops, Aurelius, Valentinus, as well as to the whole holy synod met in Carthage, Cyril salutes your holiness in God.","Dominis honorabilissimis, sanctis fratribus et coepiscopis nostris, Aurelio et Valentino, necnon et universae sanctae synodo Carthagini congregatae, Cyrillus in Deo salutem."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p606","CANON CXXXV. (Not numbered in the Greek.)","I have received with all joy at the hands of our son, the presbyter Innocent, the letters of your reverence so full of piety, in which you express the hope that we will send you most accurate copies of the decrees of the holy Fathers at the Synod held at Nice the metropolis of Bithynia from the archives of our church; with our own certificate of accuracy attached thereto. In answer to which request, most honourable lords and brethren, I have thought it necessary to send to you, with our compliments, by our son, Innocent the presbyter, the bearer of these, most faithful copies of the decisions of the synod held at Nice in Bithynia. And when ye have sought in the history of the church, you will find them there also. Concerning Easter, as you have written, we announce to you that we shall celebrate it on the xviiith before the calends of May of the next indiction. The subscription. May God and our Lord preserve your holy synod as we desire, dear brethren.","Accepi cum omni gaudio per filium nostrum Innocentium presbyterum litteras reverentiae vestrae tam religione plenas, in quibus spem exprimitis quod mittamus vobis emendatissima exemplaria decretorum sanctorum Patrum in Synodo habita apud Nicaeam metropolim Bithyniae ex chartophylacio ecclesiae nostrae; cum nostra manu adhibita veritatis attestatione. Ad quod petitionis vestrae votum, honorabilissimi domini et fratres, necessarium duxi ut transmitterem vobis cum obsequio per filium nostrum Innocentium presbyterum harum portitorem fidelissima exemplaria gestorum synodi apud Nicaeam Bithyniae habitae. Et cum in historia ecclesiastica quaesieritis, etiam ibi ea invenietis. De Pascha autem, sicut scripsistis, annuntiamus vobis quod celebrabitur XVIII Kalendas Maias proximae indictionis. Subscriptio. Deus et Dominus noster sanctam synodum vestram sicut desideramus conservet, dilectissimi fratres."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p607","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXXV.","According to your written request, we have sent to your charity most faithful copies of the authentic decrees of the Synod which was held at Nice, a city of Bithynia.","Secundum petitionem vestram scripto traditam, misimus caritati vestrae fidelissima exemplaria authenticorum decretorum Synodi quae habita est apud Nicaeam, civitatem Bithyniae."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p608","CANON CXXXVI. (Not numbered in the Greek but with a new heading.)","Here beginneth the letter of Atticus, bishop of Constantinople to the same.","Incipit epistola Attici episcopi Constantinopolitani ad eosdem."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p609","CANON CXXXVI. (Not numbered in the Greek but with a new heading.)","TO our holy lords, and rightly most blessed brethren and fellow bishops, Aurelius, Valentine, and to the other beloved ones met together in the Synod held at Carthage, Atticus the bishop.","Sanctis dominis, et recte beatissimis fratribus et coepiscopis, Aurelio et Valentino, et ceteris in Synodo Carthaginensi congregatis carissimis, Atticus episcopus."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p610","CANON CXXXVI. (Not numbered in the Greek but with a new heading.)","By our son Marcellus the subdeacon, I have received with all thanksgiving the writings of your holiness, praising the Lord that I enjoyed the blessing of so many of my brethren. O my lords and most blessed brethren, ye have written asking me to send you most accurate copies of the canons enacted at the city of Nice, the metropolis of Bithynia, by the Fathers for the exposition of the faith. And who is there that would deny to his brethren the common faith, or the statutes decreed by the Fathers. Wherefore by the same son of mine, Marcellus, your subdeacon, who was in great haste, I have sent to you the canons in full as they were adopted by the Fathers in the city of Nice; and I ask of you that your holy synod would have me much in your prayers. The subscription. May our God keep your sanctity, as we desire, most holy brethren.","Per filium nostrum Marcellum subdiaconum, accepi cum omni gratiarum actione scripta sanctitatis vestrae, laudans Dominum quia tot fratrum meorum benedictione fruitus sum. O domini mei et beatissimi fratres, scripsistis rogantes ut mitterem vobis emendatissima exemplaria canonum ab Patribus apud Nicaeam metropolim Bithyniae de expositione fidei editorum. Et quis est qui neget fratribus communem fidem, vel statuta a Patribus decreta? Quapropter per eundem filium meum Marcellum subdiaconem vestrum, qui magnam habebat festinationem, misi vobis canones integros sicut a Patribus apud Nicaeam civitatem constituti sunt; et peto a vobis ut vestra sancta synodus mei memor in orationibus vestris multum habeat. Subscriptio. Deus noster custodiat sanctitatem vestram, sicut desideramus, sanctissimi fratres."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p611","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXXVI.","The Canons of the Synod of Nice are sent, as they were decreed by the Fathers, in accordance with your letters.","Canones Synodi Nicaenae mittuntur, sicut a Patribus decreta sunt, secundum litteras vestras."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p612","CANON CXXXVII. (Continuation of the last in the Greek.)","Here begin the examples of the Nicene Council, sent on the sixth day before the calends of December in the year 419, after the consulate of the most glorious emperor Honorius for the XIIth time and Theodosius for the VIIIth time, Augustuses, to Boniface the bishop of the City of Rome.","Incipiunt exemplaria Concilii Nicaeni, missa VI Kalendas Decembres anno 419, post consulatum gloriosissimi imperatoris Honorii XII et Theodosii VIII Augustorum, ad Bonifacium episcopum urbis Romae."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p613","CANON CXXXVII. (Continuation of the last in the Greek.)","WE believe in one God etc.…the Catholic and Apostolic Church anathematizes them.","Credimus in unum Deum, etc.…Catholica et Apostolica Ecclesia eos anathematizat."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p614","CANON CXXXVII. (Continuation of the last in the Greek.)","To this symbol of the faith there were also annexed copies of the statutes of the same Nicene Councils from the aforenamed pontiffs, in all respects as are contained above; which we do not think it necessary to write out here again.","Huic symbolo fidei annexa sunt etiam exemplaria statutorum eorundem Nicaenorum Conciliorum a praefatis pontificibus, per omnia sicut superius continentur; quae non putavimus necessarium iterum hic describere."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p615","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXXVII.","The Canons of the Synod of Nice are sent, as they were decreed by the Fathers, in accordance with your letters. [Here follows the Nicene Creed in full.]","Canones Synodi Nicaenae mittuntur, sicut a Patribus decreta sunt, secundum litteras vestras. [Hic sequitur Symbolum Nicaenum in pleno.]"],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p616","CANON CXXXVIII. (Not numbered in the Greek.)","Here beginneth the epistle of the African synod to Pope Celestine, bishop of the City of Rome.","Incipit epistola synodi Africanae ad Caelestinum papam, episcopum urbis Romae."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p617","CANON CXXXVIII. (Not numbered in the Greek.)","TO the lord and most beloved and our honourable brother Celestine, Aurelius, Palatinus, Antony, Totus, Servusdei, Terentius, Fortunatus, Martin, Januarius, Optatus, Ceticius, Donatus, Theasius, Vincent, Fortunatian, and the rest of us, assembled at Carthage in the General Council of Africa.","Domino et dilectissimo et honorabili fratri Caelestino, Aurelius, Palatinus, Antonius, Totus, Servusdei, Terentius, Fortunatus, Martinus, Ianuarius, Optatus, Ceticius, Donatus, Theasius, Vincentius, Fortunatianus, et ceteri qui in Concilio Generali Africae Carthagine convenimus."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p618","CANON CXXXVIII. (Not numbered in the Greek.)","We could wish that, like as your Holiness intimated to us, in your letter sent by our fellow presbyter Leo, your pleasure at the arrival of Apiarius, so we also could send to you these writings with pleasure respecting his clearing. Then in truth both our own satisfaction and yours of late would be more reasonable; nor would that lately expressed by you concerning the hearing of him then to come, as well as that already past, seem hasty and inconsiderate. Upon the arrival, then, of our holy Brother and fellow-Bishop Faustinus, we assembled a council, and believed that he was sent with that man, in order that, as he [Apiarius] had before been restored to the presbyterate by his assistance, so now he might with his exertions be cleared of the very great crimes charged against him by the inhabitants of Tabraca. But the due course of examination in our council discovered in him such great and monstrous crimes as to overbear even Faustinus, who acted rather as an advocate of the aforementioned person than as a judge, and to prevail against what was more the zeal of a defender, than the justice of an inquirer. For first he vehemently opposed the whole assembly, inflicting on us many injuries, under pretence of asserting the privileges of the Roman Church, and wishing that he should be received into communion by us, on the ground that your Holiness, believing him to have appealed, though unable to prove it, had restored him to communion. But this we by no means allowed, as you will also better see by reading the acts. After however, a most laborious inquiry carried on for three days, during which in the greatest affliction we took cognizance of various charges against him, God the just Judge, strong and long suffering, cut short by a sudden stroke both the delays of our fellow-bishop Faustinus and the evasions of Apiarius himself, by which he was endeavouring to veil his foul enormities. For his strong and shameless obstinacy was overcome, by which he endeavoured to cover, through an impudent denial, the mire of his lusts, and God so wrought upon his conscience and published, even to the eyes of men, the secret crimes which he was already condemning in that man's heart, a very sty of wickedness, that, after his false denial he suddenly burst forth into a confession of all the crimes he was charged with, and at length convicted himself of his own accord of all infamies beyond belief, and changed to groans even the hope we had entertained, believing and desiring that he might be cleared from such shameful blots, except indeed that it was so far a relief to our sorrow, that he had delivered us from the labour of a longer inquiry, and by confession had applied some sort of remedy to his own wounds, though, lord and brother, it was unwilling, and done with a struggling conscience.","Optaremus quod, sicut Sanctitas Vestra nobis significavit per litteras per consacerdotem nostrum Leonem missas, gaudium vestrum de aduentu Apiarii, ita et nos possemus haec scripta vobis cum gaudio de eius purgatione transmittere. Tunc enim et nostra satisfactio et vestra nuper esset rationabilior; nec illud a vobis nuper expressum de audiendo eo tunc futuro, sicut et de iam praeterito, praeceps et inconsideratum videretur. Aduentu igitur sancti Fratris et Coepiscopi nostri Faustini, concilium congregavimus, et credidimus eum cum illo homine missum esse, ut sicut [Apiarius] antea per eius adiutorium presbyterio restitutus fuerat, ita nunc per eius operam a maximis criminibus sibi a Tabracensibus obiectis purgaretur. Sed ordo examinationis in concilio nostro talia et tanta in eo scelera detexit quae etiam Faustinum, qui magis advocatus praedicti hominis quam iudex agebat, superaverunt, et contra eum quod plus erat defensoris studium quam inquirentis iustitia praevaluit. Primo enim vehementer toti congregati contradixit, multas nobis iniurias inferens, sub praetextu asserendorum privilegiorum Ecclesiae Romanae, volens ut eum in communionem reciperemus, eo quod Sanctitas Vestra, credens eum appellasse quod probare non potuit, communioni restituerit. Quod nos minime permisimus, sicut et vos melius acta legentes videbitis. Post vero laboriosissimam inquisitionem tribus diebus exhibitam, cum in magno moerore varias contra eum obiectiones cognosceremus, Deus iustus Iudex, fortis et longanimis, repentino ictu et moras Faustini coepiscopi nostri et ipsius Apiarii tergiversationes praecidit, quibus taetras enormitates suas velare conabatur. Victa est enim dura et impudens eius obstinatio, qua inpudenti negatione caenosam suarum libidinum foveam contexere moliebatur, et Deus sic in conscientia eius operatus est et occulta crimina, quae iam in corde illius hominis damnabat, etiam ad oculos hominum publicavit, porcum spurcitiae, ut post falsam negationem subito in omnium quibus arguebatur criminum confessionem erumperet, et tandem ultro se incredibilium flagitiorum omnium convictum subderet, et in gemitus spem quam conceperamus, credentes et optantes eum a talibus maculis fore purgatum, commutaret; nisi quod hoc solum dolori nostro solacium fuit, quod nos longioris inquisitionis labore liberavit, et confessione vulneribus suis aliquod adhibuit medicamentum, licet, domine et frater, invitus et reluctante conscientia."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p619","CANON CXXXVIII. (Not numbered in the Greek.)","Premising, therefore, our due regards to you, we earnestly conjure you, that for the future you do not readily admit to a hearing persons coming hence, nor choose to receive to your communion those who have been excommunicated by us, because you, venerable Sir, will readily perceive that this has been prescribed even by the Nicene council. For though this seems to be there forbidden in respect of the inferior clergy, or the laity, how much more did it will this to be observed in the case of bishops, lest those who had been suspended from communion in their own Province might seem to be restored to communion hastily or unfitly by your Holiness. Let your Holiness reject, as is worthy of you, that unprincipled taking shelter with you of presbyters likewise, and the inferior clergy, both because by no ordinance of the Fathers hath the Church of Africa been deprived of this authority, and the Nicene decrees have most plainly committed not only the clergy of inferior rank, but the bishops themselves to their own Metropolitans. For they have ordained with great wisdom and justice, that all matters should be terminated in the places where they arise; and did not think that the grace of the Holy Spirit would be wanting to any Province, for the bishops of Christ (Sacerdotibus) wisely to discern, and firmly to maintain the right: especially since whosoever thinks himself wronged by any judgment may appeal to the council of his Province, or even to a General Council [i.e. of Africa] unless it be imagined that God can inspire a single individual with justice, and refuse it to an innumerable multitude of bishops (sacerdotum) assembled in council. And how shall we be able to rely on a sentence passed beyond the sea, since it will not be possible to send thither the necessary witnesses, whether from the weakness of sex, or advanced age, or any other impediment? For that your Holiness should send any on your part we can find ordained by no council of Fathers. Because with regard to what you have sent us by the same our brother bishop Faustinus, as being contained in the Nicene Council, we can find nothing of the kind in the more authentic copies of that council, which we have received from the holy Cyril our brother, Bishop of the Alexandrine Church, and from the venerable Atticus the Prelate of Constantinople, and which we formerly sent by Innocent the presbyter, and Marcellus the subdeacon through whom we received them, to Boniface the Bishop, your predecessor of venerable memory. Moreover whoever desires you to delegate any of your clergy to execute your orders, do not comply, lest it seem that we are introducing the pride of secular dominion into the Church of Christ which exhibiteth to all that desire to see God the light of simplicity and the day of humility. For now that the miserable Apiarius has been removed out of the Church of Christ for his horrible crimes, we feel confident respecting our brother Faustinus, that through the uprightness and moderation of your Holiness, Africa, without violating brotherly charity, will by no means have to endure him any longer. Lord and brother, may our Lord long preserve your Holiness to pray for us.","Praemissa itaque quae debuimus vobis obsequia, obsecramus, ut de cetero non facile audiatis hinc venientes, neque ad communionem vestram suscipere velitis excommunicatos a nobis, quia hoc etiam Niceno concilio praescriptum esse, venerande frater, facile perpendetis. Nam etsi hoc de inferioribus clericis vel laicis ibi videtur esse prohibitum, quanto magis in episcoporum causa hoc servari voluit, ne hi qui in sua provincia communione suspensi sunt, a Sanctitate Vestra temere vel indecenter communioni restitui videantur. Respuat Sanctitas Vestra, sicut dignum est, inhonestum confugium ad vos presbyterorum etiam et inferiorum clericorum, tum quia nulla patrum constitutione Africa hac auctoritate privata est, tum quia Nicena decreta manifestissime non solum inferioris gradus clericos, sed etiam ipsos episcopos suis metropolitanis commiserunt. Sapientissime enim et iustissime ordinaverunt, ut omnia negotia in locis ubi orta sunt terminentur; nec putaverunt gratiam Sancti Spiritus alicui provinciae defuturam, qua sacerdotes Christi sapienter cognoscant et firmiter teneant rectum: praesertim cum quicumque putaverit se aliqua iudicio laesum, ad concilium suae provinciae vel etiam ad universale concilium [id est Africae] appellet; nisi forte quis putet Deum uni homini posse inspirare iustitiam, et innumerabili multitudini sacerdotum in concilio congregatorum negare. Et quomodo nos possimus iudicium trans mare latum obtinere, cum necessarios testes, sive propter imbecillitatem sexus sive propter aetatem sive propter aliquod aliud impedimentum, illuc transmittere non possimus? Nam ut Sanctitas Vestra inde aliquem mittat, nulla patrum synodo constitutum est. Quod autem per eundem fratrem nostrum Faustinum episcopum misistis nobis quasi in Nicaeno Concilio continentur, nihil tale in illius concilii emendatioribus exemplaribus invenire potuimus, quae a sancto Cyrillo fratre nostro episcopo Alexandrinae Ecclesiae et a venerabili Attico Constantinopolitano antistite accepimus, et quae olim per Innocentium presbyterum et Marcellum subdiaconum per quos accepimus, ad Bonifacium episcopum, tuae memoriae venerabilem praedecessorem, misimus. Praeterea quicumque te rogaverit ut aliquem de clero tuo ad aliqua exsequenda deleges, ne comprobaveris, ne videamur nos saecularis dominationis fastum in Ecclesiam Christi inducere, quae simplicitatis lucem et humilitatis diem exhibet omnibus Deum videre volentibus. Nunc autem miserabili Apiario propter horrenda crimina de Ecclesia Christi eiecto, confidimus de fratre nostro Faustino, quod per rectitudinem et moderationem Sanctitatis Vestrae, salva fraterna caritate, Africa eum sustinere minime debebit. Domine et frater, Dominus noster Sanctitatem Vestram multo tempore conservet orantem pro nobis."],["vol3/african-code/part-2/index.html","p620","Ancient Epitome of Canon CXXXVIII.","Those excommunicated by us, ye are not be willing to admit afterwards to communion, according to the decree of the Nicene Synod. For Apiarius, who was restored by you, has resisted the Synod, and treated it with scorn, and at length has been converted and confessed himself guilty with sighs and tears.","Excommunicatos a nobis nolite postea ad communionem admittere, secundum decretum Nicaenae Synodi. Apiarius enim, qui a vobis restitutus est, restitit Synodo et eam sprevit, et tandem conversus est et se reum gemens et flens confessus est."]];